@.str = private constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL20AttrDoc_AddressSpace = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_Allocating = internal constant [135 x i8] c"Declares that a function potentially allocates heap memory, and prevents any potential inference\0Aof ``nonallocating`` by the compiler.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_AnnotateType = internal constant [1178 x i8] c"This attribute is used to add annotations to types, typically for use by static\0Aanalysis tools that are not integrated into the core Clang compiler (e.g.,\0AClang-Tidy checks or out-of-tree Clang-based tools). It is a counterpart to the\0A`annotate` attribute, which serves the same purpose, but for declarations.\0A\0AThe attribute takes a mandatory string literal argument specifying the\0Aannotation category and an arbitrary number of optional arguments that provide\0Aadditional information specific to the annotation category. The optional\0Aarguments must be constant expressions of arbitrary type.\0A\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  int* [[clang::annotate_type(\22category1\22, \22foo\22, 1)]] f(int[[clang::annotate_type(\22category2\22)]] *);\0A\0AThe attribute does not have any effect on the semantics of the type system,\0Aneither type checking rules, nor runtime semantics. In particular:\0A\0A- ``std::is_same<T, T [[clang::annotate_type(\22foo\22)]]>`` is true for all types\0A  ``T``.\0A\0A- It is not permissible for overloaded functions or template specializations\0A  to differ merely by an ``annotate_type`` attribute.\0A\0A- The presence of an ``annotate_type`` attribute will not affect name\0A  mangling.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_ArmAgnostic = internal constant [977 x i8] c"The ``__arm_agnostic`` keyword applies to prototyped function types and\0Aaffects the function's calling convention for a given state S. This\0Aattribute allows the user to describe a function that preserves S, without\0Arequiring the function to share S with its callers and without making\0Athe assumption that S exists.\0A\0AIf a function has the ``__arm_agnostic(S)`` attribute and calls a function\0Awithout this attribute, then the function's object code will contain code\0Ato preserve state S. Otherwise, the function's object code will be the same\0Aas if it did not have the attribute.\0A\0AThe attribute takes string arguments to describe state S. The supported\0Astates are:\0A\0A* ``\22sme_za_state\22`` for state enabled by PSTATE.ZA, such as ZA and ZT0.\0A\0AThe attribute ``__arm_agnostic(\22sme_za_state\22)`` cannot be used in conjunction\0Awith ``__arm_in(S)``, ``__arm_out(S)``, ``__arm_inout(S)`` or\0A``__arm_preserves(S)`` where state S describes state enabled by PSTATE.ZA,\0Asuch as \22za\22 or \22zt0\22.\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_ArmIn = internal constant [544 x i8] c"The ``__arm_in`` keyword applies to prototyped function types and specifies\0Athat the function shares a given state S with its caller.  For ``__arm_in``, the\0Afunction takes the state S as input and returns with the state S unchanged.\0A\0AThe attribute takes string arguments to instruct the compiler which state\0Ais shared.  The supported states for S are:\0A\0A* ``\22za\22`` for Matrix Storage (requires SME)\0A\0AThe attributes ``__arm_in(S)``, ``__arm_out(S)``, ``__arm_inout(S)`` and\0A``__arm_preserves(S)`` are all mutually exclusive for the same state S.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_ArmInOut = internal constant [539 x i8] c"The ``__arm_inout`` keyword applies to prototyped function types and specifies\0Athat the function shares a given state S with its caller.  For ``__arm_inout``,\0Athe function takes the state S as input and returns new state for S.\0A\0AThe attribute takes string arguments to instruct the compiler which state\0Ais shared.  The supported states for S are:\0A\0A* ``\22za\22`` for Matrix Storage (requires SME)\0A\0AThe attributes ``__arm_in(S)``, ``__arm_out(S)``, ``__arm_inout(S)`` and\0A``__arm_preserves(S)`` are all mutually exclusive for the same state S.\00", align 16
@_ZL32AttrDoc_ArmMveStrictPolymorphism = internal constant [1441 x i8] c"This attribute is used in the implementation of the ACLE intrinsics for the Arm\0AMVE instruction set. It is used to define the vector types used by the MVE\0Aintrinsics.\0A\0AIts effect is to modify the behavior of a vector type with respect to function\0Aoverloading. If a candidate function for overload resolution has a parameter\0Atype with this attribute, then the selection of that candidate function will be\0Adisallowed if the actual argument can only be converted via a lax vector\0Aconversion. The aim is to prevent spurious ambiguity in ARM MVE polymorphic\0Aintrinsics.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void overloaded(uint16x8_t vector, uint16_t scalar);\0A  void overloaded(int32x4_t vector, int32_t scalar);\0A  uint16x8_t myVector;\0A  uint16_t myScalar;\0A\0A  // myScalar is promoted to int32_t as a side effect of the addition,\0A  // so if lax vector conversions are considered for myVector, then\0A  // the two overloads are equally good (one argument conversion\0A  // each). But if the vector has the __clang_arm_mve_strict_polymorphism\0A  // attribute, only the uint16x8_t,uint16_t overload will match.\0A  overloaded(myVector, myScalar + 1);\0A\0AHowever, this attribute does not prohibit lax vector conversions in contexts\0Aother than overloading.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  uint16x8_t function();\0A\0A  // This is still permitted with lax vector conversion enabled, even\0A  // if the vector types have __clang_arm_mve_strict_polymorphism\0A  int32x4_t result = function();\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_ArmOut = internal constant [541 x i8] c"The ``__arm_out`` keyword applies to prototyped function types and specifies\0Athat the function shares a given state S with its caller.  For ``__arm_out``,\0Athe function ignores the incoming state for S and returns new state for S.\0A\0AThe attribute takes string arguments to instruct the compiler which state\0Ais shared.  The supported states for S are:\0A\0A* ``\22za\22`` for Matrix Storage (requires SME)\0A\0AThe attributes ``__arm_in(S)``, ``__arm_out(S)``, ``__arm_inout(S)`` and\0A``__arm_preserves(S)`` are all mutually exclusive for the same state S.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_ArmPreserves = internal constant [478 x i8] c"The ``__arm_preserves`` keyword applies to prototyped function types and\0Aspecifies that the function does not read a given state S and returns\0Awith state S unchanged.\0A\0AThe attribute takes string arguments to instruct the compiler which state\0Ais shared.  The supported states for S are:\0A\0A* ``\22za\22`` for Matrix Storage (requires SME)\0A\0AThe attributes ``__arm_in(S)``, ``__arm_out(S)``, ``__arm_inout(S)`` and\0A``__arm_preserves(S)`` are all mutually exclusive for the same state S.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_ArmStreaming = internal constant [685 x i8] c"The ``__arm_streaming`` keyword applies to prototyped function types and specifies\0Athat the function has a \22streaming interface\22.  This means that:\0A\0A* the function requires that the processor implements the Scalable Matrix\0A  Extension (SME).\0A\0A* the function must be entered in streaming mode (that is, with PSTATE.SM\0A  set to 1)\0A\0A* the function must return in streaming mode\0A\0AClang manages PSTATE.SM automatically; it is not the source code's\0Aresponsibility to do this.  For example, if a non-streaming\0Afunction calls an ``__arm_streaming`` function, Clang generates code\0Athat switches into streaming mode before calling the function and\0Aswitches back to non-streaming mode on return.\00", align 16
@_ZL30AttrDoc_ArmStreamingCompatible = internal constant [1052 x i8] c"The ``__arm_streaming_compatible`` keyword applies to prototyped function types and\0Aspecifies that the function has a \22streaming compatible interface\22.  This\0Ameans that:\0A\0A* the function may be entered in either non-streaming mode (PSTATE.SM=0) or\0A  in streaming mode (PSTATE.SM=1).\0A\0A* the function must return in the same mode as it was entered.\0A\0A* the code executed in the function is compatible with either mode.\0A\0AClang manages PSTATE.SM automatically; it is not the source code's\0Aresponsibility to do this.  Clang will ensure that the generated code in\0Astreaming-compatible functions is valid in either mode (PSTATE.SM=0 or\0APSTATE.SM=1). For example, if an ``__arm_streaming_compatible`` function calls a\0Anon-streaming function, Clang generates code to temporarily switch out of streaming\0Amode before calling the function and switch back to streaming-mode on return if\0A``PSTATE.SM`` is ``1`` on entry of the caller. If ``PSTATE.SM`` is ``0`` on\0Aentry to the ``__arm_streaming_compatible`` function, the call will be executed\0Awithout changing modes.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_BTFTypeTag = internal constant [526 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((btf_type_tag(\22ARGUMENT\22)))`` attribute for\0Aall targets. It only has effect when ``-g`` is specified on the command line and\0Ais currently silently ignored when not applied to a pointer type (note: this\0Ascenario may be diagnosed in the future).\0A\0AThe ``ARGUMENT`` string will be preserved in IR and emitted to DWARF for the\0Atypes used in variable declarations, function declarations, or typedef\0Adeclarations.\0A\0AFor BPF targets, the ``ARGUMENT`` string will also be emitted to .BTF ELF\0Asection.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_Blocking = internal constant [118 x i8] c"Declares that a function potentially blocks, and prevents any potential inference of ``nonblocking``\0Aby the compiler.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_CmseNSCall = internal constant [498 x i8] c"This attribute declares a non-secure function type. When compiling for secure\0Astate, a call to such a function would switch from secure to non-secure state.\0AAll non-secure function calls must happen only through a function pointer, and\0Aa non-secure function type should only be used as a base type of a pointer.\0ASee `ARMv8-M Security Extensions: Requirements on Development\0ATools - Engineering Specification Documentation\0A<https://developer.arm.com/docs/ecm0359818/latest/>`_ for more information.\00", align 16
@_ZL35AttrDoc_HLSLGroupSharedAddressSpace = internal constant [528 x i8] c"HLSL enables threads of a compute shader to exchange values via shared memory.\0AHLSL provides barrier primitives such as GroupMemoryBarrierWithGroupSync,\0Aand so on to ensure the correct ordering of reads and writes to shared memory\0Ain the shader and to avoid data races.\0AHere's an example to declare a groupshared variable.\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  groupshared GSData data[5*5*1];\0A\0AThe full documentation is available here: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3dhlsl/dx-graphics-hlsl-variable-syntax#group-shared\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_HLSLROV = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL21AttrDoc_HLSLRawBuffer = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL25AttrDoc_HLSLResourceClass = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL15AttrDoc_NoDeref = internal constant [1444 x i8] c"The ``noderef`` attribute causes clang to diagnose dereferences of annotated pointer types.\0AThis is ideally used with pointers that point to special memory which cannot be read\0Afrom or written to, but allowing for the pointer to be used in pointer arithmetic.\0AThe following are examples of valid expressions where dereferences are diagnosed:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int __attribute__((noderef)) *p;\0A  int x = *p;  // warning\0A\0A  int __attribute__((noderef)) **p2;\0A  x = **p2;  // warning\0A\0A  int * __attribute__((noderef)) *p3;\0A  p = *p3;  // warning\0A\0A  struct S {\0A    int a;\0A  };\0A  struct S __attribute__((noderef)) *s;\0A  x = s->a;    // warning\0A  x = (*s).a;  // warning\0A\0ANot all dereferences may diagnose a warning if the value directed by the pointer may not be\0Aaccessed. The following are examples of valid expressions where may not be diagnosed:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int *q;\0A  int __attribute__((noderef)) *p;\0A  q = &*p;\0A  q = *&p;\0A\0A  struct S {\0A    int a;\0A  };\0A  struct S __attribute__((noderef)) *s;\0A  p = &s->a;\0A  p = &(*s).a;\0A\0A``noderef`` is currently only supported for pointers and arrays and not usable\0Afor references or Objective-C object pointers.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  int x = 2;\0A  int __attribute__((noderef)) &y = x;  // warning: 'noderef' can only be used on an array or pointer type\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  id __attribute__((noderef)) obj = [NSObject new]; // warning: 'noderef' can only be used on an array or pointer type\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_NonAllocating = internal constant [254 x i8] c"Declares that a function or function type either does or does not allocate heap memory, according\0Ato the optional, compile-time constant boolean argument, which defaults to true. When the argument\0Ais false, the attribute is equivalent to ``allocating``.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_NonBlocking = internal constant [410 x i8] c"Declares that a function or function type either does or does not block in any way, according\0Ato the optional, compile-time constant boolean argument, which defaults to true. When the argument\0Ais false, the attribute is equivalent to ``blocking``.\0A\0AFor the purposes of diagnostics, ``nonblocking`` is considered to include the\0A``nonallocating`` guarantee and is therefore a \22stronger\22 constraint or attribute.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_ObjCGC = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL33AttrDoc_ObjCInertUnsafeUnretained = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL18AttrDoc_ObjCKindOf = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL34AttrDoc_OpenCLConstantAddressSpace = internal constant [330 x i8] c"The constant address space attribute signals that an object is located in\0Aa constant (non-modifiable) memory region. It is available to all work items.\0AAny type can be annotated with the constant address space attribute. Objects\0Awith the constant address space qualifier can be declared in any scope and must\0Ahave an initializer.\00", align 16
@_ZL33AttrDoc_OpenCLGenericAddressSpace = internal constant [402 x i8] c"The generic address space attribute is only available with OpenCL v2.0 and later.\0AIt can be used with pointer types. Variables in global and local scope and\0Afunction parameters in non-kernel functions can have the generic address space\0Atype attribute. It is intended to be a placeholder for any other address space\0Aexcept for '__constant' in OpenCL code which can be used with multiple address\0Aspaces.\00", align 16
@_ZL32AttrDoc_OpenCLGlobalAddressSpace = internal constant [439 x i8] c"The global address space attribute specifies that an object is allocated in\0Aglobal memory, which is accessible by all work items. The content stored in this\0Amemory area persists between kernel executions. Pointer types to the global\0Aaddress space are allowed as function parameters or local variables. Starting\0Awith OpenCL v2.0, the global address space can be used with global (program\0Ascope) variables and static local variable as well.\00", align 16
@_ZL38AttrDoc_OpenCLGlobalDeviceAddressSpace = internal constant [863 x i8] c"The ``global_device`` and ``global_host`` address space attributes specify that\0Aan object is allocated in global memory on the device/host. It helps to\0Adistinguish USM (Unified Shared Memory) pointers that access global device\0Amemory from those that access global host memory. These new address spaces are\0Aa subset of the ``__global/opencl_global`` address space, the full address space\0Aset model for OpenCL 2.0 with the extension looks as follows:\0A\0A  | generic->global->host\0A  |                ->device\0A  |        ->private\0A  |        ->local\0A  | constant\0A\0AAs ``global_device`` and ``global_host`` are a subset of\0A``__global/opencl_global`` address spaces it is allowed to convert\0A``global_device`` and ``global_host`` address spaces to\0A``__global/opencl_global`` address spaces (following ISO/IEC TR 18037 5.1.3\0A\22Address space nesting and rules for pointers\22).\00", align 16
@_ZL36AttrDoc_OpenCLGlobalHostAddressSpace = internal constant [863 x i8] c"The ``global_device`` and ``global_host`` address space attributes specify that\0Aan object is allocated in global memory on the device/host. It helps to\0Adistinguish USM (Unified Shared Memory) pointers that access global device\0Amemory from those that access global host memory. These new address spaces are\0Aa subset of the ``__global/opencl_global`` address space, the full address space\0Aset model for OpenCL 2.0 with the extension looks as follows:\0A\0A  | generic->global->host\0A  |                ->device\0A  |        ->private\0A  |        ->local\0A  | constant\0A\0AAs ``global_device`` and ``global_host`` are a subset of\0A``__global/opencl_global`` address spaces it is allowed to convert\0A``global_device`` and ``global_host`` address spaces to\0A``__global/opencl_global`` address spaces (following ISO/IEC TR 18037 5.1.3\0A\22Address space nesting and rules for pointers\22).\00", align 16
@_ZL31AttrDoc_OpenCLLocalAddressSpace = internal constant [465 x i8] c"The local address space specifies that an object is allocated in the local (work\0Agroup) memory area, which is accessible to all work items in the same work\0Agroup. The content stored in this memory region is not accessible after\0Athe kernel execution ends. In a kernel function scope, any variable can be in\0Athe local address space. In other scopes, only pointer types to the local address\0Aspace are allowed. Local address space variables cannot have an initializer.\00", align 16
@_ZL33AttrDoc_OpenCLPrivateAddressSpace = internal constant [433 x i8] c"The private address space specifies that an object is allocated in the private\0A(work item) memory. Other work items cannot access the same memory area and its\0Acontent is destroyed after work item execution ends. Local variables can be\0Adeclared in the private address space. Function arguments are always in the\0Aprivate address space. Kernel function arguments of a pointer or an array type\0Acannot point to the private address space.\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_Ptr32 = internal constant [326 x i8] c"The ``__ptr32`` qualifier represents a native pointer on a 32-bit system. On a\0A64-bit system, a pointer with ``__ptr32`` is extended to a 64-bit pointer. The\0A``__sptr`` and ``__uptr`` qualifiers can be used to specify whether the pointer\0Ais sign extended or zero extended. This qualifier is enabled under\0A``-fms-extensions``.\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_Ptr64 = internal constant [203 x i8] c"The ``__ptr64`` qualifier represents a native pointer on a 64-bit system. On a\0A32-bit system, a ``__ptr64`` pointer is truncated to a 32-bit pointer. This\0Aqualifier is enabled under ``-fms-extensions``.\00", align 16
@_ZL12AttrDoc_SPtr = internal constant [117 x i8] c"The ``__sptr`` qualifier specifies that a 32-bit pointer should be sign\0Aextended when converted to a 64-bit pointer.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_TypeNonNull = internal constant [648 x i8] c"The ``_Nonnull`` nullability qualifier indicates that null is not a meaningful\0Avalue for a value of the ``_Nonnull`` pointer type. For example, given a\0Adeclaration such as:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    int fetch(int * _Nonnull ptr);\0A\0Aa caller of ``fetch`` should not provide a null value, and the compiler will\0Aproduce a warning if it sees a literal null value passed to ``fetch``. Note\0Athat, unlike the declaration attribute ``nonnull``, the presence of\0A``_Nonnull`` does not imply that passing null is undefined behavior: ``fetch``\0Ais free to consider null undefined behavior or (perhaps for\0Abackward-compatibility reasons) defensively handle null.\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_TypeNullUnspecified = internal constant [372 x i8] c"The ``_Null_unspecified`` nullability qualifier indicates that neither the\0A``_Nonnull`` nor ``_Nullable`` qualifiers make sense for a particular pointer\0Atype. It is used primarily to indicate that the role of null with specific\0Apointers in a nullability-annotated header is unclear, e.g., due to\0Aoverly-complex implementations or historical factors with a long-lived API.\00", align 16
@_ZL26AttrDoc_TypeNullableResult = internal constant [941 x i8] c"The ``_Nullable_result`` nullability qualifier means that a value of the\0A``_Nullable_result`` pointer can be ``nil``, just like ``_Nullable``. Where this\0Aattribute differs from ``_Nullable`` is when it's used on a parameter to a\0Acompletion handler in a Swift async method. For instance, here:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: objc\0A\0A    -(void)fetchSomeDataWithID:(int)identifier\0A             completionHandler:(void (^)(Data *_Nullable_result result, NSError *error))completionHandler;\0A\0AThis method asynchronously calls ``completionHandler`` when the data is\0Aavailable, or calls it with an error. ``_Nullable_result`` indicates to the\0ASwift importer that this is the uncommon case where ``result`` can get ``nil``\0Aeven if no error has occurred, and will therefore import it as a Swift optional\0Atype. Otherwise, if ``result`` was annotated with ``_Nullable``, the Swift\0Aimporter will assume that ``result`` will always be non-nil unless an error\0Aoccurred.\00", align 16
@_ZL12AttrDoc_UPtr = internal constant [117 x i8] c"The ``__uptr`` qualifier specifies that a 32-bit pointer should be zero\0Aextended when converted to a 64-bit pointer.\00", align 16
@_ZL26AttrDoc_WebAssemblyFuncref = internal constant [516 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((export_name(<name>)))``\0Aattribute for the WebAssembly target. This attribute may be attached to a\0Afunction declaration, where it modifies how the symbol is to be exported\0Afrom the linked WebAssembly.\0A\0AWebAssembly functions are exported via string name. By default when a symbol\0Ais exported, the export name for C/C++ symbols are the same as their C/C++\0Asymbol names. This attribute can be used to override the default behavior, and\0Arequest a specific string name be used instead.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_CXXAssume = internal constant [628 x i8] c"The ``assume`` attribute is used to indicate to the optimizer that a\0Acertain condition is assumed to be true at a certain point in the\0Aprogram. If this condition is violated at runtime, the behavior is\0Aundefined. ``assume`` can only be applied to a null statement.\0A\0ADifferent optimisers are likely to react differently to the presence of\0Athis attribute; in some cases, adding ``assume`` may affect performance\0Anegatively. It should be used with parsimony and care.\0A\0AExample:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  int f(int x, int y) {\0A    [[assume(x == 27)]];\0A    [[assume(x == y)]];\0A    return y + 1; // May be optimised to `return 28`.\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_CodeAlign = internal constant [849 x i8] c"The ``clang::code_align(N)`` attribute applies to a loop and specifies the byte\0Aalignment for a loop. The attribute accepts a positive integer constant\0Ainitialization expression indicating the number of bytes for the minimum\0Aalignment boundary. Its value must be a power of 2, between 1 and 4096\0A(inclusive).\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void foo() {\0A    int var = 0;\0A    [[clang::code_align(16)]] for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) var++;\0A  }\0A\0A  void Array(int *array, size_t n) {\0A    [[clang::code_align(64)]] for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) array[i] = 0;\0A  }\0A\0A  void count () {\0A    int a1[10], int i = 0;\0A    [[clang::code_align(32)]] while (i < 10) { a1[i] += 3; }\0A  }\0A\0A  void check() {\0A    int a = 10;\0A    [[clang::code_align(8)]] do {\0A      a = a + 1;\0A    } while (a < 20);\0A  }\0A\0A  template<int A>\0A  void func() {\0A    [[clang::code_align(A)]] for(;;) { }\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_FallThrough = internal constant [1612 x i8] c"The ``fallthrough`` (or ``clang::fallthrough``) attribute is used\0Ato annotate intentional fall-through\0Abetween switch labels. It can only be applied to a null statement placed at a\0Apoint of execution between any statement and the next switch label. It is\0Acommon to mark these places with a specific comment, but this attribute is\0Ameant to replace comments with a more strict annotation, which can be checked\0Aby the compiler. This attribute doesn't change semantics of the code and can\0Abe used wherever an intended fall-through occurs. It is designed to mimic\0Acontrol-flow statements like ``break;``, so it can be placed in most places\0Awhere ``break;`` can, but only if there are no statements on the execution path\0Abetween it and the next switch label.\0A\0ABy default, Clang does not warn on unannotated fallthrough from one ``switch``\0Acase to another. Diagnostics on fallthrough without a corresponding annotation\0Acan be enabled with the ``-Wimplicit-fallthrough`` argument.\0A\0AHere is an example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  // compile with -Wimplicit-fallthrough\0A  switch (n) {\0A  case 22:\0A  case 33:  // no warning: no statements between case labels\0A    f();\0A  case 44:  // warning: unannotated fall-through\0A    g();\0A    [[clang::fallthrough]];\0A  case 55:  // no warning\0A    if (x) {\0A      h();\0A      break;\0A    }\0A    else {\0A      i();\0A      [[clang::fallthrough]];\0A    }\0A  case 66:  // no warning\0A    p();\0A    [[clang::fallthrough]]; // warning: fallthrough annotation does not\0A                            //          directly precede case label\0A    q();\0A  case 77:  // warning: unannotated fall-through\0A    r();\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_HLSLControlFlowHint = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL20AttrDoc_HLSLLoopHint = internal constant [574 x i8] c"The ``[loop]`` directive allows loop optimization hints to be\0Aspecified for the subsequent loop. The directive allows unrolling to\0Abe disabled and is not compatible with [unroll(x)].\0A\0ASpecifying the parameter, ``[loop]``, directs the\0Aunroller to not unroll the loop.\0A\0A.. code-block:: hlsl\0A\0A  [loop]\0A  for (...) {\0A    ...\0A  }\0A\0A.. code-block:: hlsl\0A\0A  [loop]\0A  while (...) {\0A    ...\0A  }\0A\0A.. code-block:: hlsl\0A\0A  [loop]\0A  do {\0A    ...\0A  } while (...)\0A\0ASee `hlsl loop extensions <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3dhlsl/dx-graphics-hlsl-for>`_\0Afor details.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Likely = internal constant [4943 x i8] c"The ``likely`` and ``unlikely`` attributes are used as compiler hints.\0AThe attributes are used to aid the compiler to determine which branch is\0Alikely or unlikely to be taken. This is done by marking the branch substatement\0Awith one of the two attributes.\0A\0AIt isn't allowed to annotate a single statement with both ``likely`` and\0A``unlikely``. Annotating the ``true`` and ``false`` branch of an ``if``\0Astatement with the same likelihood attribute will result in a diagnostic and\0Athe attributes are ignored on both branches.\0A\0AIn a ``switch`` statement it's allowed to annotate multiple ``case`` labels\0Aor the ``default`` label with the same likelihood attribute. This makes\0A* all labels without an attribute have a neutral likelihood,\0A* all labels marked ``[[likely]]`` have an equally positive likelihood, and\0A* all labels marked ``[[unlikely]]`` have an equally negative likelihood.\0AThe neutral likelihood is the more likely of path execution than the negative\0Alikelihood. The positive likelihood is the more likely of path of execution\0Athan the neutral likelihood.\0A\0AThese attributes have no effect on the generated code when using\0APGO (Profile-Guided Optimization) or at optimization level 0.\0A\0AIn Clang, the attributes will be ignored if they're not placed on\0A* the ``case`` or ``default`` label of a ``switch`` statement,\0A* or on the substatement of an ``if`` or ``else`` statement,\0A* or on the substatement of an ``for`` or ``while`` statement.\0AThe C++ Standard recommends to honor them on every statement in the\0Apath of execution, but that can be confusing:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  if (b) {\0A    [[unlikely]] --b; // In the path of execution,\0A                      // this branch is considered unlikely.\0A  }\0A\0A  if (b) {\0A    --b;\0A    if(b)\0A      return;\0A    [[unlikely]] --b; // Not in the path of execution,\0A  }                   // the branch has no likelihood information.\0A\0A  if (b) {\0A    --b;\0A    foo(b);\0A    // Whether or not the next statement is in the path of execution depends\0A    // on the declaration of foo():\0A    // In the path of execution: void foo(int);\0A    // Not in the path of execution: [[noreturn]] void foo(int);\0A    // This means the likelihood of the branch depends on the declaration\0A    // of foo().\0A    [[unlikely]] --b;\0A  }\0A\0A\0ABelow are some example usages of the likelihood attributes and their effects:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  if (b) [[likely]] { // Placement on the first statement in the branch.\0A    // The compiler will optimize to execute the code here.\0A  } else {\0A  }\0A\0A  if (b)\0A    [[unlikely]] b++; // Placement on the first statement in the branch.\0A  else {\0A    // The compiler will optimize to execute the code here.\0A  }\0A\0A  if (b) {\0A    [[unlikely]] b++; // Placement on the second statement in the branch.\0A  }                   // The attribute will be ignored.\0A\0A  if (b) [[likely]] {\0A    [[unlikely]] b++; // No contradiction since the second attribute\0A  }                   // is ignored.\0A\0A  if (b)\0A    ;\0A  else [[likely]] {\0A    // The compiler will optimize to execute the code here.\0A  }\0A\0A  if (b)\0A    ;\0A  else\0A    // The compiler will optimize to execute the next statement.\0A    [[likely]] b = f();\0A\0A  if (b) [[likely]]; // Both branches are likely. A diagnostic is issued\0A  else [[likely]];   // and the attributes are ignored.\0A\0A  if (b)\0A    [[likely]] int i = 5; // Issues a diagnostic since the attribute\0A                          // isn't allowed on a declaration.\0A\0A  switch (i) {\0A    [[likely]] case 1:    // This value is likely\0A      ...\0A      break;\0A\0A    [[unlikely]] case 2:  // This value is unlikely\0A      ...\0A      [[fallthrough]];\0A\0A    case 3:               // No likelihood attribute\0A      ...\0A      [[likely]] break;   // No effect\0A\0A    case 4: [[likely]] {  // attribute on substatement has no effect\0A      ...\0A      break;\0A      }\0A\0A    [[unlikely]] default: // All other values are unlikely\0A      ...\0A      break;\0A  }\0A\0A  switch (i) {\0A    [[likely]] case 0:    // This value and code path is likely\0A      ...\0A      [[fallthrough]];\0A\0A    case 1:               // No likelihood attribute, code path is neutral\0A      break;              // falling through has no effect on the likelihood\0A\0A    case 2:               // No likelihood attribute, code path is neutral\0A      [[fallthrough]];\0A\0A    [[unlikely]] default: // This value and code path are both unlikely\0A      break;\0A  }\0A\0A  for(int i = 0; i != size; ++i) [[likely]] {\0A    ...               // The loop is the likely path of execution\0A  }\0A\0A  for(const auto &E : Elements) [[likely]] {\0A    ...               // The loop is the likely path of execution\0A  }\0A\0A  while(i != size) [[unlikely]] {\0A    ...               // The loop is the unlikely path of execution\0A  }                   // The generated code will optimize to skip the loop body\0A\0A  while(true) [[unlikely]] {\0A    ...               // The attribute has no effect\0A  }                   // Clang elides the comparison and generates an infinite\0A                      // loop\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_MustTail = internal constant [1712 x i8] c"If a ``return`` statement is marked ``musttail``, this indicates that the\0Acompiler must generate a tail call for the program to be correct, even when\0Aoptimizations are disabled. This guarantees that the call will not cause\0Aunbounded stack growth if it is part of a recursive cycle in the call graph.\0A\0AIf the callee is a virtual function that is implemented by a thunk, there is\0Ano guarantee in general that the thunk tail-calls the implementation of the\0Avirtual function, so such a call in a recursive cycle can still result in\0Aunbounded stack growth.\0A\0A``clang::musttail`` can only be applied to a ``return`` statement whose value\0Ais the result of a function call (even functions returning void must use\0A``return``, although no value is returned). The target function must have the\0Asame number of arguments as the caller. The types of the return value and all\0Aarguments must be similar according to C++ rules (differing only in cv\0Aqualifiers or array size), including the implicit \22this\22 argument, if any.\0AAny variables in scope, including all arguments to the function and the\0Areturn value must be trivially destructible. The calling convention of the\0Acaller and callee must match, and they must not be variadic functions or have\0Aold style K&R C function declarations.\0A\0AThe lifetimes of all local variables and function parameters end immediately\0Abefore the call to the function. This means that it is undefined behaviour to\0Apass a pointer or reference to a local variable to the called function, which\0Ais not the case without the attribute. Clang will emit a warning in common\0Acases where this happens.\0A\0A``clang::musttail`` provides assurances that the tail call can be optimized on\0Aall targets, not just one.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_OpenCLUnrollHint = internal constant [395 x i8] c"The opencl_unroll_hint attribute qualifier can be used to specify that a loop\0A(for, while and do loops) can be unrolled. This attribute qualifier can be\0Aused to specify full unrolling or partial unrolling by a specified amount.\0AThis is a compiler hint and the compiler may ignore this directive. See\0A`OpenCL v2.0 <https://www.khronos.org/registry/cl/specs/opencl-2.0.pdf>`_\0As6.11.5 for details.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_Unlikely = internal constant [4943 x i8] c"The ``likely`` and ``unlikely`` attributes are used as compiler hints.\0AThe attributes are used to aid the compiler to determine which branch is\0Alikely or unlikely to be taken. This is done by marking the branch substatement\0Awith one of the two attributes.\0A\0AIt isn't allowed to annotate a single statement with both ``likely`` and\0A``unlikely``. Annotating the ``true`` and ``false`` branch of an ``if``\0Astatement with the same likelihood attribute will result in a diagnostic and\0Athe attributes are ignored on both branches.\0A\0AIn a ``switch`` statement it's allowed to annotate multiple ``case`` labels\0Aor the ``default`` label with the same likelihood attribute. This makes\0A* all labels without an attribute have a neutral likelihood,\0A* all labels marked ``[[likely]]`` have an equally positive likelihood, and\0A* all labels marked ``[[unlikely]]`` have an equally negative likelihood.\0AThe neutral likelihood is the more likely of path execution than the negative\0Alikelihood. The positive likelihood is the more likely of path of execution\0Athan the neutral likelihood.\0A\0AThese attributes have no effect on the generated code when using\0APGO (Profile-Guided Optimization) or at optimization level 0.\0A\0AIn Clang, the attributes will be ignored if they're not placed on\0A* the ``case`` or ``default`` label of a ``switch`` statement,\0A* or on the substatement of an ``if`` or ``else`` statement,\0A* or on the substatement of an ``for`` or ``while`` statement.\0AThe C++ Standard recommends to honor them on every statement in the\0Apath of execution, but that can be confusing:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  if (b) {\0A    [[unlikely]] --b; // In the path of execution,\0A                      // this branch is considered unlikely.\0A  }\0A\0A  if (b) {\0A    --b;\0A    if(b)\0A      return;\0A    [[unlikely]] --b; // Not in the path of execution,\0A  }                   // the branch has no likelihood information.\0A\0A  if (b) {\0A    --b;\0A    foo(b);\0A    // Whether or not the next statement is in the path of execution depends\0A    // on the declaration of foo():\0A    // In the path of execution: void foo(int);\0A    // Not in the path of execution: [[noreturn]] void foo(int);\0A    // This means the likelihood of the branch depends on the declaration\0A    // of foo().\0A    [[unlikely]] --b;\0A  }\0A\0A\0ABelow are some example usages of the likelihood attributes and their effects:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  if (b) [[likely]] { // Placement on the first statement in the branch.\0A    // The compiler will optimize to execute the code here.\0A  } else {\0A  }\0A\0A  if (b)\0A    [[unlikely]] b++; // Placement on the first statement in the branch.\0A  else {\0A    // The compiler will optimize to execute the code here.\0A  }\0A\0A  if (b) {\0A    [[unlikely]] b++; // Placement on the second statement in the branch.\0A  }                   // The attribute will be ignored.\0A\0A  if (b) [[likely]] {\0A    [[unlikely]] b++; // No contradiction since the second attribute\0A  }                   // is ignored.\0A\0A  if (b)\0A    ;\0A  else [[likely]] {\0A    // The compiler will optimize to execute the code here.\0A  }\0A\0A  if (b)\0A    ;\0A  else\0A    // The compiler will optimize to execute the next statement.\0A    [[likely]] b = f();\0A\0A  if (b) [[likely]]; // Both branches are likely. A diagnostic is issued\0A  else [[likely]];   // and the attributes are ignored.\0A\0A  if (b)\0A    [[likely]] int i = 5; // Issues a diagnostic since the attribute\0A                          // isn't allowed on a declaration.\0A\0A  switch (i) {\0A    [[likely]] case 1:    // This value is likely\0A      ...\0A      break;\0A\0A    [[unlikely]] case 2:  // This value is unlikely\0A      ...\0A      [[fallthrough]];\0A\0A    case 3:               // No likelihood attribute\0A      ...\0A      [[likely]] break;   // No effect\0A\0A    case 4: [[likely]] {  // attribute on substatement has no effect\0A      ...\0A      break;\0A      }\0A\0A    [[unlikely]] default: // All other values are unlikely\0A      ...\0A      break;\0A  }\0A\0A  switch (i) {\0A    [[likely]] case 0:    // This value and code path is likely\0A      ...\0A      [[fallthrough]];\0A\0A    case 1:               // No likelihood attribute, code path is neutral\0A      break;              // falling through has no effect on the likelihood\0A\0A    case 2:               // No likelihood attribute, code path is neutral\0A      [[fallthrough]];\0A\0A    [[unlikely]] default: // This value and code path are both unlikely\0A      break;\0A  }\0A\0A  for(int i = 0; i != size; ++i) [[likely]] {\0A    ...               // The loop is the likely path of execution\0A  }\0A\0A  for(const auto &E : Elements) [[likely]] {\0A    ...               // The loop is the likely path of execution\0A  }\0A\0A  while(i != size) [[unlikely]] {\0A    ...               // The loop is the unlikely path of execution\0A  }                   // The generated code will optimize to skip the loop body\0A\0A  while(true) [[unlikely]] {\0A    ...               // The attribute has no effect\0A  }                   // Clang elides the comparison and generates an infinite\0A                      // loop\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_AlwaysInline = internal constant [1635 x i8] c"Inlining heuristics are disabled and inlining is always attempted regardless of\0Aoptimization level.\0A\0A``[[clang::always_inline]]`` spelling can be used as a statement attribute; other\0Aspellings of the attribute are not supported on statements. If a statement is\0Amarked ``[[clang::always_inline]]`` and contains calls, the compiler attempts\0Ato inline those calls.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int example(void) {\0A    int i;\0A    [[clang::always_inline]] foo(); // attempts to inline foo\0A    [[clang::always_inline]] i = bar(); // attempts to inline bar\0A    [[clang::always_inline]] return f(42, baz(bar())); // attempts to inline everything\0A  }\0A\0AA declaration statement, which is a statement, is not a statement that can have an\0Aattribute associated with it (the attribute applies to the declaration, not the\0Astatement in that case). So this use case will not work:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int example(void) {\0A    [[clang::always_inline]] int i = bar();\0A    return i;\0A  }\0A\0AThis attribute does not guarantee that inline substitution actually occurs.\0A\0A<ins>Note: applying this attribute to a coroutine at the `-O0` optimization level\0Ahas no effect; other optimization levels may only partially inline and result in a\0Adiagnostic.</ins>\0A\0ASee also `the Microsoft Docs on Inline Functions`_, `the GCC Common Function\0AAttribute docs`_, and `the GCC Inline docs`_.\0A\0A.. _the Microsoft Docs on Inline Functions: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/cpp/inline-functions-cpp\0A.. _the GCC Common Function Attribute docs: https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Common-Function-Attributes.html\0A.. _the GCC Inline docs: https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Inline.html\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_NoInline = internal constant [731 x i8] c"This function attribute suppresses the inlining of a function at the call sites\0Aof the function.\0A\0A``[[clang::noinline]]`` spelling can be used as a statement attribute; other\0Aspellings of the attribute are not supported on statements. If a statement is\0Amarked ``[[clang::noinline]]`` and contains calls, those calls inside the\0Astatement will not be inlined by the compiler.\0A\0A``__noinline__`` can be used as a keyword in CUDA/HIP languages. This is to\0Aavoid diagnostics due to usage of ``__attribute__((__noinline__))``\0Awith ``__noinline__`` defined as a macro as ``__attribute__((noinline))``.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int example(void) {\0A    int r;\0A    [[clang::noinline]] foo();\0A    [[clang::noinline]] r = bar();\0A    return r;\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_NoMerge = internal constant [1472 x i8] c"If a statement is marked ``nomerge`` and contains call expressions, those call\0Aexpressions inside the statement will not be merged during optimization. This\0Aattribute can be used to prevent the optimizer from obscuring the source\0Alocation of certain calls. For example, it will prevent tail merging otherwise\0Aidentical code sequences that raise an exception or terminate the program. Tail\0Amerging normally reduces the precision of source location information, making\0Astack traces less useful for debugging. This attribute gives the user control\0Aover the tradeoff between code size and debug information precision.\0A\0A``nomerge`` attribute can also be used as function attribute to prevent all\0Acalls to the specified function from merging. It has no effect on indirect\0Acalls to such functions. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  [[clang::nomerge]] void foo(int) {}\0A\0A  void bar(int x) {\0A    auto *ptr = foo;\0A    if (x) foo(1); else foo(2); // will not be merged\0A    if (x) ptr(1); else ptr(2); // indirect call, can be merged\0A  }\0A\0A``nomerge`` attribute can also be used for pointers to functions to\0Aprevent calls through such pointer from merging. In such case the\0Aeffect applies only to a specific function pointer. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  [[clang::nomerge]] void (*foo)(int);\0A\0A  void bar(int x) {\0A    auto *ptr = foo;\0A    if (x) foo(1); else foo(2); // will not be merged\0A    if (x) ptr(1); else ptr(2); // 'ptr' has no 'nomerge' attribute, can be merged\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_Suppress = internal constant [3542 x i8] c"The ``suppress`` attribute suppresses unwanted warnings coming from static\0Aanalysis tools such as the Clang Static Analyzer. The tool will not report\0Aany issues in source code annotated with the attribute.\0A\0AThe attribute cannot be used to suppress traditional Clang warnings, because\0Amany such warnings are emitted before the attribute is fully parsed.\0AConsider using ``#pragma clang diagnostic`` to control such diagnostics,\0Aas described in `Controlling Diagnostics via Pragmas\0A<https://clang.llvm.org/docs/UsersManual.html#controlling-diagnostics-via-pragmas>`_.\0A\0AThe ``suppress`` attribute can be placed on an individual statement in order to\0Asuppress warnings about undesirable behavior occurring at that statement:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  int foo() {\0A    int *x = nullptr;\0A    ...\0A    [[clang::suppress]]\0A    return *x;  // null pointer dereference warning suppressed here\0A  }\0A\0APutting the attribute on a compound statement suppresses all warnings in scope:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  int foo() {\0A    [[clang::suppress]] {\0A      int *x = nullptr;\0A      ...\0A      return *x;  // warnings suppressed in the entire scope\0A    }\0A  }\0A\0AThe attribute can also be placed on entire declarations of functions, classes,\0Avariables, member variables, and so on, to suppress warnings related\0Ato the declarations themselves. When used this way, the attribute additionally\0Asuppresses all warnings in the lexical scope of the declaration:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  class [[clang::suppress]] C {\0A    int foo() {\0A      int *x = nullptr;\0A      ...\0A      return *x;  // warnings suppressed in the entire class scope\0A    }\0A\0A    int bar();\0A  };\0A\0A  int C::bar() {\0A    int *x = nullptr;\0A    ...\0A    return *x;  // warning NOT suppressed! - not lexically nested in 'class C{}'\0A  }\0A\0ASome static analysis warnings are accompanied by one or more notes, and the\0Aline of code against which the warning is emitted isn't necessarily the best\0Afor suppression purposes. In such cases the tools are allowed to implement\0Aadditional ways to suppress specific warnings based on the attribute attached\0Ato a note location.\0A\0AFor example, the Clang Static Analyzer suppresses memory leak warnings when\0Athe suppression attribute is placed at the allocation site (highlited by\0Aa \22note: memory is allocated\22), which may be different from the line of code\0Aat which the program \22loses track\22 of the pointer (where the warning\0Ais ultimately emitted):\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int bar1(bool coin_flip) {\0A    __attribute__((suppress))\0A    int *result = (int *)malloc(sizeof(int));\0A    if (coin_flip)\0A      return 1;  // warning about this leak path is suppressed\0A\0A    return *result;  // warning about this leak path is also suppressed\0A  }\0A\0A  int bar2(bool coin_flip) {\0A    int *result = (int *)malloc(sizeof(int));\0A    if (coin_flip)\0A      return 1;  // leak warning on this path NOT suppressed\0A\0A    __attribute__((suppress))\0A    return *result;  // leak warning is suppressed only on this path\0A  }\0A\0A\0AWhen written as ``[[gsl::suppress]]``, this attribute suppresses specific\0Aclang-tidy diagnostics for rules of the `C++ Core Guidelines`_ in a portable\0Away. The attribute can be attached to declarations, statements, and at\0Anamespace scope.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  [[gsl::suppress(\22Rh-public\22)]]\0A  void f_() {\0A    int *p;\0A    [[gsl::suppress(\22type\22)]] {\0A      p = reinterpret_cast<int*>(7);\0A    }\0A  }\0A  namespace N {\0A    [[clang::suppress(\22type\22, \22bounds\22)]];\0A    ...\0A  }\0A\0A.. _`C++ Core Guidelines`: https://github.com/isocpp/CppCoreGuidelines/blob/master/CppCoreGuidelines.md#inforce-enforcement\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_AArch64SVEPcs = internal constant [1071 x i8] c"On AArch64 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of a\0Afunction to preserve additional Scalable Vector registers and Scalable\0APredicate registers relative to the default calling convention used for\0AAArch64.\0A\0AThis means it is more efficient to call such functions from code that performs\0Aextensive scalable vector and scalable predicate calculations, because fewer\0Alive SVE registers need to be saved. This property makes it well-suited for SVE\0Amath library functions, which are typically leaf functions that require a small\0Anumber of registers.\0A\0AHowever, using this attribute also means that it is more expensive to call\0Aa function that adheres to the default calling convention from within such\0Aa function. Therefore, it is recommended that this attribute is only used\0Afor leaf functions.\0A\0AFor more information, see the documentation for `aarch64_sve_pcs` in the\0AARM C Language Extension (ACLE) documentation.\0A\0A.. _`aarch64_sve_pcs`: https://github.com/ARM-software/acle/blob/main/main/acle.md#scalable-vector-extension-procedure-call-standard-attribute\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_AArch64VectorPcs = internal constant [1046 x i8] c"On AArch64 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of a\0Afunction to preserve additional floating-point and Advanced SIMD registers\0Arelative to the default calling convention used for AArch64.\0A\0AThis means it is more efficient to call such functions from code that performs\0Aextensive floating-point and vector calculations, because fewer live SIMD and FP\0Aregisters need to be saved. This property makes it well-suited for e.g.\0Afloating-point or vector math library functions, which are typically leaf\0Afunctions that require a small number of registers.\0A\0AHowever, using this attribute also means that it is more expensive to call\0Aa function that adheres to the default calling convention from within such\0Aa function. Therefore, it is recommended that this attribute is only used\0Afor leaf functions.\0A\0AFor more information, see the documentation for `aarch64_vector_pcs`_ on\0Athe Arm Developer website.\0A\0A.. _`aarch64_vector_pcs`: https://developer.arm.com/products/software-development-tools/hpc/arm-compiler-for-hpc/vector-function-abi\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_AMDGPUKernelCall = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_AcquireHandle = internal constant [838 x i8] c"If this annotation is on a function or a function type it is assumed to return\0Aa new handle. In case this annotation is on an output parameter,\0Athe function is assumed to fill the corresponding argument with a new\0Ahandle. The attribute requires a string literal argument which used to\0Aidentify the handle with later uses of ``use_handle`` or\0A``release_handle``.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  // Output arguments from Zircon.\0A  zx_status_t zx_socket_create(uint32_t options,\0A                               zx_handle_t __attribute__((acquire_handle(\22zircon\22))) * out0,\0A                               zx_handle_t* out1 [[clang::acquire_handle(\22zircon\22)]]);\0A\0A\0A  // Returned handle.\0A  [[clang::acquire_handle(\22tag\22)]] int open(const char *path, int oflag, ... );\0A  int open(const char *path, int oflag, ... ) __attribute__((acquire_handle(\22tag\22)));\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_AnyX86NoCfCheck = internal constant [646 x i8] c"Jump Oriented Programming attacks rely on tampering with addresses used by\0Aindirect call / jmp, e.g. redirect control-flow to non-programmer\0Aintended bytes in the binary.\0AX86 Supports Indirect Branch Tracking (IBT) as part of Control-Flow\0AEnforcement Technology (CET). IBT instruments ENDBR instructions used to\0Aspecify valid targets of indirect call / jmp.\0AThe ``nocf_check`` attribute has two roles:\0A1. Appertains to a function - do not add ENDBR instruction at the beginning of\0Athe function.\0A2. Appertains to a function pointer - do not track the target function of this\0Apointer (by adding nocf_check prefix to the indirect-call instruction).\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_CDecl = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_CountedBy = internal constant [2279 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``counted_by`` attribute on the flexible array member of a\0Astructure in C. The argument for the attribute is the name of a field member\0Aholding the count of elements in the flexible array. This information can be\0Aused to improve the results of the array bound sanitizer and the\0A``__builtin_dynamic_object_size`` builtin. The ``count`` field member must be\0Awithin the same non-anonymous, enclosing struct as the flexible array member.\0A\0AThis example specifies that the flexible array member ``array`` has the number\0Aof elements allocated for it in ``count``:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  struct bar;\0A\0A  struct foo {\0A    size_t count;\0A    char other;\0A    struct bar *array[] __attribute__((counted_by(count)));\0A  };\0A\0AThis establishes a relationship between ``array`` and ``count``. Specifically,\0A``array`` must have at least ``count`` number of elements available. It's the\0Auser's responsibility to ensure that this relationship is maintained through\0Achanges to the structure.\0A\0AIn the following example, the allocated array erroneously has fewer elements\0Athan what's specified by ``p->count``. This would result in an out-of-bounds\0Aaccess not being detected.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #define SIZE_INCR 42\0A\0A  struct foo *p;\0A\0A  void foo_alloc(size_t count) {\0A    p = malloc(MAX(sizeof(struct foo),\0A                   offsetof(struct foo, array[0]) + count * sizeof(struct bar *)));\0A    p->count = count + SIZE_INCR;\0A  }\0A\0AThe next example updates ``p->count``, but breaks the relationship requirement\0Athat ``p->array`` must have at least ``p->count`` number of elements available:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #define SIZE_INCR 42\0A\0A  struct foo *p;\0A\0A  void foo_alloc(size_t count) {\0A    p = malloc(MAX(sizeof(struct foo),\0A                   offsetof(struct foo, array[0]) + count * sizeof(struct bar *)));\0A    p->count = count;\0A  }\0A\0A  void use_foo(int index, int val) {\0A    p->count += SIZE_INCR + 1; /* 'count' is now larger than the number of elements of 'array'. */\0A    p->array[index] = val;     /* The sanitizer can't properly check this access. */\0A  }\0A\0AIn this example, an update to ``p->count`` maintains the relationship\0Arequirement:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void use_foo(int index, int val) {\0A    if (p->count == 0)\0A      return;\0A    --p->count;\0A    p->array[index] = val;\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_CountedByOrNull = internal constant [2279 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``counted_by`` attribute on the flexible array member of a\0Astructure in C. The argument for the attribute is the name of a field member\0Aholding the count of elements in the flexible array. This information can be\0Aused to improve the results of the array bound sanitizer and the\0A``__builtin_dynamic_object_size`` builtin. The ``count`` field member must be\0Awithin the same non-anonymous, enclosing struct as the flexible array member.\0A\0AThis example specifies that the flexible array member ``array`` has the number\0Aof elements allocated for it in ``count``:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  struct bar;\0A\0A  struct foo {\0A    size_t count;\0A    char other;\0A    struct bar *array[] __attribute__((counted_by(count)));\0A  };\0A\0AThis establishes a relationship between ``array`` and ``count``. Specifically,\0A``array`` must have at least ``count`` number of elements available. It's the\0Auser's responsibility to ensure that this relationship is maintained through\0Achanges to the structure.\0A\0AIn the following example, the allocated array erroneously has fewer elements\0Athan what's specified by ``p->count``. This would result in an out-of-bounds\0Aaccess not being detected.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #define SIZE_INCR 42\0A\0A  struct foo *p;\0A\0A  void foo_alloc(size_t count) {\0A    p = malloc(MAX(sizeof(struct foo),\0A                   offsetof(struct foo, array[0]) + count * sizeof(struct bar *)));\0A    p->count = count + SIZE_INCR;\0A  }\0A\0AThe next example updates ``p->count``, but breaks the relationship requirement\0Athat ``p->array`` must have at least ``p->count`` number of elements available:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #define SIZE_INCR 42\0A\0A  struct foo *p;\0A\0A  void foo_alloc(size_t count) {\0A    p = malloc(MAX(sizeof(struct foo),\0A                   offsetof(struct foo, array[0]) + count * sizeof(struct bar *)));\0A    p->count = count;\0A  }\0A\0A  void use_foo(int index, int val) {\0A    p->count += SIZE_INCR + 1; /* 'count' is now larger than the number of elements of 'array'. */\0A    p->array[index] = val;     /* The sanitizer can't properly check this access. */\0A  }\0A\0AIn this example, an update to ``p->count`` maintains the relationship\0Arequirement:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void use_foo(int index, int val) {\0A    if (p->count == 0)\0A      return;\0A    --p->count;\0A    p->array[index] = val;\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_FastCall = internal constant [609 x i8] c"On 32-bit x86 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of a\0Afunction to use ECX and EDX as register parameters and clear parameters off of\0Athe stack on return. This convention does not support variadic calls or\0Aunprototyped functions in C, and has no effect on x86_64 targets. This calling\0Aconvention is supported primarily for compatibility with existing code. Users\0Aseeking register parameters should use the ``regparm`` attribute, which does\0Anot require callee-cleanup. See the documentation for `__fastcall`_ on MSDN.\0A\0A.. _`__fastcall`: http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/6xa169sk.aspx\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_IntelOclBicc = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_LifetimeBound = internal constant [2469 x i8] c"The ``lifetimebound`` attribute on a function parameter or implicit object\0Aparameter indicates that objects that are referred to by that parameter may\0Aalso be referred to by the return value of the annotated function (or, for a\0Aparameter of a constructor, by the value of the constructed object).\0A\0ABy default, a reference is considered to refer to its referenced object, a\0Apointer is considered to refer to its pointee, a ``std::initializer_list<T>``\0Ais considered to refer to its underlying array, and aggregates (arrays and\0Asimple ``struct``\\s) are considered to refer to all objects that their\0Atransitive subobjects refer to.\0A\0AClang warns if it is able to detect that an object or reference refers to\0Aanother object with a shorter lifetime. For example, Clang will warn if a\0Afunction returns a reference to a local variable, or if a reference is bound to\0Aa temporary object whose lifetime is not extended. By using the\0A``lifetimebound`` attribute, this determination can be extended to look through\0Auser-declared functions. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    #include <map>\0A    #include <string>\0A\0A    using namespace std::literals;\0A\0A    // Returns m[key] if key is present, or default_value if not.\0A    template<typename T, typename U>\0A    const U &get_or_default(const std::map<T, U> &m [[clang::lifetimebound]],\0A                            const T &key, /* note, not lifetimebound */\0A                            const U &default_value [[clang::lifetimebound]]) {\0A      if (auto iter = m.find(key); iter != m.end()) return iter->second;\0A      else return default_value;\0A    }\0A\0A    int main() {\0A      std::map<std::string, std::string> m;\0A      // warning: temporary bound to local reference 'val1' will be destroyed\0A      // at the end of the full-expression\0A      const std::string &val1 = get_or_default(m, \22foo\22s, \22bar\22s);\0A\0A      // No warning in this case.\0A      std::string def_val = \22bar\22s;\0A      const std::string &val2 = get_or_default(m, \22foo\22s, def_val);\0A\0A      return 0;\0A    }\0A\0AThe attribute can be applied to the implicit ``this`` parameter of a member\0Afunction by writing the attribute after the function type:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    struct string {\0A      // The returned pointer should not outlive ``*this``.\0A      const char *data() const [[clang::lifetimebound]];\0A    };\0A\0AThis attribute is inspired by the C++ committee paper `P0936R0\0A<http://wg21.link/p0936r0>`_, but does not affect whether temporary objects\0Ahave their lifetimes extended.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_LifetimeCaptureBy = internal constant [3682 x i8] c"Similar to `lifetimebound`_, the ``lifetime_capture_by(X)`` attribute on a\0Afunction parameter or implicit object parameter indicates that the capturing\0Aentity ``X`` may refer to the object referred by that parameter.\0A\0ABelow is a list of types of the parameters and what they're considered to refer to:\0A\0A- A reference param (of non-view type) is considered to refer to its referenced object.\0A- A pointer param (of non-view type) is considered to refer to its pointee.\0A- View type param (type annotated with ``[[gsl::Pointer()]]``) is considered to refer\0A  to its pointee (gsl owner). This holds true even if the view type appears as a reference\0A  in the parameter. For example, both ``std::string_view`` and\0A  ``const std::string_view &`` are considered to refer to a ``std::string``.\0A- A ``std::initializer_list<T>`` is considered to refer to its underlying array.\0A- Aggregates (arrays and simple ``struct``\\s) are considered to refer to all\0A  objects that their transitive subobjects refer to.\0A\0AClang would diagnose when a temporary object is used as an argument to such an\0Aannotated parameter.\0AIn this case, the capturing entity ``X`` could capture a dangling reference to this\0Atemporary object.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void addToSet(std::string_view a [[clang::lifetime_capture_by(s)]], std::set<std::string_view>& s) {\0A    s.insert(a);\0A  }\0A  void use() {\0A    std::set<std::string_view> s;\0A    addToSet(std::string(), s); // Warning: object whose reference is captured by 's' will be destroyed at the end of the full-expression.\0A    //       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^\0A    std::string local;\0A    addToSet(local, s); // Ok.\0A  }\0A\0AThe capturing entity ``X`` can be one of the following:\0A\0A- Another (named) function parameter.\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    void addToSet(std::string_view a [[clang::lifetime_capture_by(s)]], std::set<std::string_view>& s) {\0A      s.insert(a);\0A    }\0A\0A- ``this`` (in case of member functions).\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    class S {\0A      void addToSet(std::string_view a [[clang::lifetime_capture_by(this)]]) {\0A        s.insert(a);\0A      }\0A      std::set<std::string_view> s;\0A    };\0A\0A  Note: When applied to a constructor parameter, `[[clang::lifetime_capture_by(this)]]` is just an alias of `[[clang::lifetimebound]]`.\0A\0A- `global`, `unknown`.\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    std::set<std::string_view> s;\0A    void addToSet(std::string_view a [[clang::lifetime_capture_by(global)]]) {\0A      s.insert(a);\0A    }\0A    void addSomewhere(std::string_view a [[clang::lifetime_capture_by(unknown)]]);\0A\0AThe attribute can be applied to the implicit ``this`` parameter of a member\0Afunction by writing the attribute after the function type:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  struct S {\0A    const char *data(std::set<S*>& s) [[clang::lifetime_capture_by(s)]] {\0A      s.insert(this);\0A    }\0A  };\0A\0AThe attribute supports specifying more than one capturing entities:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void addToSets(std::string_view a [[clang::lifetime_capture_by(s1, s2)]],\0A                 std::set<std::string_view>& s1,\0A                 std::set<std::string_view>& s2) {\0A    s1.insert(a);\0A    s2.insert(a);\0A  }\0A\0ALimitation: The capturing entity ``X`` is not used by the analysis and is\0Aused for documentation purposes only. This is because the analysis is\0Astatement-local and only detects use of a temporary as an argument to the\0Aannotated parameter.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void addToSet(std::string_view a [[clang::lifetime_capture_by(s)]], std::set<std::string_view>& s);\0A  void use() {\0A    std::set<std::string_view> s;\0A    if (foo()) {\0A      std::string str;\0A      addToSet(str, s); // Not detected.\0A    }\0A  }\0A\0A.. _`lifetimebound`: https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#lifetimebound\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_M68kRTD = internal constant [412 x i8] c"On M68k targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of a function\0Ato clear parameters off the stack on return. In other words, callee is\0Aresponsible for cleaning out the stack space allocated for incoming paramters.\0AThis convention does not support variadic calls or unprototyped functions in C.\0AWhen targeting M68010 or newer CPUs, this calling convention is implemented\0Ausing the `rtd` instruction.\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_MSABI = internal constant [218 x i8] c"On non-Windows x86_64 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of\0Aa function to match the default convention used on Windows x86_64. This\0Aattribute has no effect on Windows targets or non-x86_64 targets.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_NSReturnsRetained = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_ObjCOwnership = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Pascal = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL11AttrDoc_Pcs = internal constant [156 x i8] c"On ARM targets, this attribute can be used to select calling conventions\0Asimilar to ``stdcall`` on x86. Valid parameter values are \22aapcs\22 and\0A\22aapcs-vfp\22.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_PreserveAll = internal constant [1408 x i8] c"On X86-64 and AArch64 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of\0Aa function. The ``preserve_all`` calling convention attempts to make the code\0Ain the caller even less intrusive than the ``preserve_most`` calling convention.\0AThis calling convention also behaves identical to the ``C`` calling convention\0Aon how arguments and return values are passed, but it uses a different set of\0Acaller/callee-saved registers. This removes the burden of saving and\0Arecovering a large register set before and after the call in the caller. If\0Athe arguments are passed in callee-saved registers, then they will be\0Apreserved by the callee across the call. This doesn't apply for values\0Areturned in callee-saved registers.\0A\0A- On X86-64 the callee preserves all general purpose registers, except for\0A  R11. R11 can be used as a scratch register. Furthermore it also preserves\0A  all floating-point registers (XMMs/YMMs).\0A\0A- On AArch64 the callee preserve all general purpose registers, except X0-X8 and\0A  X16-X18. Furthermore it also preserves lower 128 bits of V8-V31 SIMD - floating\0A  point registers.\0A\0AThe idea behind this convention is to support calls to runtime functions\0Athat don't need to call out to any other functions.\0A\0AThis calling convention, like the ``preserve_most`` calling convention, will be\0Aused by a future version of the Objective-C runtime and should be considered\0Aexperimental at this time.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_PreserveMost = internal constant [2258 x i8] c"On X86-64 and AArch64 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of\0Aa function. The ``preserve_most`` calling convention attempts to make the code\0Ain the caller as unintrusive as possible. This convention behaves identically\0Ato the ``C`` calling convention on how arguments and return values are passed,\0Abut it uses a different set of caller/callee-saved registers. This alleviates\0Athe burden of saving and recovering a large register set before and after the\0Acall in the caller. If the arguments are passed in callee-saved registers,\0Athen they will be preserved by the callee across the call. This doesn't\0Aapply for values returned in callee-saved registers.\0A\0A- On X86-64 the callee preserves all general purpose registers, except for\0A  R11. R11 can be used as a scratch register. Floating-point registers\0A  (XMMs/YMMs) are not preserved and need to be saved by the caller.\0A\0A- On AArch64 the callee preserve all general purpose registers, except X0-X8 and\0A  X16-X18.\0A\0AThe idea behind this convention is to support calls to runtime functions\0Athat have a hot path and a cold path. The hot path is usually a small piece\0Aof code that doesn't use many registers. The cold path might need to call out to\0Aanother function and therefore only needs to preserve the caller-saved\0Aregisters, which haven't already been saved by the caller. The\0A``preserve_most`` calling convention is very similar to the ``cold`` calling\0Aconvention in terms of caller/callee-saved registers, but they are used for\0Adifferent types of function calls. ``coldcc`` is for function calls that are\0Ararely executed, whereas ``preserve_most`` function calls are intended to be\0Aon the hot path and definitely executed a lot. Furthermore ``preserve_most``\0Adoesn't prevent the inliner from inlining the function call.\0A\0AThis calling convention will be used by a future version of the Objective-C\0Aruntime and should therefore still be considered experimental at this time.\0AAlthough this convention was created to optimize certain runtime calls to\0Athe Objective-C runtime, it is not limited to this runtime and might be used\0Aby other runtimes in the future too. The current implementation only\0Asupports X86-64 and AArch64, but the intention is to support more architectures\0Ain the future.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_PreserveNone = internal constant [1015 x i8] c"On X86-64 and AArch64 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of a function.\0AThe ``preserve_none`` calling convention tries to preserve as few general\0Aregisters as possible. So all general registers are caller saved registers. It\0Aalso uses more general registers to pass arguments. This attribute doesn't\0Aimpact floating-point registers. ``preserve_none``'s ABI is still unstable, and\0Amay be changed in the future.\0A\0A- On X86-64, only RSP and RBP are preserved by the callee.\0A  Registers R12, R13, R14, R15, RDI, RSI, RDX, RCX, R8, R9, R11, and RAX now can\0A  be used to pass function arguments. Floating-point registers (XMMs/YMMs) still\0A  follow the C calling convention.\0A- On AArch64, only LR and FP are preserved by the callee.\0A  Registers X20-X28, X0-X7, and X9-X14 are used to pass function arguments.\0A  X8, X16-X19, SIMD and floating-point registers follow the AAPCS calling\0A  convention. X15 is not available for argument passing on Windows, but is\0A  used to pass arguments on other platforms.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_RISCVVectorCC = internal constant [257 x i8] c"The ``riscv_vector_cc`` attribute can be applied to a function. It preserves 15\0Aregisters namely, v1-v7 and v24-v31 as callee-saved. Callers thus don't need\0Ato save these registers before function calls, and callees only need to save\0Athem if they use them.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_RegCall = internal constant [393 x i8] c"On x86 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention to\0A`__regcall`_ convention. This convention aims to pass as many arguments\0Aas possible in registers. It also tries to utilize registers for the\0Areturn value whenever it is possible.\0A\0A.. _`__regcall`: https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/docs/dpcpp-cpp-compiler/developer-guide-reference/2023-2/c-c-sycl-calling-conventions.html\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_SizedBy = internal constant [2279 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``counted_by`` attribute on the flexible array member of a\0Astructure in C. The argument for the attribute is the name of a field member\0Aholding the count of elements in the flexible array. This information can be\0Aused to improve the results of the array bound sanitizer and the\0A``__builtin_dynamic_object_size`` builtin. The ``count`` field member must be\0Awithin the same non-anonymous, enclosing struct as the flexible array member.\0A\0AThis example specifies that the flexible array member ``array`` has the number\0Aof elements allocated for it in ``count``:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  struct bar;\0A\0A  struct foo {\0A    size_t count;\0A    char other;\0A    struct bar *array[] __attribute__((counted_by(count)));\0A  };\0A\0AThis establishes a relationship between ``array`` and ``count``. Specifically,\0A``array`` must have at least ``count`` number of elements available. It's the\0Auser's responsibility to ensure that this relationship is maintained through\0Achanges to the structure.\0A\0AIn the following example, the allocated array erroneously has fewer elements\0Athan what's specified by ``p->count``. This would result in an out-of-bounds\0Aaccess not being detected.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #define SIZE_INCR 42\0A\0A  struct foo *p;\0A\0A  void foo_alloc(size_t count) {\0A    p = malloc(MAX(sizeof(struct foo),\0A                   offsetof(struct foo, array[0]) + count * sizeof(struct bar *)));\0A    p->count = count + SIZE_INCR;\0A  }\0A\0AThe next example updates ``p->count``, but breaks the relationship requirement\0Athat ``p->array`` must have at least ``p->count`` number of elements available:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #define SIZE_INCR 42\0A\0A  struct foo *p;\0A\0A  void foo_alloc(size_t count) {\0A    p = malloc(MAX(sizeof(struct foo),\0A                   offsetof(struct foo, array[0]) + count * sizeof(struct bar *)));\0A    p->count = count;\0A  }\0A\0A  void use_foo(int index, int val) {\0A    p->count += SIZE_INCR + 1; /* 'count' is now larger than the number of elements of 'array'. */\0A    p->array[index] = val;     /* The sanitizer can't properly check this access. */\0A  }\0A\0AIn this example, an update to ``p->count`` maintains the relationship\0Arequirement:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void use_foo(int index, int val) {\0A    if (p->count == 0)\0A      return;\0A    --p->count;\0A    p->array[index] = val;\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_SizedByOrNull = internal constant [2279 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``counted_by`` attribute on the flexible array member of a\0Astructure in C. The argument for the attribute is the name of a field member\0Aholding the count of elements in the flexible array. This information can be\0Aused to improve the results of the array bound sanitizer and the\0A``__builtin_dynamic_object_size`` builtin. The ``count`` field member must be\0Awithin the same non-anonymous, enclosing struct as the flexible array member.\0A\0AThis example specifies that the flexible array member ``array`` has the number\0Aof elements allocated for it in ``count``:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  struct bar;\0A\0A  struct foo {\0A    size_t count;\0A    char other;\0A    struct bar *array[] __attribute__((counted_by(count)));\0A  };\0A\0AThis establishes a relationship between ``array`` and ``count``. Specifically,\0A``array`` must have at least ``count`` number of elements available. It's the\0Auser's responsibility to ensure that this relationship is maintained through\0Achanges to the structure.\0A\0AIn the following example, the allocated array erroneously has fewer elements\0Athan what's specified by ``p->count``. This would result in an out-of-bounds\0Aaccess not being detected.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #define SIZE_INCR 42\0A\0A  struct foo *p;\0A\0A  void foo_alloc(size_t count) {\0A    p = malloc(MAX(sizeof(struct foo),\0A                   offsetof(struct foo, array[0]) + count * sizeof(struct bar *)));\0A    p->count = count + SIZE_INCR;\0A  }\0A\0AThe next example updates ``p->count``, but breaks the relationship requirement\0Athat ``p->array`` must have at least ``p->count`` number of elements available:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #define SIZE_INCR 42\0A\0A  struct foo *p;\0A\0A  void foo_alloc(size_t count) {\0A    p = malloc(MAX(sizeof(struct foo),\0A                   offsetof(struct foo, array[0]) + count * sizeof(struct bar *)));\0A    p->count = count;\0A  }\0A\0A  void use_foo(int index, int val) {\0A    p->count += SIZE_INCR + 1; /* 'count' is now larger than the number of elements of 'array'. */\0A    p->array[index] = val;     /* The sanitizer can't properly check this access. */\0A  }\0A\0AIn this example, an update to ``p->count`` maintains the relationship\0Arequirement:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void use_foo(int index, int val) {\0A    if (p->count == 0)\0A      return;\0A    --p->count;\0A    p->array[index] = val;\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_StdCall = internal constant [448 x i8] c"On 32-bit x86 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of a\0Afunction to clear parameters off of the stack on return. This convention does\0Anot support variadic calls or unprototyped functions in C, and has no effect on\0Ax86_64 targets. This calling convention is used widely by the Windows API and\0ACOM applications. See the documentation for `__stdcall`_ on MSDN.\0A\0A.. _`__stdcall`: http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/zxk0tw93.aspx\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_SwiftAsyncCall = internal constant [1268 x i8] c"The ``swiftasynccall`` attribute indicates that a function is\0Acompatible with the low-level conventions of Swift async functions,\0Aprovided it declares the right formal arguments.\0A\0AIn most respects, this is similar to the ``swiftcall`` attribute, except for\0Athe following:\0A\0A- A parameter may be marked ``swift_async_context``, ``swift_context``\0A  or ``swift_indirect_result`` (with the same restrictions on parameter\0A  ordering as ``swiftcall``) but the parameter attribute\0A  ``swift_error_result`` is not permitted.\0A\0A- A ``swiftasynccall`` function must have return type ``void``.\0A\0A- Within a ``swiftasynccall`` function, a call to a ``swiftasynccall``\0A  function that is the immediate operand of a ``return`` statement is\0A  guaranteed to be performed as a tail call. This syntax is allowed even\0A  in C as an extension (a call to a void-returning function cannot be a\0A  return operand in standard C). If something in the calling function would\0A  semantically be performed after a guaranteed tail call, such as the\0A  non-trivial destruction of a local variable or temporary,\0A  then the program is ill-formed.\0A\0AQuery for this attribute with ``__has_attribute(swiftasynccall)``. Query if\0Athe target supports the calling convention with\0A``__has_extension(swiftasynccc)``.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_SwiftAttr = internal constant [415 x i8] c"The ``swift_attr`` provides a Swift-specific annotation for the declaration\0Aor type to which the attribute appertains to. It can be used on any declaration\0Aor type in Clang. This kind of annotation is ignored by Clang as it doesn't have any\0Asemantic meaning in languages supported by Clang. The Swift compiler can\0Ainterpret these annotations according to its own rules when importing C or\0AObjective-C declarations.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_SwiftCall = internal constant [2815 x i8] c"The ``swiftcall`` attribute indicates that a function should be called\0Ausing the Swift calling convention for a function or function pointer.\0A\0AThe lowering for the Swift calling convention, as described by the Swift\0AABI documentation, occurs in multiple phases. The first, \22high-level\22\0Aphase breaks down the formal parameters and results into innately direct\0Aand indirect components, adds implicit parameters for the generic\0Asignature, and assigns the context and error ABI treatments to parameters\0Awhere applicable. The second phase breaks down the direct parameters\0Aand results from the first phase and assigns them to registers or the\0Astack. The ``swiftcall`` convention only handles this second phase of\0Alowering; the C function type must accurately reflect the results\0Aof the first phase, as follows:\0A\0A- Results classified as indirect by high-level lowering should be\0A  represented as parameters with the ``swift_indirect_result`` attribute.\0A\0A- Results classified as direct by high-level lowering should be represented\0A  as follows:\0A\0A  - First, remove any empty direct results.\0A\0A  - If there are no direct results, the C result type should be ``void``.\0A\0A  - If there is one direct result, the C result type should be a type with\0A    the exact layout of that result type.\0A\0A  - If there are a multiple direct results, the C result type should be\0A    a struct type with the exact layout of a tuple of those results.\0A\0A- Parameters classified as indirect by high-level lowering should be\0A  represented as parameters of pointer type.\0A\0A- Parameters classified as direct by high-level lowering should be\0A  omitted if they are empty types; otherwise, they should be represented\0A  as a parameter type with a layout exactly matching the layout of the\0A  Swift parameter type.\0A\0A- The context parameter, if present, should be represented as a trailing\0A  parameter with the ``swift_context`` attribute.\0A\0A- The error result parameter, if present, should be represented as a\0A  trailing parameter (always following a context parameter) with the\0A  ``swift_error_result`` attribute.\0A\0A``swiftcall`` does not support variadic arguments or unprototyped functions.\0A\0AThe parameter ABI treatment attributes are aspects of the function type.\0AA function type which applies an ABI treatment attribute to a\0Aparameter is a different type from an otherwise-identical function type\0Athat does not. A single parameter may not have multiple ABI treatment\0Aattributes.\0A\0ASupport for this feature is target-dependent, although it should be\0Asupported on every target that Swift supports. Query for this attribute\0Awith ``__has_attribute(swiftcall)``. Query if the target supports the\0Acalling convention with ``__has_extension(swiftcc)``. This implies\0Asupport for the ``swift_context``, ``swift_error_result``, and\0A``swift_indirect_result`` attributes.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_SysVABI = internal constant [217 x i8] c"On Windows x86_64 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of a\0Afunction to match the default convention used on Sys V targets such as Linux,\0AMac, and BSD. This attribute has no effect on other targets.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_ThisCall = internal constant [465 x i8] c"On 32-bit x86 targets, this attribute changes the calling convention of a\0Afunction to use ECX for the first parameter (typically the implicit ``this``\0Aparameter of C++ methods) and clear parameters off of the stack on return. This\0Aconvention does not support variadic calls or unprototyped functions in C, and\0Ahas no effect on x86_64 targets. See the documentation for `__thiscall`_ on\0AMSDN.\0A\0A.. _`__thiscall`: http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ek8tkfbw.aspx\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_TypeNullable = internal constant [576 x i8] c"The ``_Nullable`` nullability qualifier indicates that a value of the\0A``_Nullable`` pointer type can be null. For example, given:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    int fetch_or_zero(int * _Nullable ptr);\0A\0Aa caller of ``fetch_or_zero`` can provide null.\0A\0AThe ``_Nullable`` attribute on classes indicates that the given class can\0Arepresent null values, and so the ``_Nullable``, ``_Nonnull`` etc qualifiers\0Amake sense for this type. For example:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    class _Nullable ArenaPointer { ... };\0A\0A    ArenaPointer _Nonnull x = ...;\0A    ArenaPointer _Nullable y = nullptr;\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_VectorCall = internal constant [1114 x i8] c"On 32-bit x86 *and* x86_64 targets, this attribute changes the calling\0Aconvention of a function to pass vector parameters in SSE registers.\0A\0AOn 32-bit x86 targets, this calling convention is similar to ``__fastcall``.\0AThe first two integer parameters are passed in ECX and EDX. Subsequent integer\0Aparameters are passed in memory, and callee clears the stack. On x86_64\0Atargets, the callee does *not* clear the stack, and integer parameters are\0Apassed in RCX, RDX, R8, and R9 as is done for the default Windows x64 calling\0Aconvention.\0A\0AOn both 32-bit x86 and x86_64 targets, vector and floating point arguments are\0Apassed in XMM0-XMM5. Homogeneous vector aggregates of up to four elements are\0Apassed in sequential SSE registers if enough are available. If AVX is enabled,\0A256 bit vectors are passed in YMM0-YMM5. Any vector or aggregate type that\0Acannot be passed in registers for any reason is passed by reference, which\0Aallows the caller to align the parameter memory.\0A\0ASee the documentation for `__vectorcall`_ on MSDN for more details.\0A\0A.. _`__vectorcall`: http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dn375768.aspx\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_Annotate = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_HLSLParamModifier = internal constant [598 x i8] c"HLSL function parameters are passed by value. Parameter declarations support\0Athree qualifiers to denote parameter passing behavior. The three qualifiers are\0A`in`, `out` and `inout`.\0A\0AParameters annotated with `in` or with no annotation are passed by value from\0Athe caller to the callee.\0A\0AParameters annotated with `out` are written to the argument after the callee\0Areturns (Note: arguments values passed into `out` parameters *are not* copied\0Ainto the callee).\0A\0AParameters annotated with `inout` are copied into the callee via a temporary,\0Aand copied back to the argument after the callee returns.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_SwiftAsyncContext = internal constant [317 x i8] c"The ``swift_async_context`` attribute marks a parameter of a ``swiftasynccall``\0Afunction as having the special asynchronous context-parameter ABI treatment.\0A\0AIf the function is not ``swiftasynccall``, this attribute only generates\0Aextended frame information.\0A\0AA context parameter must have pointer or reference type.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_SwiftContext = internal constant [495 x i8] c"The ``swift_context`` attribute marks a parameter of a ``swiftcall``\0Aor ``swiftasynccall`` function as having the special context-parameter\0AABI treatment.\0A\0AThis treatment generally passes the context value in a special register\0Awhich is normally callee-preserved.\0A\0AA ``swift_context`` parameter must either be the last parameter or must be\0Afollowed by a ``swift_error_result`` parameter (which itself must always be\0Athe last parameter).\0A\0AA context parameter must have pointer or reference type.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_SwiftErrorResult = internal constant [1427 x i8] c"The ``swift_error_result`` attribute marks a parameter of a ``swiftcall``\0Afunction as having the special error-result ABI treatment.\0A\0AThis treatment generally passes the underlying error value in and out of\0Athe function through a special register which is normally callee-preserved.\0AThis is modeled in C by pretending that the register is addressable memory:\0A\0A- The caller appears to pass the address of a variable of pointer type.\0A  The current value of this variable is copied into the register before\0A  the call; if the call returns normally, the value is copied back into the\0A  variable.\0A\0A- The callee appears to receive the address of a variable. This address\0A  is actually a hidden location in its own stack, initialized with the\0A  value of the register upon entry. When the function returns normally,\0A  the value in that hidden location is written back to the register.\0A\0AA ``swift_error_result`` parameter must be the last parameter, and it must be\0Apreceded by a ``swift_context`` parameter.\0A\0AA ``swift_error_result`` parameter must have type ``T**`` or ``T*&`` for some\0Atype T. Note that no qualifiers are permitted on the intermediate level.\0A\0AIt is undefined behavior if the caller does not pass a pointer or\0Areference to a valid object.\0A\0AThe standard convention is that the error value itself (that is, the\0Avalue stored in the apparent argument) will be null upon function entry,\0Abut this is not enforced by the ABI.\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_SwiftIndirectResult = internal constant [1189 x i8] c"The ``swift_indirect_result`` attribute marks a parameter of a ``swiftcall``\0Aor ``swiftasynccall`` function as having the special indirect-result ABI\0Atreatment.\0A\0AThis treatment gives the parameter the target's normal indirect-result\0AABI treatment, which may involve passing it differently from an ordinary\0Aparameter. However, only the first indirect result will receive this\0Atreatment. Furthermore, low-level lowering may decide that a direct result\0Amust be returned indirectly; if so, this will take priority over the\0A``swift_indirect_result`` parameters.\0A\0AA ``swift_indirect_result`` parameter must either be the first parameter or\0Afollow another ``swift_indirect_result`` parameter.\0A\0AA ``swift_indirect_result`` parameter must have type ``T*`` or ``T&`` for\0Asome object type ``T``. If ``T`` is a complete type at the point of\0Adefinition of a function, it is undefined behavior if the argument\0Avalue does not point to storage of adequate size and alignment for a\0Avalue of type ``T``.\0A\0AMaking indirect results explicit in the signature allows C functions to\0Adirectly construct objects into them without relying on language\0Aoptimizations like C++'s named return value optimization (NRVO).\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_CFConsumed = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_CarriesDependency = internal constant [611 x i8] c"The ``carries_dependency`` attribute specifies dependency propagation into and\0Aout of functions.\0A\0AWhen specified on a function or Objective-C method, the ``carries_dependency``\0Aattribute means that the return value carries a dependency out of the function,\0Aso that the implementation need not constrain ordering upon return from that\0Afunction. Implementations of the function and its caller may choose to preserve\0Adependencies instead of emitting memory ordering instructions such as fences.\0A\0ANote, this attribute does not change the meaning of the program, but may result\0Ain generation of more efficient code.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_NSConsumed = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_NonNull = internal constant [1657 x i8] c"The ``nonnull`` attribute indicates that some function parameters must not be\0Anull, and can be used in several different ways. It's original usage\0A(`from GCC <https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Common-Function-Attributes.html#Common-Function-Attributes>`_)\0Ais as a function (or Objective-C method) attribute that specifies which\0Aparameters of the function are nonnull in a comma-separated list. For example:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    extern void * my_memcpy (void *dest, const void *src, size_t len)\0A                    __attribute__((nonnull (1, 2)));\0A\0AHere, the ``nonnull`` attribute indicates that parameters 1 and 2\0Acannot have a null value. Omitting the parenthesized list of parameter indices\0Ameans that all parameters of pointer type cannot be null:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    extern void * my_memcpy (void *dest, const void *src, size_t len)\0A                    __attribute__((nonnull));\0A\0AClang also allows the ``nonnull`` attribute to be placed directly on a function\0A(or Objective-C method) parameter, eliminating the need to specify the\0Aparameter index ahead of type. For example:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    extern void * my_memcpy (void *dest __attribute__((nonnull)),\0A                             const void *src __attribute__((nonnull)), size_t len);\0A\0ANote that the ``nonnull`` attribute indicates that passing null to a non-null\0Aparameter is undefined behavior, which the optimizer may take advantage of to,\0Ae.g., remove null checks. The ``_Nonnull`` type qualifier indicates that a\0Apointer cannot be null in a more general manner (because it is part of the type\0Asystem) and does not imply undefined behavior, making it more widely applicable.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_OSConsumed = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_PassObjectSize = internal constant [3833 x i8] c".. Note:: The mangling of functions with parameters that are annotated with\0A  ``pass_object_size`` is subject to change. You can get around this by\0A  using ``__asm__(\22foo\22)`` to explicitly name your functions, thus preserving\0A  your ABI; also, non-overloadable C functions with ``pass_object_size`` are\0A  not mangled.\0A\0AThe ``pass_object_size(Type)`` attribute can be placed on function parameters to\0Ainstruct clang to call ``__builtin_object_size(param, Type)`` at each callsite\0Aof said function, and implicitly pass the result of this call in as an invisible\0Aargument of type ``size_t`` directly after the parameter annotated with\0A``pass_object_size``. Clang will also replace any calls to\0A``__builtin_object_size(param, Type)`` in the function by said implicit\0Aparameter.\0A\0AExample usage:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int bzero1(char *const p __attribute__((pass_object_size(0))))\0A      __attribute__((noinline)) {\0A    int i = 0;\0A    for (/**/; i < (int)__builtin_object_size(p, 0); ++i) {\0A      p[i] = 0;\0A    }\0A    return i;\0A  }\0A\0A  int main() {\0A    char chars[100];\0A    int n = bzero1(&chars[0]);\0A    assert(n == sizeof(chars));\0A    return 0;\0A  }\0A\0AIf successfully evaluating ``__builtin_object_size(param, Type)`` at the\0Acallsite is not possible, then the \22failed\22 value is passed in. So, using the\0Adefinition of ``bzero1`` from above, the following code would exit cleanly:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int main2(int argc, char *argv[]) {\0A    int n = bzero1(argv);\0A    assert(n == -1);\0A    return 0;\0A  }\0A\0A``pass_object_size`` plays a part in overload resolution. If two overload\0Acandidates are otherwise equally good, then the overload with one or more\0Aparameters with ``pass_object_size`` is preferred. This implies that the choice\0Abetween two identical overloads both with ``pass_object_size`` on one or more\0Aparameters will always be ambiguous; for this reason, having two such overloads\0Ais illegal. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  #define PS(N) __attribute__((pass_object_size(N)))\0A  // OK\0A  void Foo(char *a, char *b); // Overload A\0A  // OK -- overload A has no parameters with pass_object_size.\0A  void Foo(char *a PS(0), char *b PS(0)); // Overload B\0A  // Error -- Same signature (sans pass_object_size) as overload B, and both\0A  // overloads have one or more parameters with the pass_object_size attribute.\0A  void Foo(void *a PS(0), void *b);\0A\0A  // OK\0A  void Bar(void *a PS(0)); // Overload C\0A  // OK\0A  void Bar(char *c PS(1)); // Overload D\0A\0A  void main() {\0A    char known[10], *unknown;\0A    Foo(unknown, unknown); // Calls overload B\0A    Foo(known, unknown); // Calls overload B\0A    Foo(unknown, known); // Calls overload B\0A    Foo(known, known); // Calls overload B\0A\0A    Bar(known); // Calls overload D\0A    Bar(unknown); // Calls overload D\0A  }\0A\0ACurrently, ``pass_object_size`` is a bit restricted in terms of its usage:\0A\0A* Only one use of ``pass_object_size`` is allowed per parameter.\0A\0A* It is an error to take the address of a function with ``pass_object_size`` on\0A  any of its parameters. If you wish to do this, you can create an overload\0A  without ``pass_object_size`` on any parameters.\0A\0A* It is an error to apply the ``pass_object_size`` attribute to parameters that\0A  are not pointers. Additionally, any parameter that ``pass_object_size`` is\0A  applied to must be marked ``const`` at its function's definition.\0A\0AClang also supports the ``pass_dynamic_object_size`` attribute, which behaves\0Aidentically to ``pass_object_size``, but evaluates a call to\0A``__builtin_dynamic_object_size`` at the callee instead of\0A``__builtin_object_size``. ``__builtin_dynamic_object_size`` provides some extra\0Aruntime checks when the object size can't be determined at compile-time. You can\0Aread more about ``__builtin_dynamic_object_size`` `here\0A<https://clang.llvm.org/docs/LanguageExtensions.html#evaluating-object-size-dynamically>`_.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_ReleaseHandle = internal constant [354 x i8] c"If a function parameter is annotated with ``release_handle(tag)`` it is assumed to\0Aclose the handle. It is also assumed to require an open handle to work with. The\0Aattribute requires a string literal argument to identify the handle being released.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  zx_status_t zx_handle_close(zx_handle_t handle [[clang::release_handle(\22tag\22)]]);\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_UseHandle = internal constant [523 x i8] c"A function taking a handle by value might close the handle. If a function\0Aparameter is annotated with ``use_handle(tag)`` it is assumed to not to change\0Athe state of the handle. It is also assumed to require an open handle to work with.\0AThe attribute requires a string literal argument to identify the handle being used.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  zx_status_t zx_port_wait(zx_handle_t handle [[clang::use_handle(\22zircon\22)]],\0A                           zx_time_t deadline,\0A                           zx_port_packet_t* packet);\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_HLSLPackOffset = internal constant [500 x i8] c"The packoffset attribute is used to change the layout of a cbuffer.\0AAttribute spelling in HLSL is: ``packoffset( c[Subcomponent][.component] )``.\0AA subcomponent is a register number, which is an integer. A component is in the form of [.xyzw].\0A\0AExamples:\0A\0A.. code-block:: hlsl\0A\0A  cbuffer A {\0A    float3 a : packoffset(c0.y);\0A    float4 b : packoffset(c4);\0A  }\0A\0AThe full documentation is available here: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3dhlsl/dx-graphics-hlsl-variable-packoffset\00", align 16
@_ZL31AttrDoc_HLSLSV_DispatchThreadID = internal constant [581 x i8] c"The ``SV_DispatchThreadID`` semantic, when applied to an input parameter,\0Aspecifies a data binding to map the global thread offset within the Dispatch\0Acall (per dimension of the group) to the specified parameter.\0AWhen applied to a field of a struct, the data binding is specified to the field\0Awhen the struct is used as a parameter type.\0AThe semantic on the field is ignored when not used as a parameter.\0AThis attribute is only supported in compute shaders.\0A\0AThe full documentation is available here: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3dhlsl/sv-dispatchthreadid\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_HLSLSV_GroupID = internal constant [287 x i8] c"The ``SV_GroupID`` semantic, when applied to an input parameter, specifies which\0Athread group a shader is executing in. This attribute is only supported in compute shaders.\0A\0AThe full documentation is available here: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3dhlsl/sv-groupid\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_HLSLSV_GroupIndex = internal constant [314 x i8] c"The ``SV_GroupIndex`` semantic, when applied to an input parameter, specifies a\0Adata binding to map the group index to the specified parameter. This attribute\0Ais only supported in compute shaders.\0A\0AThe full documentation is available here: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3dhlsl/sv-groupindex\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_HLSLSV_GroupThreadID = internal constant [317 x i8] c"The ``SV_GroupThreadID`` semantic, when applied to an input parameter, specifies which\0Aindividual thread within a thread group is executing in. This attribute is\0Aonly supported in compute shaders.\0A\0AThe full documentation is available here: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3dhlsl/sv-groupthreadid\00", align 16
@_ZL31AttrDoc_AMDGPUFlatWorkGroupSize = internal constant [1050 x i8] c"The flat work-group size is the number of work-items in the work-group size\0Aspecified when the kernel is dispatched. It is the product of the sizes of the\0Ax, y, and z dimension of the work-group.\0A\0AClang supports the\0A``__attribute__((amdgpu_flat_work_group_size(<min>, <max>)))`` attribute for the\0AAMDGPU target. This attribute may be attached to a kernel function definition\0Aand is an optimization hint.\0A\0A``<min>`` parameter specifies the minimum flat work-group size, and ``<max>``\0Aparameter specifies the maximum flat work-group size (must be greater than\0A``<min>``) to which all dispatches of the kernel will conform. Passing ``0, 0``\0Aas ``<min>, <max>`` implies the default behavior (``128, 256``).\0A\0AIf specified, the AMDGPU target backend might be able to produce better machine\0Acode for barriers and perform scratch promotion by estimating available group\0Asegment size.\0A\0AAn error will be given if:\0A  - Specified values violate subtarget specifications;\0A  - Specified values are not compatible with values provided through other\0A    attributes.\00", align 16
@_ZL30AttrDoc_AMDGPUMaxNumWorkGroups = internal constant [952 x i8] c"This attribute specifies the max number of work groups when the kernel\0Ais dispatched.\0A\0AClang supports the\0A``__attribute__((amdgpu_max_num_work_groups(<x>, <y>, <z>)))`` or\0A``[[clang::amdgpu_max_num_work_groups(<x>, <y>, <z>)]]`` attribute for the\0AAMDGPU target. This attribute may be attached to HIP or OpenCL kernel function\0Adefinitions and is an optimization hint.\0A\0AThe ``<x>`` parameter specifies the maximum number of work groups in the x dimension.\0ASimilarly ``<y>`` and ``<z>`` are for the y and z dimensions respectively.\0AEach of the three values must be greater than 0 when provided. The ``<x>`` parameter\0Ais required, while ``<y>`` and ``<z>`` are optional with default value of 1.\0A\0AIf specified, the AMDGPU target backend might be able to produce better machine\0Acode.\0A\0AAn error will be given if:\0A  - Specified values violate subtarget specifications;\0A  - Specified values are not compatible with values provided through other\0A    attributes.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_AMDGPUNumSGPR = internal constant [1137 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((amdgpu_num_sgpr(<num_sgpr>)))`` and\0A``__attribute__((amdgpu_num_vgpr(<num_vgpr>)))`` attributes for the AMDGPU\0Atarget. These attributes may be attached to a kernel function definition and are\0Aan optimization hint.\0A\0AIf these attributes are specified, then the AMDGPU target backend will attempt\0Ato limit the number of SGPRs and/or VGPRs used to the specified value(s). The\0Anumber of used SGPRs and/or VGPRs may further be rounded up to satisfy the\0Aallocation requirements or constraints of the subtarget. Passing ``0`` as\0A``num_sgpr`` and/or ``num_vgpr`` implies the default behavior (no limits).\0A\0AThese attributes can be used to test the AMDGPU target backend. It is\0Arecommended that the ``amdgpu_waves_per_eu`` attribute be used to control\0Aresources such as SGPRs and VGPRs since it is aware of the limits for different\0Asubtargets.\0A\0AAn error will be given if:\0A  - Specified values violate subtarget specifications;\0A  - Specified values are not compatible with values provided through other\0A    attributes;\0A  - The AMDGPU target backend is unable to create machine code that can meet the\0A    request.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_AMDGPUNumVGPR = internal constant [1137 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((amdgpu_num_sgpr(<num_sgpr>)))`` and\0A``__attribute__((amdgpu_num_vgpr(<num_vgpr>)))`` attributes for the AMDGPU\0Atarget. These attributes may be attached to a kernel function definition and are\0Aan optimization hint.\0A\0AIf these attributes are specified, then the AMDGPU target backend will attempt\0Ato limit the number of SGPRs and/or VGPRs used to the specified value(s). The\0Anumber of used SGPRs and/or VGPRs may further be rounded up to satisfy the\0Aallocation requirements or constraints of the subtarget. Passing ``0`` as\0A``num_sgpr`` and/or ``num_vgpr`` implies the default behavior (no limits).\0A\0AThese attributes can be used to test the AMDGPU target backend. It is\0Arecommended that the ``amdgpu_waves_per_eu`` attribute be used to control\0Aresources such as SGPRs and VGPRs since it is aware of the limits for different\0Asubtargets.\0A\0AAn error will be given if:\0A  - Specified values violate subtarget specifications;\0A  - Specified values are not compatible with values provided through other\0A    attributes;\0A  - The AMDGPU target backend is unable to create machine code that can meet the\0A    request.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_AMDGPUWavesPerEU = internal constant [2637 x i8] c"A compute unit (CU) is responsible for executing the wavefronts of a work-group.\0AIt is composed of one or more execution units (EU), which are responsible for\0Aexecuting the wavefronts. An EU can have enough resources to maintain the state\0Aof more than one executing wavefront. This allows an EU to hide latency by\0Aswitching between wavefronts in a similar way to symmetric multithreading on a\0ACPU. In order to allow the state for multiple wavefronts to fit on an EU, the\0Aresources used by a single wavefront have to be limited. For example, the number\0Aof SGPRs and VGPRs. Limiting such resources can allow greater latency hiding,\0Abut can result in having to spill some register state to memory.\0A\0AClang supports the ``__attribute__((amdgpu_waves_per_eu(<min>[, <max>])))``\0Aattribute for the AMDGPU target. This attribute may be attached to a kernel\0Afunction definition and is an optimization hint.\0A\0A``<min>`` parameter specifies the requested minimum number of waves per EU, and\0A*optional* ``<max>`` parameter specifies the requested maximum number of waves\0Aper EU (must be greater than ``<min>`` if specified). If ``<max>`` is omitted,\0Athen there is no restriction on the maximum number of waves per EU other than\0Athe one dictated by the hardware for which the kernel is compiled. Passing\0A``0, 0`` as ``<min>, <max>`` implies the default behavior (no limits).\0A\0AIf specified, this attribute allows an advanced developer to tune the number of\0Awavefronts that are capable of fitting within the resources of an EU. The AMDGPU\0Atarget backend can use this information to limit resources, such as number of\0ASGPRs, number of VGPRs, size of available group and private memory segments, in\0Asuch a way that guarantees that at least ``<min>`` wavefronts and at most\0A``<max>`` wavefronts are able to fit within the resources of an EU. Requesting\0Amore wavefronts can hide memory latency but limits available registers which\0Acan result in spilling. Requesting fewer wavefronts can help reduce cache\0Athrashing, but can reduce memory latency hiding.\0A\0AThis attribute controls the machine code generated by the AMDGPU target backend\0Ato ensure it is capable of meeting the requested values. However, when the\0Akernel is executed, there may be other reasons that prevent meeting the request,\0Afor example, there may be wavefronts from other kernels executing on the EU.\0A\0AAn error will be given if:\0A  - Specified values violate subtarget specifications;\0A  - Specified values are not compatible with values provided through other\0A    attributes;\0A\0AThe AMDGPU target backend will emit a warning whenever it is unable to\0Acreate machine code that meets the request.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_ARMInterrupt = internal constant [2042 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((interrupt(\22TYPE\22)))`` attribute on\0AARM targets. This attribute may be attached to a function definition and\0Ainstructs the backend to generate appropriate function entry/exit code so that\0Ait can be used directly as an interrupt service routine.\0A\0AThe parameter passed to the interrupt attribute is optional, but if\0Aprovided it must be a string literal with one of the following values: \22IRQ\22,\0A\22FIQ\22, \22SWI\22, \22ABORT\22, \22UNDEF\22.\0A\0AThe semantics are as follows:\0A\0A- If the function is AAPCS, Clang instructs the backend to realign the stack to\0A  8 bytes on entry. This is a general requirement of the AAPCS at public\0A  interfaces, but may not hold when an exception is taken. Doing this allows\0A  other AAPCS functions to be called.\0A- If the CPU is M-class this is all that needs to be done since the architecture\0A  itself is designed in such a way that functions obeying the normal AAPCS ABI\0A  constraints are valid exception handlers.\0A- If the CPU is not M-class, the prologue and epilogue are modified to save all\0A  non-banked registers that are used, so that upon return the user-mode state\0A  will not be corrupted. Note that to avoid unnecessary overhead, only\0A  general-purpose (integer) registers are saved in this way. If VFP operations\0A  are needed, that state must be saved manually.\0A\0A  Specifically, interrupt kinds other than \22FIQ\22 will save all core registers\0A  except \22lr\22 and \22sp\22. \22FIQ\22 interrupts will save r0-r7.\0A- If the CPU is not M-class, the return instruction is changed to one of the\0A  canonical sequences permitted by the architecture for exception return. Where\0A  possible the function itself will make the necessary \22lr\22 adjustments so that\0A  the \22preferred return address\22 is selected.\0A\0A  Unfortunately the compiler is unable to make this guarantee for an \22UNDEF\22\0A  handler, where the offset from \22lr\22 to the preferred return address depends on\0A  the execution state of the code which generated the exception. In this case\0A  a sequence equivalent to \22movs pc, lr\22 will be used.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_AVRInterrupt = internal constant [559 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((interrupt))`` attribute on\0AAVR targets. This attribute may be attached to a function definition and instructs\0Athe backend to generate appropriate function entry/exit code so that it can be used\0Adirectly as an interrupt service routine.\0A\0AOn the AVR, the hardware globally disables interrupts when an interrupt is executed.\0AThe first instruction of an interrupt handler declared with this attribute is a SEI\0Ainstruction to re-enable interrupts. See also the signal attribute that\0Adoes not insert a SEI instruction.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_AVRSignal = internal constant [371 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((signal))`` attribute on\0AAVR targets. This attribute may be attached to a function definition and instructs\0Athe backend to generate appropriate function entry/exit code so that it can be used\0Adirectly as an interrupt service routine.\0A\0AInterrupt handler functions defined with the signal attribute do not re-enable interrupts.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_AcquireCapability = internal constant [44 x i8] c"Marks a function as acquiring a capability.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_AcquiredAfter = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_AcquiredBefore = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_AlignMac68k = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL20AttrDoc_AlignNatural = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL15AttrDoc_Aligned = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_AllocAlign = internal constant [1269 x i8] c"Use ``__attribute__((alloc_align(<alignment>))`` on a function\0Adeclaration to specify that the return value of the function (which must be a\0Apointer type) is at least as aligned as the value of the indicated parameter. The\0Aparameter is given by its index in the list of formal parameters; the first\0Aparameter has index 1 unless the function is a C++ non-static member function,\0Ain which case the first parameter has index 2 to account for the implicit ``this``\0Aparameter.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  // The returned pointer has the alignment specified by the first parameter.\0A  void *a(size_t align) __attribute__((alloc_align(1)));\0A\0A  // The returned pointer has the alignment specified by the second parameter.\0A  void *b(void *v, size_t align) __attribute__((alloc_align(2)));\0A\0A  // The returned pointer has the alignment specified by the second visible\0A  // parameter, however it must be adjusted for the implicit 'this' parameter.\0A  void *Foo::b(void *v, size_t align) __attribute__((alloc_align(3)));\0A\0ANote that this attribute merely informs the compiler that a function always\0Areturns a sufficiently aligned pointer. It does not cause the compiler to\0Aemit code to enforce that alignment. The behavior is undefined if the returned\0Apointer is not sufficiently aligned.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_AllocSize = internal constant [1327 x i8] c"The ``alloc_size`` attribute can be placed on functions that return pointers in\0Aorder to hint to the compiler how many bytes of memory will be available at the\0Areturned pointer. ``alloc_size`` takes one or two arguments.\0A\0A- ``alloc_size(N)`` implies that argument number N equals the number of\0A  available bytes at the returned pointer.\0A- ``alloc_size(N, M)`` implies that the product of argument number N and\0A  argument number M equals the number of available bytes at the returned\0A  pointer.\0A\0AArgument numbers are 1-based.\0A\0AAn example of how to use ``alloc_size``\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void *my_malloc(int a) __attribute__((alloc_size(1)));\0A  void *my_calloc(int a, int b) __attribute__((alloc_size(1, 2)));\0A\0A  int main() {\0A    void *const p = my_malloc(100);\0A    assert(__builtin_object_size(p, 0) == 100);\0A    void *const a = my_calloc(20, 5);\0A    assert(__builtin_object_size(a, 0) == 100);\0A  }\0A\0A.. Note:: This attribute works differently in clang than it does in GCC.\0A  Specifically, clang will only trace ``const`` pointers (as above); we give up\0A  on pointers that are not marked as ``const``. In the vast majority of cases,\0A  this is unimportant, because LLVM has support for the ``alloc_size``\0A  attribute. However, this may cause mildly unintuitive behavior when used with\0A  other attributes, such as ``enable_if``.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_AlwaysDestroy = internal constant [366 x i8] c"The ``always_destroy`` attribute specifies that a variable with static or thread\0Astorage duration should have its exit-time destructor run. This attribute is the\0Adefault unless clang was invoked with -fno-c++-static-destructors.\0A\0AIf a variable is explicitly declared with this attribute, Clang will silence\0Aotherwise applicable ``-Wexit-time-destructors`` warnings.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_AnalyzerNoReturn = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_AnyX86Interrupt = internal constant [1578 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((interrupt))`` attribute on X86\0Atargets. This attribute may be attached to a function definition and instructs\0Athe backend to generate appropriate function entry/exit code so that it can be\0Aused directly as an interrupt service routine.\0A\0AInterrupt handlers have access to the stack frame pushed onto the stack by the processor,\0Aand return using the ``IRET`` instruction. All registers in an interrupt handler are callee-saved.\0AException handlers also have access to the error code pushed onto the stack by the processor,\0Awhen applicable.\0A\0AAn interrupt handler must take the following arguments:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A   __attribute__ ((interrupt))\0A   void f (struct stack_frame *frame) {\0A       ...\0A   }\0A\0A  Where ``struct stack_frame`` is a suitable struct matching the stack frame pushed by the\0A  processor.\0A\0AAn exception handler must take the following arguments:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A   __attribute__ ((interrupt))\0A   void g (struct stack_frame *frame, unsigned long code) {\0A       ...\0A   }\0A\0A  On 32-bit targets, the ``code`` argument should be of type ``unsigned int``.\0A\0AException handlers should only be used when an error code is pushed by the processor.\0AUsing the incorrect handler type will crash the system.\0A\0AInterrupt and exception handlers cannot be called by other functions and must have return type ``void``.\0A\0AInterrupt and exception handlers should only call functions with the 'no_caller_saved_registers'\0Aattribute, or should be compiled with the '-mgeneral-regs-only' flag to avoid saving unused\0Anon-GPR registers.\00", align 16
@_ZL36AttrDoc_AnyX86NoCallerSavedRegisters = internal constant [1469 x i8] c"Use this attribute to indicate that the specified function has no\0Acaller-saved registers. That is, all registers are callee-saved except for\0Aregisters used for passing parameters to the function or returning parameters\0Afrom the function.\0AThe compiler saves and restores any modified registers that were not used for\0Apassing or returning arguments to the function.\0A\0AThe user can call functions specified with the 'no_caller_saved_registers'\0Aattribute from an interrupt handler without saving and restoring all\0Acall-clobbered registers.\0A\0AFunctions specified with the 'no_caller_saved_registers' attribute should only\0Acall other functions with the 'no_caller_saved_registers' attribute, or should be\0Acompiled with the '-mgeneral-regs-only' flag to avoid saving unused non-GPR registers.\0A\0ANote that 'no_caller_saved_registers' attribute is not a calling convention.\0AIn fact, it only overrides the decision of which registers should be saved by\0Athe caller, but not how the parameters are passed from the caller to the callee.\0A\0AFor example:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    __attribute__ ((no_caller_saved_registers, fastcall))\0A    void f (int arg1, int arg2) {\0A      ...\0A    }\0A\0A  In this case parameters 'arg1' and 'arg2' will be passed in registers.\0A  In this case, on 32-bit x86 targets, the function 'f' will use ECX and EDX as\0A  register parameters. However, it will not assume any scratch registers and\0A  should save and restore any modified registers except for ECX and EDX.\00", align 16
@_ZL29AttrDoc_ArcWeakrefUnavailable = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_ArgumentWithTypeTag = internal constant [1462 x i8] c"Use ``__attribute__((argument_with_type_tag(arg_kind, arg_idx,\0Atype_tag_idx)))`` on a function declaration to specify that the function\0Aaccepts a type tag that determines the type of some other argument.\0A\0AThis attribute is primarily useful for checking arguments of variadic functions\0A(``pointer_with_type_tag`` can be used in most non-variadic cases).\0A\0AIn the attribute prototype above:\0A  * ``arg_kind`` is an identifier that should be used when annotating all\0A    applicable type tags.\0A  * ``arg_idx`` provides the position of a function argument. The expected type of\0A    this function argument will be determined by the function argument specified\0A    by ``type_tag_idx``. In the code example below, \223\22 means that the type of the\0A    function's third argument will be determined by ``type_tag_idx``.\0A  * ``type_tag_idx`` provides the position of a function argument. This function\0A    argument will be a type tag. The type tag will determine the expected type of\0A    the argument specified by ``arg_idx``. In the code example below, \222\22 means\0A    that the type tag associated with the function's second argument should agree\0A    with the type of the argument specified by ``arg_idx``.\0A\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  int fcntl(int fd, int cmd, ...)\0A      __attribute__(( argument_with_type_tag(fcntl,3,2) ));\0A  // The function's second argument will be a type tag; this type tag will\0A  // determine the expected type of the function's third argument.\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_ArmBuiltinAlias = internal constant [1064 x i8] c"This attribute is used in the implementation of the ACLE intrinsics.\0AIt allows the intrinsic functions to\0Abe declared using the names defined in ACLE, and still be recognized\0Aas clang builtins equivalent to the underlying name. For example,\0A``arm_mve.h`` declares the function ``vaddq_u32`` with\0A``__attribute__((__clang_arm_mve_alias(__builtin_arm_mve_vaddq_u32)))``,\0Aand similarly, one of the type-overloaded declarations of ``vaddq``\0Awill have the same attribute. This ensures that both functions are\0Arecognized as that clang builtin, and in the latter case, the choice\0Aof which builtin to identify the function as can be deferred until\0Aafter overload resolution.\0A\0AThis attribute can only be used to set up the aliases for certain Arm\0Aintrinsic functions; it is intended for use only inside ``arm_*.h``\0Aand is not a general mechanism for declaring arbitrary aliases for\0Aclang builtin functions.\0A\0AIn order to avoid duplicating the attribute definitions for similar\0Apurpose for other architecture, there is a general form for the\0Aattribute `clang_builtin_alias`.\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_ArmLocallyStreaming = internal constant [676 x i8] c"The ``__arm_locally_streaming`` keyword applies to function declarations\0Aand specifies that all the statements in the function are executed in\0Astreaming mode. This means that:\0A\0A* the function requires that the target processor implements the Scalable Matrix\0A  Extension (SME).\0A\0A* the program automatically puts the machine into streaming mode before\0A  executing the statements and automatically restores the previous mode\0A  afterwards.\0A\0AClang manages PSTATE.SM automatically; it is not the source code's\0Aresponsibility to do this.  For example, Clang will emit code to enable\0Astreaming mode at the start of the function, and disable streaming mode\0Aat the end of the function.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_ArmNew = internal constant [931 x i8] c"The ``__arm_new`` keyword applies to function declarations and specifies\0Athat the function will create a new scope for state S.\0A\0AThe attribute takes string arguments to instruct the compiler for which state\0Ato create new scope.  The supported states for S are:\0A\0A* ``\22za\22`` for Matrix Storage (requires SME)\0A\0AFor state ``\22za\22``, this means that:\0A\0A* the function requires that the target processor implements the Scalable Matrix\0A  Extension (SME).\0A\0A* the function will commit any lazily saved ZA data.\0A\0A* the function will create a new ZA context and enable PSTATE.ZA.\0A\0A* the function will disable PSTATE.ZA (by setting it to 0) before returning.\0A\0AFor ``__arm_new(\22za\22)`` functions Clang will set up the ZA context automatically\0Aon entry to the function and disable it before returning. For example, if ZA is\0Ain a dormant state Clang will generate the code to commit a lazy-save and set up\0Aa new ZA state before executing user code.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_Artificial = internal constant [268 x i8] c"The ``artificial`` attribute can be applied to an inline function. If such a\0Afunction is inlined, the attribute indicates that debuggers should associate\0Athe resulting instructions with the call site, rather than with the\0Acorresponding line within the inlined callee.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_AsmLabel = internal constant [1442 x i8] c"This attribute can be used on a function or variable to specify its symbol name.\0A\0AOn some targets, all C symbols are prefixed by default with a single character,\0Atypically ``_``. This was done historically to distinguish them from symbols\0Aused by other languages. (This prefix is also added to the standard Itanium\0AC++ ABI prefix on \22mangled\22 symbol names, so that e.g. on such targets the true\0Asymbol name for a C++ variable declared as ``int cppvar;`` would be\0A``__Z6cppvar``; note the two underscores.)  This prefix is *not* added to the\0Asymbol names specified by the ``asm`` attribute; programmers wishing to match a\0AC symbol name must compensate for this.\0A\0AFor example, consider the following C code:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int var1 asm(\22altvar\22) = 1;  // \22altvar\22 in symbol table.\0A  int var2 = 1; // \22_var2\22 in symbol table.\0A\0A  void func1(void) asm(\22altfunc\22);\0A  void func1(void) {} // \22altfunc\22 in symbol table.\0A  void func2(void) {} // \22_func2\22 in symbol table.\0A\0AClang's implementation of this attribute is compatible with GCC's, `documented here <https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Asm-Labels.html>`_.\0A\0AWhile it is possible to use this attribute to name a special symbol used\0Ainternally by the compiler, such as an LLVM intrinsic, this is neither\0Arecommended nor supported and may cause the compiler to crash or miscompile.\0AUsers who wish to gain access to intrinsic behavior are strongly encouraged to\0Arequest new builtin functions.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_AssertCapability = internal constant [111 x i8] c"Marks a function that dynamically tests whether a capability is held, and halts\0Athe program if it is not held.\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_AssertExclusiveLock = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
