@_ZL21AttrDoc_AssumeAligned = internal constant [774 x i8] c"Use ``__attribute__((assume_aligned(<alignment>[,<offset>]))`` on a function\0Adeclaration to specify that the return value of the function (which must be a\0Apointer type) has the specified offset, in bytes, from an address with the\0Aspecified alignment. The offset is taken to be zero if omitted.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  // The returned pointer value has 32-byte alignment.\0A  void *a() __attribute__((assume_aligned (32)));\0A\0A  // The returned pointer value is 4 bytes greater than an address having\0A  // 32-byte alignment.\0A  void *b() __attribute__((assume_aligned (32, 4)));\0A\0ANote that this attribute provides information to the compiler regarding a\0Acondition that the code already ensures is true. It does not cause the compiler\0Ato enforce the provided alignment assumption.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_Availability = internal constant [9737 x i8] c"The ``availability`` attribute can be placed on declarations to describe the\0Alifecycle of that declaration relative to operating system versions. Consider\0Athe function declaration for a hypothetical function ``f``:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void f(void) __attribute__((availability(macos,introduced=10.4,deprecated=10.6,obsoleted=10.7)));\0A\0AThe availability attribute states that ``f`` was introduced in macOS 10.4,\0Adeprecated in macOS 10.6, and obsoleted in macOS 10.7. This information\0Ais used by Clang to determine when it is safe to use ``f``: for example, if\0AClang is instructed to compile code for macOS 10.5, a call to ``f()``\0Asucceeds. If Clang is instructed to compile code for macOS 10.6, the call\0Asucceeds but Clang emits a warning specifying that the function is deprecated.\0AFinally, if Clang is instructed to compile code for macOS 10.7, the call\0Afails because ``f()`` is no longer available.\0A\0AClang is instructed to compile code for a minimum deployment version using\0Athe ``-target`` or ``-mtargetos`` command line arguments. For example,\0AmacOS 10.7 would be specified as ``-target x86_64-apple-macos10.7`` or\0A``-mtargetos=macos10.7``. Variants like Mac Catalyst are specified as\0A``-target arm64-apple-ios15.0-macabi`` or ``-mtargetos=ios15.0-macabi``\0A\0AThe availability attribute is a comma-separated list starting with the\0Aplatform name and then including clauses specifying important milestones in the\0Adeclaration's lifetime (in any order) along with additional information. Those\0Aclauses can be:\0A\0Aintroduced=\\ *version*\0A  The first version in which this declaration was introduced.\0A\0Adeprecated=\\ *version*\0A  The first version in which this declaration was deprecated, meaning that\0A  users should migrate away from this API.\0A\0Aobsoleted=\\ *version*\0A  The first version in which this declaration was obsoleted, meaning that it\0A  was removed completely and can no longer be used.\0A\0Aunavailable\0A  This declaration is never available on this platform.\0A\0Amessage=\\ *string-literal*\0A  Additional message text that Clang will provide when emitting a warning or\0A  error about use of a deprecated or obsoleted declaration. Useful to direct\0A  users to replacement APIs.\0A\0Areplacement=\\ *string-literal*\0A  Additional message text that Clang will use to provide Fix-It when emitting\0A  a warning about use of a deprecated declaration. The Fix-It will replace\0A  the deprecated declaration with the new declaration specified.\0A\0Aenvironment=\\ *identifier*\0A  Target environment in which this declaration is available. If present,\0A  the availability attribute applies only to targets with the same platform\0A  and environment. The parameter is currently supported only in HLSL.\0A\0AMultiple availability attributes can be placed on a declaration, which may\0Acorrespond to different platforms. For most platforms, the availability\0Aattribute with the platform corresponding to the target platform will be used;\0Aany others will be ignored. However, the availability for ``watchOS`` and\0A``tvOS`` can be implicitly inferred from an ``iOS`` availability attribute.\0AAny explicit availability attributes for those platforms are still preferred over\0Athe implicitly inferred availability attributes. If no availability attribute\0Aspecifies availability for the current target platform, the availability\0Aattributes are ignored. Supported platforms are:\0A\0A``iOS``\0A``macOS``\0A``tvOS``\0A``watchOS``\0A``iOSApplicationExtension``\0A``macOSApplicationExtension``\0A``tvOSApplicationExtension``\0A``watchOSApplicationExtension``\0A``macCatalyst``\0A``macCatalystApplicationExtension``\0A``visionOS``\0A``visionOSApplicationExtension``\0A``driverkit``\0A``swift``\0A``android``\0A``fuchsia``\0A``ohos``\0A``zos``\0A``ShaderModel``\0A\0ASome platforms have alias names:\0A\0A``ios``\0A``macos``\0A``macosx (deprecated)``\0A``tvos``\0A``watchos``\0A``ios_app_extension``\0A``macos_app_extension``\0A``macosx_app_extension (deprecated)``\0A``tvos_app_extension``\0A``watchos_app_extension``\0A``maccatalyst``\0A``maccatalyst_app_extension``\0A``visionos``\0A``visionos_app_extension``\0A``shadermodel``\0A\0ASupported environment names for the ShaderModel platform:\0A\0A``pixel``\0A``vertex``\0A``geometry``\0A``hull``\0A``domain``\0A``compute``\0A``raygeneration``\0A``intersection``\0A``anyhit``\0A``closesthit``\0A``miss``\0A``callable``\0A``mesh``\0A``amplification``\0A``library``\0A\0AA declaration can typically be used even when deploying back to a platform\0Aversion prior to when the declaration was introduced. When this happens, the\0Adeclaration is `weakly linked\0A<https://developer.apple.com/library/mac/#documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPFrameworks/Concepts/WeakLinking.html>`_,\0Aas if the ``weak_import`` attribute were added to the declaration. A\0Aweakly-linked declaration may or may not be present a run-time, and a program\0Acan determine whether the declaration is present by checking whether the\0Aaddress of that declaration is non-NULL.\0A\0AThe flag ``strict`` disallows using API when deploying back to a\0Aplatform version prior to when the declaration was introduced. An\0Aattempt to use such API before its introduction causes a hard error.\0AWeakly-linking is almost always a better API choice, since it allows\0Ausers to query availability at runtime.\0A\0AIf there are multiple declarations of the same entity, the availability\0Aattributes must either match on a per-platform basis or later\0Adeclarations must not have availability attributes for that\0Aplatform. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void g(void) __attribute__((availability(macos,introduced=10.4)));\0A  void g(void) __attribute__((availability(macos,introduced=10.4))); // okay, matches\0A  void g(void) __attribute__((availability(ios,introduced=4.0))); // okay, adds a new platform\0A  void g(void); // okay, inherits both macos and ios availability from above.\0A  void g(void) __attribute__((availability(macos,introduced=10.5))); // error: mismatch\0A\0AWhen one method overrides another, the overriding method can be more widely available than the overridden method, e.g.,:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  @interface A\0A  - (id)method __attribute__((availability(macos,introduced=10.4)));\0A  - (id)method2 __attribute__((availability(macos,introduced=10.4)));\0A  @end\0A\0A  @interface B : A\0A  - (id)method __attribute__((availability(macos,introduced=10.3))); // okay: method moved into base class later\0A  - (id)method __attribute__((availability(macos,introduced=10.5))); // error: this method was available via the base class in 10.4\0A  @end\0A\0AStarting with the macOS 10.12 SDK, the ``API_AVAILABLE`` macro from\0A``<os/availability.h>`` can simplify the spelling:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  @interface A\0A  - (id)method API_AVAILABLE(macos(10.11)));\0A  - (id)otherMethod API_AVAILABLE(macos(10.11), ios(11.0));\0A  @end\0A\0AAvailability attributes can also be applied using a ``#pragma clang attribute``.\0AAny explicit availability attribute whose platform corresponds to the target\0Aplatform is applied to a declaration regardless of the availability attributes\0Aspecified in the pragma. For example, in the code below,\0A``hasExplicitAvailabilityAttribute`` will use the ``macOS`` availability\0Aattribute that is specified with the declaration, whereas\0A``getsThePragmaAvailabilityAttribute`` will use the ``macOS`` availability\0Aattribute that is applied by the pragma.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #pragma clang attribute push (__attribute__((availability(macOS, introduced=10.12))), apply_to=function)\0A  void getsThePragmaAvailabilityAttribute(void);\0A  void hasExplicitAvailabilityAttribute(void) __attribute__((availability(macos,introduced=10.4)));\0A  #pragma clang attribute pop\0A\0AFor platforms like ``watchOS`` and ``tvOS``, whose availability attributes can\0Abe implicitly inferred from an ``iOS`` availability attribute, the logic is\0Aslightly more complex. The explicit and the pragma-applied availability\0Aattributes whose platform corresponds to the target platform are applied as\0Adescribed in the previous paragraph. However, the implicitly inferred attributes\0Aare applied to a declaration only when there is no explicit or pragma-applied\0Aavailability attribute whose platform corresponds to the target platform. For\0Aexample, the function below will receive the ``tvOS`` availability from the\0Apragma rather than using the inferred ``iOS`` availability from the declaration:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #pragma clang attribute push (__attribute__((availability(tvOS, introduced=12.0))), apply_to=function)\0A  void getsThePragmaTVOSAvailabilityAttribute(void) __attribute__((availability(iOS,introduced=11.0)));\0A  #pragma clang attribute pop\0A\0AThe compiler is also able to apply implicitly inferred attributes from a pragma\0Aas well. For example, when targeting ``tvOS``, the function below will receive\0Aa ``tvOS`` availability attribute that is implicitly inferred from the ``iOS``\0Aavailability attribute applied by the pragma:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #pragma clang attribute push (__attribute__((availability(iOS, introduced=12.0))), apply_to=function)\0A  void infersTVOSAvailabilityFromPragma(void);\0A  #pragma clang attribute pop\0A\0AThe implicit attributes that are inferred from explicitly specified attributes\0Awhose platform corresponds to the target platform are applied to the declaration\0Aeven if there is an availability attribute that can be inferred from a pragma.\0AFor example, the function below will receive the ``tvOS, introduced=11.0``\0Aavailability that is inferred from the attribute on the declaration rather than\0Ainferring availability from the pragma:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #pragma clang attribute push (__attribute__((availability(iOS, unavailable))), apply_to=function)\0A  void infersTVOSAvailabilityFromAttributeNextToDeclaration(void)\0A    __attribute__((availability(iOS,introduced=11.0)));\0A  #pragma clang attribute pop\0A\0AAlso see the documentation for `@available\0A<http://clang.llvm.org/docs/LanguageExtensions.html#objective-c-available>`_\00", align 16
@_ZL40AttrDoc_AvailableOnlyInDefaultEvalMethod = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_BPFFastCall = internal constant [606 x i8] c"Functions annotated with this attribute are likely to be inlined by BPF JIT.\0AIt is assumed that inlined implementation uses less caller saved registers,\0Athan a regular function.\0ASpecifically, the following registers are likely to be preserved:\0A- ``R0`` if function return value is ``void``;\0A- ``R2-R5` if function takes 1 argument;\0A- ``R3-R5` if function takes 2 arguments;\0A- ``R4-R5` if function takes 3 arguments;\0A- ``R5`` if function takes 4 arguments;\0A\0AFor such functions Clang generates code pattern that allows BPF JIT\0Ato recognize and remove unnecessary spills and fills of the preserved\0Aregisters.\00", align 16
@_ZL30AttrDoc_BPFPreserveAccessIndex = internal constant [334 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((preserve_access_index))``\0Aattribute for the BPF target. This attribute may be attached to a\0Astruct or union declaration, where if -g is specified, it enables\0Apreserving struct or union member access debuginfo indices of this\0Astruct or union, similar to clang ``__builtin_preserve_access_index()``.\00", align 16
@_ZL31AttrDoc_BPFPreserveStaticOffset = internal constant [811 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((preserve_static_offset))``\0Aattribute for the BPF target. This attribute may be attached to a\0Astruct or union declaration. Reading or writing fields of types having\0Asuch annotation is guaranteed to generate LDX/ST/STX instruction with\0Aoffset corresponding to the field.\0A\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  struct foo {\0A    int a;\0A    int b;\0A  };\0A\0A  struct bar {\0A    int a;\0A    struct foo b;\0A  } __attribute__((preserve_static_offset));\0A\0A  void buz(struct bar *g) {\0A    g->b.a = 42;\0A  }\0A\0AThe assignment to ``g``'s field would produce an ST instruction with\0Aoffset 8: ``*(u32)(r1 + 8) = 42;``.\0A\0AWithout this attribute generated instructions might be different,\0Adepending on optimizations behavior. E.g. the example above could be\0Arewritten as ``r1 += 8; *(u32)(r1 + 0) = 42;``.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_BTFDeclTag = internal constant [396 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((btf_decl_tag(\22ARGUMENT\22)))`` attribute for\0Aall targets. This attribute may be attached to a struct/union, struct/union\0Afield, function, function parameter, variable or typedef declaration. If -g is\0Aspecified, the ``ARGUMENT`` info will be preserved in IR and be emitted to\0Adwarf. For BPF targets, the ``ARGUMENT`` info will be emitted to .BTF ELF\0Asection too.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Blocks = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
