@_ZL19AttrDoc_C11NoReturn = internal constant [313 x i8] c"A function declared as ``_Noreturn`` shall not return to its caller. The\0Acompiler will generate a diagnostic for a function declared as ``_Noreturn``\0Athat appears to be capable of returning to its caller. Despite being a type\0Aspecifier, the ``_Noreturn`` attribute cannot be specified on a function\0Apointer type.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_CFAuditedTransfer = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_CFGuard = internal constant [994 x i8] c"Code can indicate CFG checks are not wanted with the ``__declspec(guard(nocf))``\0Aattribute. This directs the compiler to not insert any CFG checks for the entire\0Afunction. This approach is typically used only sparingly in specific situations\0Awhere the programmer has manually inserted \22CFG-equivalent\22 protection. The\0Aprogrammer knows that they are calling through some read-only function table\0Awhose address is obtained through read-only memory references and for which the\0Aindex is masked to the function table limit. This approach may also be applied\0Ato small wrapper functions that are not inlined and that do nothing more than\0Amake a call through a function pointer. Since incorrect usage of this directive\0Acan compromise the security of CFG, the programmer must be very careful using\0Athe directive. Typically, this usage is limited to very small functions that\0Aonly call one function.\0A\0A`Control Flow Guard documentation <https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secbp/pe-metadata>`\00", align 16
@_ZL29AttrDoc_CFICanonicalJumpTable = internal constant [204 x i8] c"Use ``__attribute__((cfi_canonical_jump_table))`` on a function declaration to\0Amake the function's CFI jump table canonical. See :ref:`the CFI documentation\0A<cfi-canonical-jump-tables>` for more details.\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_CFReturnsNotRetained = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_CFReturnsRetained = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_CFUnknownTransfer = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_CPUDispatch = internal constant [3065 x i8] c"The ``cpu_specific`` and ``cpu_dispatch`` attributes are used to define and\0Aresolve multiversioned functions. This form of multiversioning provides a\0Amechanism for declaring versions across translation units and manually\0Aspecifying the resolved function list. A specified CPU defines a set of minimum\0Afeatures that are required for the function to be called. The result of this is\0Athat future processors execute the most restrictive version of the function the\0Anew processor can execute.\0A\0AIn addition, unlike the ICC implementation of this feature, the selection of the\0Aversion does not consider the manufacturer or microarchitecture of the processor.\0AIt tests solely the list of features that are both supported by the specified\0Aprocessor and present in the compiler-rt library. This can be surprising at times,\0Aas the runtime processor may be from a completely different manufacturer, as long\0Aas it supports the same feature set.\0A\0AThis can additionally be surprising, as some processors are indistringuishable from\0Aothers based on the list of testable features. When this happens, the variant\0Ais selected in an unspecified manner.\0A\0AFunction versions are defined with ``cpu_specific``, which takes one or more CPU\0Anames as a parameter. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  // Declares and defines the ivybridge version of single_cpu.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_specific(ivybridge)))\0A  void single_cpu(void){}\0A\0A  // Declares and defines the atom version of single_cpu.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_specific(atom)))\0A  void single_cpu(void){}\0A\0A  // Declares and defines both the ivybridge and atom version of multi_cpu.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_specific(ivybridge, atom)))\0A  void multi_cpu(void){}\0A\0AA dispatching (or resolving) function can be declared anywhere in a project's\0Asource code with ``cpu_dispatch``. This attribute takes one or more CPU names\0Aas a parameter (like ``cpu_specific``). Functions marked with ``cpu_dispatch``\0Aare not expected to be defined, only declared. If such a marked function has a\0Adefinition, any side effects of the function are ignored; trivial function\0Abodies are permissible for ICC compatibility.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  // Creates a resolver for single_cpu above.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_dispatch(ivybridge, atom)))\0A  void single_cpu(void){}\0A\0A  // Creates a resolver for multi_cpu, but adds a 3rd version defined in another\0A  // translation unit.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_dispatch(ivybridge, atom, sandybridge)))\0A  void multi_cpu(void){}\0A\0ANote that it is possible to have a resolving function that dispatches based on\0Amore or fewer options than are present in the program. Specifying fewer will\0Aresult in the omitted options not being considered during resolution. Specifying\0Aa version for resolution that isn't defined in the program will result in a\0Alinking failure.\0A\0AIt is also possible to specify a CPU name of ``generic`` which will be resolved\0Aif the executing processor doesn't satisfy the features required in the CPU\0Aname. The behavior of a program executing on a processor that doesn't satisfy\0Aany option of a multiversioned function is undefined.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_CPUSpecific = internal constant [3065 x i8] c"The ``cpu_specific`` and ``cpu_dispatch`` attributes are used to define and\0Aresolve multiversioned functions. This form of multiversioning provides a\0Amechanism for declaring versions across translation units and manually\0Aspecifying the resolved function list. A specified CPU defines a set of minimum\0Afeatures that are required for the function to be called. The result of this is\0Athat future processors execute the most restrictive version of the function the\0Anew processor can execute.\0A\0AIn addition, unlike the ICC implementation of this feature, the selection of the\0Aversion does not consider the manufacturer or microarchitecture of the processor.\0AIt tests solely the list of features that are both supported by the specified\0Aprocessor and present in the compiler-rt library. This can be surprising at times,\0Aas the runtime processor may be from a completely different manufacturer, as long\0Aas it supports the same feature set.\0A\0AThis can additionally be surprising, as some processors are indistringuishable from\0Aothers based on the list of testable features. When this happens, the variant\0Ais selected in an unspecified manner.\0A\0AFunction versions are defined with ``cpu_specific``, which takes one or more CPU\0Anames as a parameter. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  // Declares and defines the ivybridge version of single_cpu.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_specific(ivybridge)))\0A  void single_cpu(void){}\0A\0A  // Declares and defines the atom version of single_cpu.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_specific(atom)))\0A  void single_cpu(void){}\0A\0A  // Declares and defines both the ivybridge and atom version of multi_cpu.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_specific(ivybridge, atom)))\0A  void multi_cpu(void){}\0A\0AA dispatching (or resolving) function can be declared anywhere in a project's\0Asource code with ``cpu_dispatch``. This attribute takes one or more CPU names\0Aas a parameter (like ``cpu_specific``). Functions marked with ``cpu_dispatch``\0Aare not expected to be defined, only declared. If such a marked function has a\0Adefinition, any side effects of the function are ignored; trivial function\0Abodies are permissible for ICC compatibility.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  // Creates a resolver for single_cpu above.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_dispatch(ivybridge, atom)))\0A  void single_cpu(void){}\0A\0A  // Creates a resolver for multi_cpu, but adds a 3rd version defined in another\0A  // translation unit.\0A  __attribute__((cpu_dispatch(ivybridge, atom, sandybridge)))\0A  void multi_cpu(void){}\0A\0ANote that it is possible to have a resolving function that dispatches based on\0Amore or fewer options than are present in the program. Specifying fewer will\0Aresult in the omitted options not being considered during resolution. Specifying\0Aa version for resolution that isn't defined in the program will result in a\0Alinking failure.\0A\0AIt is also possible to specify a CPU name of ``generic`` which will be resolved\0Aif the executing processor doesn't satisfy the features required in the CPU\0Aname. The behavior of a program executing on a processor that doesn't satisfy\0Aany option of a multiversioned function is undefined.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_CUDAConstant = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
