@_ZL21AttrDoc_CXX11NoReturn = internal constant [365 x i8] c"A function declared as ``[[noreturn]]`` shall not return to its caller. The\0Acompiler will generate a diagnostic for a function declared as ``[[noreturn]]``\0Athat appears to be capable of returning to its caller.\0A\0AThe ``[[_Noreturn]]`` spelling is deprecated and only exists to ease code\0Amigration for code using ``[[noreturn]]`` after including ``<stdnoreturn.h>``.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_CallableWhen = internal constant [270 x i8] c"Use ``__attribute__((callable_when(...)))`` to indicate what states a method\0Amay be called in. Valid states are unconsumed, consumed, or unknown. Each\0Aargument to this attribute must be a quoted string. E.g.:\0A\0A``__attribute__((callable_when(\22unconsumed\22, \22unknown\22)))``\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_Callback = internal constant [2582 x i8] c"The ``callback`` attribute specifies that the annotated function may invoke the\0Aspecified callback zero or more times. The callback, as well as the passed\0Aarguments, are identified by their parameter name or position (starting with\0A1!) in the annotated function. The first position in the attribute identifies\0Athe callback callee, the following positions declare describe its arguments.\0AThe callback callee is required to be callable with the number, and order, of\0Athe specified arguments. The index ``0``, or the identifier ``this``, is used to\0Arepresent an implicit \22this\22 pointer in class methods. If there is no implicit\0A\22this\22 pointer it shall not be referenced. The index '-1', or the name \22__\22,\0Arepresents an unknown callback callee argument. This can be a value which is\0Anot present in the declared parameter list, or one that is, but is potentially\0Ainspected, captured, or modified. Parameter names and indices can be mixed in\0Athe callback attribute.\0A\0AThe ``callback`` attribute, which is directly translated to ``callback``\0Ametadata <http://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#callback-metadata>, make the\0Aconnection between the call to the annotated function and the callback callee.\0AThis can enable interprocedural optimizations which were otherwise impossible.\0AIf a function parameter is mentioned in the ``callback`` attribute, through its\0Aposition, it is undefined if that parameter is used for anything other than the\0Aactual callback. Inspected, captured, or modified parameters shall not be\0Alisted in the ``callback`` metadata.\0A\0AExample encodings for the callback performed by ``pthread_create`` are shown\0Abelow. The explicit attribute annotation indicates that the third parameter\0A(``start_routine``) is called zero or more times by the ``pthread_create`` function,\0Aand that the fourth parameter (``arg``) is passed along. Note that the callback\0Abehavior of ``pthread_create`` is automatically recognized by Clang. In addition,\0Athe declarations of ``__kmpc_fork_teams`` and ``__kmpc_fork_call``, generated for\0A``#pragma omp target teams`` and ``#pragma omp parallel``, respectively, are also\0Aautomatically recognized as broker functions. Further functions might be added\0Ain the future.\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    __attribute__((callback (start_routine, arg)))\0A    int pthread_create(pthread_t *thread, const pthread_attr_t *attr,\0A                       void *(*start_routine) (void *), void *arg);\0A\0A    __attribute__((callback (3, 4)))\0A    int pthread_create(pthread_t *thread, const pthread_attr_t *attr,\0A                       void *(*start_routine) (void *), void *arg);\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_Capability = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_CapturedRecord = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL15AttrDoc_Cleanup = internal constant [850 x i8] c"This attribute allows a function to be run when a local variable goes out of\0Ascope. The attribute takes the identifier of a function with a parameter type\0Athat is a pointer to the type with the attribute.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  static void foo (int *) { ... }\0A  static void bar (int *) { ... }\0A  void baz (void) {\0A    int x __attribute__((cleanup(foo)));\0A    {\0A      int y __attribute__((cleanup(bar)));\0A    }\0A  }\0A\0AThe above example will result in a call to ``bar`` being passed the address of\0A`y`` when ``y`` goes out of scope, then a call to ``foo`` being passed the\0Aaddress of ``x`` when ``x`` goes out of scope. If two or more variables share\0Athe same scope, their ``cleanup`` callbacks are invoked in the reverse order\0Athe variables were declared in. It is not possible to check the return value\0A(if any) of these ``cleanup`` callback functions.\00", align 16
@_ZL26AttrDoc_ClspvLibclcBuiltin = internal constant [305 x i8] c"Attribute used by `clspv`_ (OpenCL-C to Vulkan SPIR-V compiler) to identify functions coming from `libclc`_ (OpenCL-C builtin library).\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void __attribute__((clspv_libclc_builtin)) libclc_builtin() {}\0A\0A.. _`clspv`: https://github.com/google/clspv\0A.. _`libclc`: https://libclc.llvm.org\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_CmseNSEntry = internal constant [465 x i8] c"This attribute declares a function that can be called from non-secure state, or\0Afrom secure state. Entering from and returning to non-secure state would switch\0Ato and from secure state, respectively, and prevent flow of information\0Ato non-secure state, except via return values. See `ARMv8-M Security Extensions:\0ARequirements on Development Tools - Engineering Specification Documentation\0A<https://developer.arm.com/docs/ecm0359818/latest/>`_ for more information.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_CodeModel = internal constant [136 x i8] c"The ``model`` attribute allows overriding the translation unit's\0Acode model (specified by ``-mcmodel``) for a specific global variable.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_CodeSeg = internal constant [395 x i8] c"The ``__declspec(code_seg)`` attribute enables the placement of code into separate\0Anamed segments that can be paged or locked in memory individually. This attribute\0Ais used to control the placement of instantiated templates and compiler-generated\0Acode. See the documentation for `__declspec(code_seg)`_ on MSDN.\0A\0A.. _`__declspec(code_seg)`: http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dn636922.aspx\00", align 16
@_ZL12AttrDoc_Cold = internal constant [241 x i8] c"``__attribute__((cold))`` marks a function as cold, as a manual alternative to PGO hotness data.\0AIf PGO data is available, the profile count based hotness overrides the ``__attribute__((cold))`` annotation (unlike ``__attribute__((hot))``).\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Common = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_Const = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_ConstInit = internal constant [1995 x i8] c"This attribute specifies that the variable to which it is attached is intended\0Ato have a `constant initializer <http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/constant_initialization>`_\0Aaccording to the rules of [basic.start.static]. The variable is required to\0Ahave static or thread storage duration. If the initialization of the variable\0Ais not a constant initializer an error will be produced. This attribute may\0Aonly be used in C++; the ``constinit`` spelling is only accepted in C++20\0Aonwards.\0A\0ANote that in C++03 strict constant expression checking is not done. Instead\0Athe attribute reports if Clang can emit the variable as a constant, even if it's\0Anot technically a 'constant initializer'. This behavior is non-portable.\0A\0AStatic storage duration variables with constant initializers avoid hard-to-find\0Abugs caused by the indeterminate order of dynamic initialization. They can also\0Abe safely used during dynamic initialization across translation units.\0A\0AThis attribute acts as a compile time assertion that the requirements\0Afor constant initialization have been met. Since these requirements change\0Abetween dialects and have subtle pitfalls it's important to fail fast instead\0Aof silently falling back on dynamic initialization.\0A\0AThe first use of the attribute on a variable must be part of, or precede, the\0Ainitializing declaration of the variable. C++20 requires the ``constinit``\0Aspelling of the attribute to be present on the initializing declaration if it\0Ais used anywhere. The other spellings can be specified on a forward declaration\0Aand omitted on a later initializing declaration.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  // -std=c++14\0A  #define SAFE_STATIC [[clang::require_constant_initialization]]\0A  struct T {\0A    constexpr T(int) {}\0A    ~T(); // non-trivial\0A  };\0A  SAFE_STATIC T x = {42}; // Initialization OK. Doesn't check destructor.\0A  SAFE_STATIC T y = 42; // error: variable does not have a constant initializer\0A  // copy initialization is not a constant expression on a non-literal type.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_Constructor = internal constant [1229 x i8] c"The ``constructor`` attribute causes the function to be called before entering\0A``main()``, and the ``destructor`` attribute causes the function to be called\0Aafter returning from ``main()`` or when the ``exit()`` function has been\0Acalled. Note, ``quick_exit()``, ``_Exit()``, and ``abort()`` prevent a function\0Amarked ``destructor`` from being called.\0A\0AThe constructor or destructor function should not accept any arguments and its\0Areturn type should be ``void``.\0A\0AThe attributes accept an optional argument used to specify the priority order\0Ain which to execute constructor and destructor functions. The priority is\0Agiven as an integer constant expression between 101 and 65535 (inclusive).\0APriorities outside of that range are reserved for use by the implementation. A\0Alower value indicates a higher priority of initialization. Note that only the\0Arelative ordering of values is important. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  __attribute__((constructor(200))) void foo(void);\0A  __attribute__((constructor(101))) void bar(void);\0A\0A``bar()`` will be called before ``foo()``, and both will be called before\0A``main()``. If no argument is given to the ``constructor`` or ``destructor``\0Aattribute, they default to the value ``65535``.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_Consumable = internal constant [282 x i8] c"Each ``class`` that uses any of the typestate annotations must first be marked\0Ausing the ``consumable`` attribute. Failure to do so will result in a warning.\0A\0AThis attribute accepts a single parameter that must be one of the following:\0A``unknown``, ``consumed``, or ``unconsumed``.\00", align 16
@_ZL26AttrDoc_ConsumableAutoCast = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_ConsumableSetOnRead = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_Convergent = internal constant [933 x i8] c"The ``convergent`` attribute can be placed on a function declaration. It is\0Atranslated into the LLVM ``convergent`` attribute, which indicates that the call\0Ainstructions of a function with this attribute cannot be made control-dependent\0Aon any additional values.\0A\0AIn languages designed for SPMD/SIMT programming model, e.g. OpenCL or CUDA,\0Athe call instructions of a function with this attribute must be executed by\0Aall work items or threads in a work group or sub group.\0A\0AThis attribute is different from ``noduplicate`` because it allows duplicating\0Afunction calls if it can be proved that the duplicated function calls are\0Anot made control-dependent on any additional values, e.g., unrolling a loop\0Aexecuted by all work items.\0A\0ASample usage:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void convfunc(void) __attribute__((convergent));\0A  // Setting it as a C++11 attribute is also valid in a C++ program.\0A  // void convfunc(void) [[clang::convergent]];\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_CoroAwaitElidable = internal constant [1696 x i8] c"The ``[[clang::coro_await_elidable]]`` is a class attribute which can be\0Aapplied to a coroutine return type. It provides a hint to the compiler to apply\0AHeap Allocation Elision more aggressively.\0A\0AWhen a coroutine function returns such a type, a direct call expression therein\0Athat returns a prvalue of a type attributed ``[[clang::coro_await_elidable]]``\0Ais said to be under a safe elide context if one of the following is true:\0A- it is the immediate right-hand side operand to a co_await expression.\0A- it is an argument to a ``[[clang::coro_await_elidable_argument]]`` parameter\0Aor parameter pack of another direct call expression under a safe elide context.\0A\0ADo note that the safe elide context applies only to the call expression itself,\0Aand the context does not transitively include any of its subexpressions unless\0Aexceptional rules of ``[[clang::coro_await_elidable_argument]]`` apply.\0A\0AThe compiler performs heap allocation elision on call expressions under a safe\0Aelide context, if the callee is a coroutine.\0A\0AExample:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  class [[clang::coro_await_elidable]] Task { ... };\0A\0A  Task foo();\0A  Task bar() {\0A    co_await foo(); // foo()'s coroutine frame on this line is elidable\0A    auto t = foo(); // foo()'s coroutine frame on this line is NOT elidable\0A    co_await t;\0A  }\0A\0ASuch elision replaces the heap allocated activation frame of the callee coroutine\0Awith a local variable within the enclosing braces in the caller's stack frame.\0AThe local variable, like other variables in coroutines, may be collected into the\0Acoroutine frame, which may be allocated on the heap. The behavior is undefined\0Aif the caller coroutine is destroyed earlier than the callee coroutine.\00", align 16
@_ZL33AttrDoc_CoroAwaitElidableArgument = internal constant [1421 x i8] c"The ``[[clang::coro_await_elidable_argument]]`` is a function parameter attribute.\0AIt works in conjunction with ``[[clang::coro_await_elidable]]`` to propagate a\0Asafe elide context to a parameter or parameter pack if the function is called\0Aunder a safe elide context.\0A\0AThis is sometimes necessary on utility functions used to compose or modify the\0Abehavior of a callee coroutine.\0A\0AExample:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  template <typename T>\0A  class [[clang::coro_await_elidable]] Task { ... };\0A\0A  template <typename... T>\0A  class [[clang::coro_await_elidable]] WhenAll { ... };\0A\0A  // `when_all` is a utility function that composes coroutines. It does not\0A  // need to be a coroutine to propagate.\0A  template <typename... T>\0A  WhenAll<T...> when_all([[clang::coro_await_elidable_argument]] Task<T> tasks...);\0A\0A  Task<int> foo();\0A  Task<int> bar();\0A  Task<void> example1() {\0A    // `when_all``, `foo``, and `bar` are all elide safe because `when_all` is\0A    // under a safe elide context and, thanks to the [[clang::coro_await_elidable_argument]]\0A    // attribute, such context is propagated to foo and bar.\0A    co_await when_all(foo(), bar());\0A  }\0A\0A  Task<void> example2() {\0A    // `when_all` and `bar` are elide safe. `foo` is not elide safe.\0A    auto f = foo();\0A    co_await when_all(f, bar());\0A  }\0A\0A\0A  Task<void> example3() {\0A    // None of the calls are elide safe.\0A    auto t = when_all(foo(), bar());\0A    co_await t;\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL32AttrDoc_CoroDisableLifetimeBound = internal constant [3125 x i8] c"The ``[[clang::coro_lifetimebound]]`` is a class attribute which can be applied\0Ato a coroutine return type (`CRT`_) (i.e.\0Ait should also be annotated with ``[[clang::coro_return_type]]``).\0A\0AAll parameters of a function are considered to be lifetime bound if the function returns a\0Acoroutine return type (CRT) annotated with ``[[clang::coro_lifetimebound]]``.\0AThis lifetime bound analysis can be disabled for a coroutine wrapper or a coroutine by annotating the function\0Awith ``[[clang::coro_disable_lifetimebound]]`` function attribute .\0ASee `documentation`_ of ``[[clang::lifetimebound]]`` for details about lifetime bound analysis.\0A\0A\0AReference parameters of a coroutine are susceptible to capturing references to temporaries or local variables.\0A\0AFor example,\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  task<int> coro(const int& a) { co_return a + 1; }\0A  task<int> dangling_refs(int a) {\0A    // `coro` captures reference to a temporary. `foo` would now contain a dangling reference to `a`.\0A    auto foo = coro(1);\0A    // `coro` captures reference to local variable `a` which is destroyed after the return.\0A    return coro(a);\0A  }\0A\0ALifetime bound static analysis can be used to detect such instances when coroutines capture references\0Awhich may die earlier than the coroutine frame itself. In the above example, if the CRT `task` is annotated with\0A``[[clang::coro_lifetimebound]]``, then lifetime bound analysis would detect capturing reference to\0Atemporaries or return address of a local variable.\0A\0ABoth coroutines and coroutine wrappers are part of this analysis.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  template <typename T> struct [[clang::coro_return_type, clang::coro_lifetimebound]] Task {\0A    using promise_type = some_promise_type;\0A  };\0A\0A  Task<int> coro(const int& a) { co_return a + 1; }\0A  [[clang::coro_wrapper]] Task<int> coro_wrapper(const int& a, const int& b) {\0A    return a > b ? coro(a) : coro(b);\0A  }\0A  Task<int> temporary_reference() {\0A    auto foo = coro(1); // warning: capturing reference to a temporary which would die after the expression.\0A\0A    int a = 1;\0A    auto bar = coro_wrapper(a, 0); // warning: `b` captures reference to a temporary.\0A\0A    co_return co_await coro(1); // fine.\0A  }\0A  [[clang::coro_wrapper]] Task<int> stack_reference(int a) {\0A    return coro(a); // warning: returning address of stack variable `a`.\0A  }\0A\0AThis analysis can be disabled for all calls to a particular function by annotating the function\0Awith function attribute ``[[clang::coro_disable_lifetimebound]]``.\0AFor example, this could be useful for coroutine wrappers which accept reference parameters\0Abut do not pass them to the underlying coroutine or pass them by value.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  Task<int> coro(int a) { co_return a + 1; }\0A  [[clang::coro_wrapper, clang::coro_disable_lifetimebound]] Task<int> coro_wrapper(const int& a) {\0A    return coro(a + 1);\0A  }\0A  void use() {\0A    auto task = coro_wrapper(1); // use of temporary is fine as the argument is not lifetime bound.\0A  }\0A\0A.. _`documentation`: https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#lifetimebound\0A.. _`CRT`: https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#coro-return-type\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_CoroLifetimeBound = internal constant [3125 x i8] c"The ``[[clang::coro_lifetimebound]]`` is a class attribute which can be applied\0Ato a coroutine return type (`CRT`_) (i.e.\0Ait should also be annotated with ``[[clang::coro_return_type]]``).\0A\0AAll parameters of a function are considered to be lifetime bound if the function returns a\0Acoroutine return type (CRT) annotated with ``[[clang::coro_lifetimebound]]``.\0AThis lifetime bound analysis can be disabled for a coroutine wrapper or a coroutine by annotating the function\0Awith ``[[clang::coro_disable_lifetimebound]]`` function attribute .\0ASee `documentation`_ of ``[[clang::lifetimebound]]`` for details about lifetime bound analysis.\0A\0A\0AReference parameters of a coroutine are susceptible to capturing references to temporaries or local variables.\0A\0AFor example,\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  task<int> coro(const int& a) { co_return a + 1; }\0A  task<int> dangling_refs(int a) {\0A    // `coro` captures reference to a temporary. `foo` would now contain a dangling reference to `a`.\0A    auto foo = coro(1);\0A    // `coro` captures reference to local variable `a` which is destroyed after the return.\0A    return coro(a);\0A  }\0A\0ALifetime bound static analysis can be used to detect such instances when coroutines capture references\0Awhich may die earlier than the coroutine frame itself. In the above example, if the CRT `task` is annotated with\0A``[[clang::coro_lifetimebound]]``, then lifetime bound analysis would detect capturing reference to\0Atemporaries or return address of a local variable.\0A\0ABoth coroutines and coroutine wrappers are part of this analysis.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  template <typename T> struct [[clang::coro_return_type, clang::coro_lifetimebound]] Task {\0A    using promise_type = some_promise_type;\0A  };\0A\0A  Task<int> coro(const int& a) { co_return a + 1; }\0A  [[clang::coro_wrapper]] Task<int> coro_wrapper(const int& a, const int& b) {\0A    return a > b ? coro(a) : coro(b);\0A  }\0A  Task<int> temporary_reference() {\0A    auto foo = coro(1); // warning: capturing reference to a temporary which would die after the expression.\0A\0A    int a = 1;\0A    auto bar = coro_wrapper(a, 0); // warning: `b` captures reference to a temporary.\0A\0A    co_return co_await coro(1); // fine.\0A  }\0A  [[clang::coro_wrapper]] Task<int> stack_reference(int a) {\0A    return coro(a); // warning: returning address of stack variable `a`.\0A  }\0A\0AThis analysis can be disabled for all calls to a particular function by annotating the function\0Awith function attribute ``[[clang::coro_disable_lifetimebound]]``.\0AFor example, this could be useful for coroutine wrappers which accept reference parameters\0Abut do not pass them to the underlying coroutine or pass them by value.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  Task<int> coro(int a) { co_return a + 1; }\0A  [[clang::coro_wrapper, clang::coro_disable_lifetimebound]] Task<int> coro_wrapper(const int& a) {\0A    return coro(a + 1);\0A  }\0A  void use() {\0A    auto task = coro_wrapper(1); // use of temporary is fine as the argument is not lifetime bound.\0A  }\0A\0A.. _`documentation`: https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#lifetimebound\0A.. _`CRT`: https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#coro-return-type\00", align 16
@_ZL35AttrDoc_CoroOnlyDestroyWhenComplete = internal constant [1571 x i8] c"The `coro_only_destroy_when_complete` attribute should be marked on a C++ class. The coroutines\0Awhose return type is marked with the attribute are assumed to be destroyed only after the coroutine has\0Areached the final suspend point.\0A\0AThis is helpful for the optimizers to reduce the size of the destroy function for the coroutines.\0A\0AFor example,\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  A foo() {\0A    dtor d;\0A    co_await something();\0A    dtor d1;\0A    co_await something();\0A    dtor d2;\0A    co_return 43;\0A  }\0A\0AThe compiler may generate the following pseudocode:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void foo.destroy(foo.Frame *frame) {\0A    switch(frame->suspend_index()) {\0A      case 1:\0A        frame->d.~dtor();\0A        break;\0A      case 2:\0A        frame->d.~dtor();\0A        frame->d1.~dtor();\0A        break;\0A      case 3:\0A        frame->d.~dtor();\0A        frame->d1.~dtor();\0A        frame->d2.~dtor();\0A        break;\0A      default: // coroutine completed or haven't started\0A        break;\0A    }\0A\0A    frame->promise.~promise_type();\0A    delete frame;\0A  }\0A\0AThe `foo.destroy()` function's purpose is to release all of the resources\0Ainitialized for the coroutine when it is destroyed in a suspended state.\0AHowever, if the coroutine is only ever destroyed at the final suspend state,\0Athe rest of the conditions are superfluous.\0A\0AThe user can use the `coro_only_destroy_when_complete` attributo suppress\0Ageneration of the other destruction cases, optimizing the above `foo.destroy` to:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void foo.destroy(foo.Frame *frame) {\0A    frame->promise.~promise_type();\0A    delete frame;\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_CoroReturnType = internal constant [2282 x i8] c"The ``[[clang::coro_return_type]]`` attribute is used to help static analyzers to recognize\0Acoroutines from the function signatures.\0A\0AThe ``coro_return_type`` attribute should be marked on a C++ class to mark it as\0Aa **coroutine return type (CRT)**.\0A\0AA function ``R func(P1, .., PN)`` has a coroutine return type (CRT) ``R`` if ``R``\0Ais marked by ``[[clang::coro_return_type]]`` and  ``R`` has a promise type associated to it\0A(i.e., std::coroutine_traits<R, P1, .., PN>::promise_type is a valid promise type).\0A\0AIf the return type of a function is a ``CRT`` then the function must be a coroutine.\0AOtherwise the program is invalid. It is allowed for a non-coroutine to return a ``CRT``\0Aif the function is marked with ``[[clang::coro_wrapper]]``.\0A\0AThe ``[[clang::coro_wrapper]]`` attribute should be marked on a C++ function to mark it as\0Aa **coroutine wrapper**. A coroutine wrapper is a function which returns a ``CRT``,\0Ais not a coroutine itself and is marked with ``[[clang::coro_wrapper]]``.\0A\0AClang will enforce that all functions that return a ``CRT`` are either coroutines or marked\0Awith ``[[clang::coro_wrapper]]``. Clang will enforce this with an error.\0A\0AFrom a language perspective, it is not possible to differentiate between a coroutine and a\0Afunction returning a CRT by merely looking at the function signature.\0A\0ACoroutine wrappers, in particular, are susceptible to capturing\0Areferences to temporaries and other lifetime issues. This allows to avoid such lifetime\0Aissues with coroutine wrappers.\0A\0AFor example,\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  // This is a CRT.\0A  template <typename T> struct [[clang::coro_return_type]] Task {\0A    using promise_type = some_promise_type;\0A  };\0A\0A  Task<int> increment(int a) { co_return a + 1; } // Fine. This is a coroutine.\0A  Task<int> foo() { return increment(1); } // Error. foo is not a coroutine.\0A\0A  // Fine for a coroutine wrapper to return a CRT.\0A  [[clang::coro_wrapper]] Task<int> foo() { return increment(1); }\0A\0A  void bar() {\0A    // Invalid. This intantiates a function which returns a CRT but is not marked as\0A    // a coroutine wrapper.\0A    std::function<Task<int>(int)> f = increment;\0A  }\0A\0ANote: ``a_promise_type::get_return_object`` is exempted from this analysis as it is a necessary\0Aimplementation detail of any coroutine library.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_CoroWrapper = internal constant [2282 x i8] c"The ``[[clang::coro_return_type]]`` attribute is used to help static analyzers to recognize\0Acoroutines from the function signatures.\0A\0AThe ``coro_return_type`` attribute should be marked on a C++ class to mark it as\0Aa **coroutine return type (CRT)**.\0A\0AA function ``R func(P1, .., PN)`` has a coroutine return type (CRT) ``R`` if ``R``\0Ais marked by ``[[clang::coro_return_type]]`` and  ``R`` has a promise type associated to it\0A(i.e., std::coroutine_traits<R, P1, .., PN>::promise_type is a valid promise type).\0A\0AIf the return type of a function is a ``CRT`` then the function must be a coroutine.\0AOtherwise the program is invalid. It is allowed for a non-coroutine to return a ``CRT``\0Aif the function is marked with ``[[clang::coro_wrapper]]``.\0A\0AThe ``[[clang::coro_wrapper]]`` attribute should be marked on a C++ function to mark it as\0Aa **coroutine wrapper**. A coroutine wrapper is a function which returns a ``CRT``,\0Ais not a coroutine itself and is marked with ``[[clang::coro_wrapper]]``.\0A\0AClang will enforce that all functions that return a ``CRT`` are either coroutines or marked\0Awith ``[[clang::coro_wrapper]]``. Clang will enforce this with an error.\0A\0AFrom a language perspective, it is not possible to differentiate between a coroutine and a\0Afunction returning a CRT by merely looking at the function signature.\0A\0ACoroutine wrappers, in particular, are susceptible to capturing\0Areferences to temporaries and other lifetime issues. This allows to avoid such lifetime\0Aissues with coroutine wrappers.\0A\0AFor example,\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  // This is a CRT.\0A  template <typename T> struct [[clang::coro_return_type]] Task {\0A    using promise_type = some_promise_type;\0A  };\0A\0A  Task<int> increment(int a) { co_return a + 1; } // Fine. This is a coroutine.\0A  Task<int> foo() { return increment(1); } // Error. foo is not a coroutine.\0A\0A  // Fine for a coroutine wrapper to return a CRT.\0A  [[clang::coro_wrapper]] Task<int> foo() { return increment(1); }\0A\0A  void bar() {\0A    // Invalid. This intantiates a function which returns a CRT but is not marked as\0A    // a coroutine wrapper.\0A    std::function<Task<int>(int)> f = increment;\0A  }\0A\0ANote: ``a_promise_type::get_return_object`` is exempted from this analysis as it is a necessary\0Aimplementation detail of any coroutine library.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_DLLExport = internal constant [464 x i8] c"The ``__declspec(dllexport)`` attribute declares a variable, function, or\0AObjective-C interface to be exported from the module. It is available under the\0A``-fdeclspec`` flag for compatibility with various compilers. The primary use\0Ais for COFF object files which explicitly specify what interfaces are available\0Afor external use. See the dllexport_ documentation on MSDN for more\0Ainformation.\0A\0A.. _dllexport: https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/3y1sfaz2.aspx\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_DLLExportStaticLocal = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL17AttrDoc_DLLImport = internal constant [635 x i8] c"The ``__declspec(dllimport)`` attribute declares a variable, function, or\0AObjective-C interface to be imported from an external module. It is available\0Aunder the ``-fdeclspec`` flag for compatibility with various compilers. The\0Aprimary use is for COFF object files which explicitly specify what interfaces\0Aare imported from external modules. See the dllimport_ documentation on MSDN\0Afor more information.\0A\0ANote that a dllimport function may still be inlined, if its definition is\0Aavailable and it doesn't reference any non-dllimport functions or global\0Avariables.\0A\0A.. _dllimport: https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/3y1sfaz2.aspx\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_DLLImportStaticLocal = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL18AttrDoc_Deprecated = internal constant [844 x i8] c"The ``deprecated`` attribute can be applied to a function, a variable, or a\0Atype. This is useful when identifying functions, variables, or types that are\0Aexpected to be removed in a future version of a program.\0A\0AConsider the function declaration for a hypothetical function ``f``:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void f(void) __attribute__((deprecated(\22message\22, \22replacement\22)));\0A\0AWhen spelled as ``__attribute__((deprecated))``, the deprecated attribute can have\0Atwo optional string arguments. The first one is the message to display when\0Aemitting the warning; the second one enables the compiler to provide a Fix-It\0Ato replace the deprecated name with a new name. Otherwise, when spelled as\0A``[[gnu::deprecated]]`` or ``[[deprecated]]``, the attribute can have one optional\0Astring argument which is the message to display when emitting the warning.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_Destructor = internal constant [1229 x i8] c"The ``constructor`` attribute causes the function to be called before entering\0A``main()``, and the ``destructor`` attribute causes the function to be called\0Aafter returning from ``main()`` or when the ``exit()`` function has been\0Acalled. Note, ``quick_exit()``, ``_Exit()``, and ``abort()`` prevent a function\0Amarked ``destructor`` from being called.\0A\0AThe constructor or destructor function should not accept any arguments and its\0Areturn type should be ``void``.\0A\0AThe attributes accept an optional argument used to specify the priority order\0Ain which to execute constructor and destructor functions. The priority is\0Agiven as an integer constant expression between 101 and 65535 (inclusive).\0APriorities outside of that range are reserved for use by the implementation. A\0Alower value indicates a higher priority of initialization. Note that only the\0Arelative ordering of values is important. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  __attribute__((constructor(200))) void foo(void);\0A  __attribute__((constructor(101))) void bar(void);\0A\0A``bar()`` will be called before ``foo()``, and both will be called before\0A``main()``. If no argument is given to the ``constructor`` or ``destructor``\0Aattribute, they default to the value ``65535``.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_DiagnoseAsBuiltin = internal constant [1598 x i8] c"The ``diagnose_as_builtin`` attribute indicates that Fortify diagnostics are to\0Abe applied to the declared function as if it were the function specified by the\0Aattribute. The builtin function whose diagnostics are to be mimicked should be\0Agiven. In addition, the order in which arguments should be applied must also\0Abe given.\0A\0AFor example, the attribute can be used as follows.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  __attribute__((diagnose_as_builtin(__builtin_memset, 3, 2, 1)))\0A  void *mymemset(int n, int c, void *s) {\0A    // ...\0A  }\0A\0AThis indicates that calls to ``mymemset`` should be diagnosed as if they were\0Acalls to ``__builtin_memset``. The arguments ``3, 2, 1`` indicate by index the\0Aorder in which arguments of ``mymemset`` should be applied to\0A``__builtin_memset``. The third argument should be applied first, then the\0Asecond, and then the first. Thus (when Fortify warnings are enabled) the call\0A``mymemset(n, c, s)`` will diagnose overflows as if it were the call\0A``__builtin_memset(s, c, n)``.\0A\0AFor variadic functions, the variadic arguments must come in the same order as\0Athey would to the builtin function, after all normal arguments. For instance,\0Ato diagnose a new function as if it were `sscanf`, we can use the attribute as\0Afollows.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  __attribute__((diagnose_as_builtin(sscanf, 1, 2)))\0A  int mysscanf(const char *str, const char *format, ...)  {\0A    // ...\0A  }\0A\0AThen the call `mysscanf(\22abc def\22, \22%4s %4s\22, buf1, buf2)` will be diagnosed as\0Aif it were the call `sscanf(\22abc def\22, \22%4s %4s\22, buf1, buf2)`.\0A\0AThis attribute cannot be applied to non-static member functions.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_DiagnoseIf = internal constant [2228 x i8] c"The ``diagnose_if`` attribute can be placed on function declarations to emit\0Awarnings or errors at compile-time if calls to the attributed function meet\0Acertain user-defined criteria. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int abs(int a)\0A    __attribute__((diagnose_if(a >= 0, \22Redundant abs call\22, \22warning\22)));\0A  int must_abs(int a)\0A    __attribute__((diagnose_if(a >= 0, \22Redundant abs call\22, \22error\22)));\0A\0A  int val = abs(1); // warning: Redundant abs call\0A  int val2 = must_abs(1); // error: Redundant abs call\0A  int val3 = abs(val);\0A  int val4 = must_abs(val); // Because run-time checks are not emitted for\0A                            // diagnose_if attributes, this executes without\0A                            // issue.\0A\0A\0A``diagnose_if`` is closely related to ``enable_if``, with a few key differences:\0A\0A* Overload resolution is not aware of ``diagnose_if`` attributes: they're\0A  considered only after we select the best candidate from a given candidate set.\0A* Function declarations that differ only in their ``diagnose_if`` attributes are\0A  considered to be redeclarations of the same function (not overloads).\0A* If the condition provided to ``diagnose_if`` cannot be evaluated, no\0A  diagnostic will be emitted.\0A\0AOtherwise, ``diagnose_if`` is essentially the logical negation of ``enable_if``.\0A\0AAs a result of bullet number two, ``diagnose_if`` attributes will stack on the\0Asame function. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int foo() __attribute__((diagnose_if(1, \22diag1\22, \22warning\22)));\0A  int foo() __attribute__((diagnose_if(1, \22diag2\22, \22warning\22)));\0A\0A  int bar = foo(); // warning: diag1\0A                   // warning: diag2\0A  int (*fooptr)(void) = foo; // warning: diag1\0A                             // warning: diag2\0A\0A  constexpr int supportsAPILevel(int N) { return N < 5; }\0A  int baz(int a)\0A    __attribute__((diagnose_if(!supportsAPILevel(10),\0A                               \22Upgrade to API level 10 to use baz\22, \22error\22)));\0A  int baz(int a)\0A    __attribute__((diagnose_if(!a, \220 is not recommended.\22, \22warning\22)));\0A\0A  int (*bazptr)(int) = baz; // error: Upgrade to API level 10 to use baz\0A  int v = baz(0); // error: Upgrade to API level 10 to use baz\0A\0AQuery for this feature with ``__has_attribute(diagnose_if)``.\00", align 16
@_ZL39AttrDoc_DisableSanitizerInstrumentation = internal constant [335 x i8] c"Use the ``disable_sanitizer_instrumentation`` attribute on a function,\0AObjective-C method, or global variable, to specify that no sanitizer\0Ainstrumentation should be applied.\0A\0AThis is not the same as ``__attribute__((no_sanitize(...)))``, which depending\0Aon the tool may still insert instrumentation to prevent false positive reports.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_DisableTailCalls = internal constant [740 x i8] c"The ``disable_tail_calls`` attribute instructs the backend to not perform tail\0Acall optimization inside the marked function.\0A\0AFor example:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    int callee(int);\0A\0A    int foo(int a) __attribute__((disable_tail_calls)) {\0A      return callee(a); // This call is not tail-call optimized.\0A    }\0A\0AMarking virtual functions as ``disable_tail_calls`` is legal.\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    int callee(int);\0A\0A    class Base {\0A    public:\0A      [[clang::disable_tail_calls]] virtual int foo1() {\0A        return callee(); // This call is not tail-call optimized.\0A      }\0A    };\0A\0A    class Derived1 : public Base {\0A    public:\0A      int foo1() override {\0A        return callee(); // This call is tail-call optimized.\0A      }\0A    };\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_EmptyBases = internal constant [221 x i8] c"The empty_bases attribute permits the compiler to utilize the\0Aempty-base-optimization more frequently.\0AThis attribute only applies to struct, class, and union types.\0AIt is only supported when using the Microsoft C++ ABI.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_EnableIf = internal constant [5911 x i8] c".. Note:: Some features of this attribute are experimental. The meaning of\0A  multiple enable_if attributes on a single declaration is subject to change in\0A  a future version of clang. Also, the ABI is not standardized and the name\0A  mangling may change in future versions. To avoid that, use asm labels.\0A\0AThe ``enable_if`` attribute can be placed on function declarations to control\0Awhich overload is selected based on the values of the function's arguments.\0AWhen combined with the ``overloadable`` attribute, this feature is also\0Aavailable in C.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  int isdigit(int c);\0A  int isdigit(int c) __attribute__((enable_if(c <= -1 || c > 255, \22chosen when 'c' is out of range\22))) __attribute__((unavailable(\22'c' must have the value of an unsigned char or EOF\22)));\0A\0A  void foo(char c) {\0A    isdigit(c);\0A    isdigit(10);\0A    isdigit(-10);  // results in a compile-time error.\0A  }\0A\0AThe enable_if attribute takes two arguments, the first is an expression written\0Ain terms of the function parameters, the second is a string explaining why this\0Aoverload candidate could not be selected to be displayed in diagnostics. The\0Aexpression is part of the function signature for the purposes of determining\0Awhether it is a redeclaration (following the rules used when determining\0Awhether a C++ template specialization is ODR-equivalent), but is not part of\0Athe type.\0A\0AThe enable_if expression is evaluated as if it were the body of a\0Abool-returning constexpr function declared with the arguments of the function\0Ait is being applied to, then called with the parameters at the call site. If the\0Aresult is false or could not be determined through constant expression\0Aevaluation, then this overload will not be chosen and the provided string may\0Abe used in a diagnostic if the compile fails as a result.\0A\0ABecause the enable_if expression is an unevaluated context, there are no global\0Astate changes, nor the ability to pass information from the enable_if\0Aexpression to the function body. For example, suppose we want calls to\0Astrnlen(strbuf, maxlen) to resolve to strnlen_chk(strbuf, maxlen, size of\0Astrbuf) only if the size of strbuf can be determined:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  __attribute__((always_inline))\0A  static inline size_t strnlen(const char *s, size_t maxlen)\0A    __attribute__((overloadable))\0A    __attribute__((enable_if(__builtin_object_size(s, 0) != -1))),\0A                             \22chosen when the buffer size is known but 'maxlen' is not\22)))\0A  {\0A    return strnlen_chk(s, maxlen, __builtin_object_size(s, 0));\0A  }\0A\0AMultiple enable_if attributes may be applied to a single declaration. In this\0Acase, the enable_if expressions are evaluated from left to right in the\0Afollowing manner. First, the candidates whose enable_if expressions evaluate to\0Afalse or cannot be evaluated are discarded. If the remaining candidates do not\0Ashare ODR-equivalent enable_if expressions, the overload resolution is\0Aambiguous. Otherwise, enable_if overload resolution continues with the next\0Aenable_if attribute on the candidates that have not been discarded and have\0Aremaining enable_if attributes. In this way, we pick the most specific\0Aoverload out of a number of viable overloads using enable_if.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void f() __attribute__((enable_if(true, \22\22)));  // #1\0A  void f() __attribute__((enable_if(true, \22\22))) __attribute__((enable_if(true, \22\22)));  // #2\0A\0A  void g(int i, int j) __attribute__((enable_if(i, \22\22)));  // #1\0A  void g(int i, int j) __attribute__((enable_if(j, \22\22))) __attribute__((enable_if(true)));  // #2\0A\0AIn this example, a call to f() is always resolved to #2, as the first enable_if\0Aexpression is ODR-equivalent for both declarations, but #1 does not have another\0Aenable_if expression to continue evaluating, so the next round of evaluation has\0Aonly a single candidate. In a call to g(1, 1), the call is ambiguous even though\0A#2 has more enable_if attributes, because the first enable_if expressions are\0Anot ODR-equivalent.\0A\0AQuery for this feature with ``__has_attribute(enable_if)``.\0A\0ANote that functions with one or more ``enable_if`` attributes may not have\0Atheir address taken, unless all of the conditions specified by said\0A``enable_if`` are constants that evaluate to ``true``. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  const int TrueConstant = 1;\0A  const int FalseConstant = 0;\0A  int f(int a) __attribute__((enable_if(a > 0, \22\22)));\0A  int g(int a) __attribute__((enable_if(a == 0 || a != 0, \22\22)));\0A  int h(int a) __attribute__((enable_if(1, \22\22)));\0A  int i(int a) __attribute__((enable_if(TrueConstant, \22\22)));\0A  int j(int a) __attribute__((enable_if(FalseConstant, \22\22)));\0A\0A  void fn() {\0A    int (*ptr)(int);\0A    ptr = &f; // error: 'a > 0' is not always true\0A    ptr = &g; // error: 'a == 0 || a != 0' is not a truthy constant\0A    ptr = &h; // OK: 1 is a truthy constant\0A    ptr = &i; // OK: 'TrueConstant' is a truthy constant\0A    ptr = &j; // error: 'FalseConstant' is a constant, but not truthy\0A  }\0A\0ABecause ``enable_if`` evaluation happens during overload resolution,\0A``enable_if`` may give unintuitive results when used with templates, depending\0Aon when overloads are resolved. In the example below, clang will emit a\0Adiagnostic about no viable overloads for ``foo`` in ``bar``, but not in ``baz``:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  double foo(int i) __attribute__((enable_if(i > 0, \22\22)));\0A  void *foo(int i) __attribute__((enable_if(i <= 0, \22\22)));\0A  template <int I>\0A  auto bar() { return foo(I); }\0A\0A  template <typename T>\0A  auto baz() { return foo(T::number); }\0A\0A  struct WithNumber { constexpr static int number = 1; };\0A  void callThem() {\0A    bar<sizeof(WithNumber)>();\0A    baz<WithNumber>();\0A  }\0A\0AThis is because, in ``bar``, ``foo`` is resolved prior to template\0Ainstantiation, so the value for ``I`` isn't known (thus, both ``enable_if``\0Aconditions for ``foo`` fail). However, in ``baz``, ``foo`` is resolved during\0Atemplate instantiation, so the value for ``T::number`` is known.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_EnforceTCB = internal constant [544 x i8] c"The ``enforce_tcb`` attribute can be placed on functions to enforce that a\0A  trusted compute base (TCB) does not call out of the TCB. This generates a\0A  warning every time a function not marked with an ``enforce_tcb`` attribute is\0A  called from a function with the ``enforce_tcb`` attribute. A function may be a\0A  part of multiple TCBs. Invocations through function pointers are currently\0A  not checked. Builtins are considered to a part of every TCB.\0A\0A  - ``enforce_tcb(Name)`` indicates that this function is a part of the TCB named ``Name``\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_EnforceTCBLeaf = internal constant [320 x i8] c"The ``enforce_tcb_leaf`` attribute satisfies the requirement enforced by\0A  ``enforce_tcb`` for the marked function to be in the named TCB but does not\0A  continue to check the functions called from within the leaf function.\0A\0A  - ``enforce_tcb_leaf(Name)`` indicates that this function is a part of the TCB named ``Name``\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_EnumExtensibility = internal constant [1446 x i8] c"Attribute ``enum_extensibility`` is used to distinguish between enum definitions\0Athat are extensible and those that are not. The attribute can take either\0A``closed`` or ``open`` as an argument. ``closed`` indicates a variable of the\0Aenum type takes a value that corresponds to one of the enumerators listed in the\0Aenum definition or, when the enum is annotated with ``flag_enum``, a value that\0Acan be constructed using values corresponding to the enumerators. ``open``\0Aindicates a variable of the enum type can take any values allowed by the\0Astandard and instructs clang to be more lenient when issuing warnings.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  enum __attribute__((enum_extensibility(closed))) ClosedEnum {\0A    A0, A1\0A  };\0A\0A  enum __attribute__((enum_extensibility(open))) OpenEnum {\0A    B0, B1\0A  };\0A\0A  enum __attribute__((enum_extensibility(closed),flag_enum)) ClosedFlagEnum {\0A    C0 = 1 << 0, C1 = 1 << 1\0A  };\0A\0A  enum __attribute__((enum_extensibility(open),flag_enum)) OpenFlagEnum {\0A    D0 = 1 << 0, D1 = 1 << 1\0A  };\0A\0A  void foo1() {\0A    enum ClosedEnum ce;\0A    enum OpenEnum oe;\0A    enum ClosedFlagEnum cfe;\0A    enum OpenFlagEnum ofe;\0A\0A    ce = A1;           // no warnings\0A    ce = 100;          // warning issued\0A    oe = B1;           // no warnings\0A    oe = 100;          // no warnings\0A    cfe = C0 | C1;     // no warnings\0A    cfe = C0 | C1 | 4; // warning issued\0A    ofe = D0 | D1;     // no warnings\0A    ofe = D0 | D1 | 4; // no warnings\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_Error = internal constant [655 x i8] c"The ``error`` and ``warning`` function attributes can be used to specify a\0Acustom diagnostic to be emitted when a call to such a function is not\0Aeliminated via optimizations. This can be used to create compile time\0Aassertions that depend on optimizations, while providing diagnostics\0Apointing to precise locations of the call site in the source.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  __attribute__((warning(\22oh no\22))) void dontcall();\0A  void foo() {\0A    if (someCompileTimeAssertionThatsTrue)\0A      dontcall(); // Warning\0A\0A    dontcall(); // Warning\0A\0A    if (someCompileTimeAssertionThatsFalse)\0A      dontcall(); // No Warning\0A    sizeof(dontcall()); // No Warning\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL40AttrDoc_ExcludeFromExplicitInstantiation = internal constant [2601 x i8] c"The ``exclude_from_explicit_instantiation`` attribute opts-out a member of a\0Aclass template from being part of explicit template instantiations of that\0Aclass template. This means that an explicit instantiation will not instantiate\0Amembers of the class template marked with the attribute, but also that code\0Awhere an extern template declaration of the enclosing class template is visible\0Awill not take for granted that an external instantiation of the class template\0Awould provide those members (which would otherwise be a link error, since the\0Aexplicit instantiation won't provide those members). For example, let's say we\0Adon't want the ``data()`` method to be part of libc++'s ABI. To make sure it\0Ais not exported from the dylib, we give it hidden visibility:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    // in <string>\0A    template <class CharT>\0A    class basic_string {\0A    public:\0A      __attribute__((__visibility__(\22hidden\22)))\0A      const value_type* data() const noexcept { ... }\0A    };\0A\0A    template class basic_string<char>;\0A\0ASince an explicit template instantiation declaration for ``basic_string<char>``\0Ais provided, the compiler is free to assume that ``basic_string<char>::data()``\0Awill be provided by another translation unit, and it is free to produce an\0Aexternal call to this function. However, since ``data()`` has hidden visibility\0Aand the explicit template instantiation is provided in a shared library (as\0Aopposed to simply another translation unit), ``basic_string<char>::data()``\0Awon't be found and a link error will ensue. This happens because the compiler\0Aassumes that ``basic_string<char>::data()`` is part of the explicit template\0Ainstantiation declaration, when it really isn't. To tell the compiler that\0A``data()`` is not part of the explicit template instantiation declaration, the\0A``exclude_from_explicit_instantiation`` attribute can be used:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    // in <string>\0A    template <class CharT>\0A    class basic_string {\0A    public:\0A      __attribute__((__visibility__(\22hidden\22)))\0A      __attribute__((exclude_from_explicit_instantiation))\0A      const value_type* data() const noexcept { ... }\0A    };\0A\0A    template class basic_string<char>;\0A\0ANow, the compiler won't assume that ``basic_string<char>::data()`` is provided\0Aexternally despite there being an explicit template instantiation declaration:\0Athe compiler will implicitly instantiate ``basic_string<char>::data()`` in the\0ATUs where it is used.\0A\0AThis attribute can be used on static and non-static member functions of class\0Atemplates, static data members of class templates and member classes of class\0Atemplates.\00", align 16
@_ZL32AttrDoc_ExclusiveTrylockFunction = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_ExplicitInit = internal constant [1574 x i8] c"The ``clang::require_explicit_initialization`` attribute indicates that a\0Afield of an aggregate must be initialized explicitly by the user when an object\0Aof the aggregate type is constructed. The attribute supports both C and C++,\0Abut its usage is invalid on non-aggregates.\0A\0ANote that this attribute is *not* a memory safety feature, and is *not* intended\0Ato guard against use of uninitialized memory.\0A\0ARather, it is intended for use in \22parameter-objects\22, used to simulate,\0Afor example, the passing of named parameters.\0AThe attribute generates a warning when explicit initializers for such\0Avariables are not provided (this occurs regardless of whether any in-class field\0Ainitializers exist):\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  struct Buffer {\0A    void *address [[clang::require_explicit_initialization]];\0A    size_t length [[clang::require_explicit_initialization]] = 0;\0A  };\0A\0A  struct ArrayIOParams {\0A    size_t count [[clang::require_explicit_initialization]];\0A    size_t element_size [[clang::require_explicit_initialization]];\0A    int flags = 0;\0A  };\0A\0A  size_t ReadArray(FILE *file, struct Buffer buffer,\0A                   struct ArrayIOParams params);\0A\0A  int main() {\0A    unsigned int buf[512];\0A    ReadArray(stdin, {\0A      buf\0A      // warning: field 'length' is not explicitly initialized\0A    }, {\0A      .count = sizeof(buf) / sizeof(*buf),\0A      // warning: field 'element_size' is not explicitly initialized\0A      // (Note that a missing initializer for 'flags' is not diagnosed, because\0A      // the field is not marked as requiring explicit initialization.)\0A    });\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_ExternalSourceSymbol = internal constant [2977 x i8] c"The ``external_source_symbol`` attribute specifies that a declaration originates\0Afrom an external source and describes the nature of that source.\0A\0AThe fact that Clang is capable of recognizing declarations that were defined\0Aexternally can be used to provide better tooling support for mixed-language\0Aprojects or projects that rely on auto-generated code. For instance, an IDE that\0Auses Clang and that supports mixed-language projects can use this attribute to\0Aprovide a correct 'jump-to-definition' feature. For a concrete example,\0Aconsider a protocol that's defined in a Swift file:\0A\0A.. code-block:: swift\0A\0A  @objc public protocol SwiftProtocol {\0A    func method()\0A  }\0A\0AThis protocol can be used from Objective-C code by including a header file that\0Awas generated by the Swift compiler. The declarations in that header can use\0Athe ``external_source_symbol`` attribute to make Clang aware of the fact\0Athat ``SwiftProtocol`` actually originates from a Swift module:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  __attribute__((external_source_symbol(language=\22Swift\22,defined_in=\22module\22)))\0A  @protocol SwiftProtocol\0A  @required\0A  - (void) method;\0A  @end\0A\0AConsequently, when 'jump-to-definition' is performed at a location that\0Areferences ``SwiftProtocol``, the IDE can jump to the original definition in\0Athe Swift source file rather than jumping to the Objective-C declaration in the\0Aauto-generated header file.\0A\0AThe ``external_source_symbol`` attribute is a comma-separated list that includes\0Aclauses that describe the origin and the nature of the particular declaration.\0AThose clauses can be:\0A\0Alanguage=\\ *string-literal*\0A  The name of the source language in which this declaration was defined.\0A\0Adefined_in=\\ *string-literal*\0A  The name of the source container in which the declaration was defined. The\0A  exact definition of source container is language-specific, e.g. Swift's\0A  source containers are modules, so ``defined_in`` should specify the Swift\0A  module name.\0A\0AUSR=\\ *string-literal*\0A  String that specifies a unified symbol resolution (USR) value for this\0A  declaration. USR string uniquely identifies this particular declaration, and\0A  is typically used when constructing an index of a codebase.\0A  The USR value in this attribute is expected to be generated by an external\0A  compiler that compiled the native declaration using its original source\0A  language. The exact format of the USR string and its other attributes\0A  are determined by the specification of this declaration's source language.\0A  When not specified, Clang's indexer will use the Clang USR for this symbol.\0A  User can query to see if Clang supports the use of the ``USR`` clause in\0A  the ``external_source_symbol`` attribute with\0A  ``__has_attribute(external_source_symbol) >= 20230206``.\0A\0Agenerated_declaration\0A  This declaration was automatically generated by some tool.\0A\0AThe clauses can be specified in any order. The clauses that are listed above are\0Aall optional, but the attribute has to have at least one clause.\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_Final = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL16AttrDoc_FlagEnum = internal constant [290 x i8] c"This attribute can be added to an enumerator to signal to the compiler that it\0Ais intended to be used as a flag type. This will cause the compiler to assume\0Athat the range of the type includes all of the values that you can get by\0Amanipulating bits of the enumerator when issuing warnings.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_Flatten = internal constant [220 x i8] c"The ``flatten`` attribute causes calls within the attributed function to\0Abe inlined unless it is impossible to do so, for example if the body of the\0Acallee is unavailable or if the callee has the ``noinline`` attribute.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Format = internal constant [3497 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``format`` attribute, which indicates that the function\0Aaccepts (among other possibilities) a ``printf`` or ``scanf``-like format string\0Aand corresponding arguments or a ``va_list`` that contains these arguments.\0A\0APlease see `GCC documentation about format attribute\0A<http://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Function-Attributes.html>`_ to find details\0Aabout attribute syntax.\0A\0AClang implements two kinds of checks with this attribute.\0A\0A#. Clang checks that the function with the ``format`` attribute is called with\0A   a format string that uses format specifiers that are allowed, and that\0A   arguments match the format string. This is the ``-Wformat`` warning, it is\0A   on by default.\0A\0A#. Clang checks that the format string argument is a literal string. This is\0A   the ``-Wformat-nonliteral`` warning, it is off by default.\0A\0A   Clang implements this mostly the same way as GCC, but there is a difference\0A   for functions that accept a ``va_list`` argument (for example, ``vprintf``).\0A   GCC does not emit ``-Wformat-nonliteral`` warning for calls to such\0A   functions. Clang does not warn if the format string comes from a function\0A   parameter, where the function is annotated with a compatible attribute,\0A   otherwise it warns. For example:\0A\0A   .. code-block:: c\0A\0A     __attribute__((__format__ (__scanf__, 1, 3)))\0A     void foo(const char* s, char *buf, ...) {\0A       va_list ap;\0A       va_start(ap, buf);\0A\0A       vprintf(s, ap); // warning: format string is not a string literal\0A     }\0A\0A   In this case we warn because ``s`` contains a format string for a\0A   ``scanf``-like function, but it is passed to a ``printf``-like function.\0A\0A   If the attribute is removed, clang still warns, because the format string is\0A   not a string literal.\0A\0A   Another example:\0A\0A   .. code-block:: c\0A\0A     __attribute__((__format__ (__printf__, 1, 3)))\0A     void foo(const char* s, char *buf, ...) {\0A       va_list ap;\0A       va_start(ap, buf);\0A\0A       vprintf(s, ap); // warning\0A     }\0A\0A   In this case Clang does not warn because the format string ``s`` and\0A   the corresponding arguments are annotated. If the arguments are\0A   incorrect, the caller of ``foo`` will receive a warning.\0A\0AAs an extension to GCC's behavior, Clang accepts the ``format`` attribute on\0Anon-variadic functions. Clang checks non-variadic format functions for the same\0Aclasses of issues that can be found on variadic functions, as controlled by the\0Asame warning flags, except that the types of formatted arguments is forced by\0Athe function signature. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  __attribute__((__format__(__printf__, 1, 2)))\0A  void fmt(const char *s, const char *a, int b);\0A\0A  void bar(void) {\0A    fmt(\22%s %i\22, \22hello\22, 123); // OK\0A    fmt(\22%i %g\22, \22hello\22, 123); // warning: arguments don't match format\0A    extern const char *fmt;\0A    fmt(fmt, \22hello\22, 123); // warning: format string is not a string literal\0A  }\0A\0AWhen using the format attribute on a variadic function, the first data parameter\0A_must_ be the index of the ellipsis in the parameter list. Clang will generate\0Aa diagnostic otherwise, as it wouldn't be possible to forward that argument list\0Ato `printf`-family functions. For instance, this is an error:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  __attribute__((__format__(__printf__, 1, 2)))\0A  void fmt(const char *s, int b, ...);\0A  // ^ error: format attribute parameter 3 is out of bounds\0A  // (must be __printf__, 1, 3)\0A\0AUsing the ``format`` attribute on a non-variadic function emits a GCC\0Acompatibility diagnostic.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_FormatArg = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_FunctionReturnThunks = internal constant [777 x i8] c"The attribute ``function_return`` can replace return instructions with jumps to\0Atarget-specific symbols. This attribute supports 2 possible values,\0Acorresponding to the values supported by the ``-mfunction-return=`` command\0Aline flag:\0A\0A* ``__attribute__((function_return(\22keep\22)))`` to disable related transforms.\0A  This is useful for undoing global setting from ``-mfunction-return=`` locally\0A  for individual functions.\0A* ``__attribute__((function_return(\22thunk-extern\22)))`` to replace returns with\0A  jumps, while NOT emitting the thunk.\0A\0AThe values ``thunk`` and ``thunk-inline`` from GCC are not supported.\0A\0AThe symbol used for ``thunk-extern`` is target specific:\0A* X86: ``__x86_return_thunk``\0A\0AAs such, this function attribute is currently only supported on X86 targets.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_GNUInline = internal constant [1609 x i8] c"The ``gnu_inline`` changes the meaning of ``extern inline`` to use GNU inline\0Asemantics, meaning:\0A\0A* If any declaration that is declared ``inline`` is not declared ``extern``,\0A  then the ``inline`` keyword is just a hint. In particular, an out-of-line\0A  definition is still emitted for a function with external linkage, even if all\0A  call sites are inlined, unlike in C99 and C++ inline semantics.\0A\0A* If all declarations that are declared ``inline`` are also declared\0A  ``extern``, then the function body is present only for inlining and no\0A  out-of-line version is emitted.\0A\0ASome important consequences: ``static inline`` emits an out-of-line\0Aversion if needed, a plain ``inline`` definition emits an out-of-line version\0Aalways, and an ``extern inline`` definition (in a header) followed by a\0A(non-``extern``) ``inline`` declaration in a source file emits an out-of-line\0Aversion of the function in that source file but provides the function body for\0Ainlining to all includers of the header.\0A\0AEither ``__GNUC_GNU_INLINE__`` (GNU inline semantics) or\0A``__GNUC_STDC_INLINE__`` (C99 semantics) will be defined (they are mutually\0Aexclusive). If ``__GNUC_STDC_INLINE__`` is defined, then the ``gnu_inline``\0Afunction attribute can be used to get GNU inline semantics on a per function\0Abasis. If ``__GNUC_GNU_INLINE__`` is defined, then the translation unit is\0Aalready being compiled with GNU inline semantics as the implied default. It is\0Aunspecified which macro is defined in a C++ compilation.\0A\0AGNU inline semantics are the default behavior with ``-std=gnu89``,\0A``-std=c89``, ``-std=c94``, or ``-fgnu89-inline``.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_GuardedBy = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_GuardedVar = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_HIPManaged = internal constant [411 x i8] c"The ``__managed__`` attribute can be applied to a global variable declaration in HIP.\0AA managed variable is emitted as an undefined global symbol in the device binary and is\0Aregistered by ``__hipRegisterManagedVariable`` in init functions. The HIP runtime allocates\0Amanaged memory and uses it to define the symbol when loading the device binary.\0AA managed variable can be accessed in both device and host code.\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_HLSLNumThreads = internal constant [359 x i8] c"The ``numthreads`` attribute applies to HLSL shaders where explcit thread counts\0Aare required. The ``X``, ``Y``, and ``Z`` values provided to the attribute\0Adictate the thread id. Total number of threads executed is ``X * Y * Z``.\0A\0AThe full documentation is available here: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3dhlsl/sm5-attributes-numthreads\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_HLSLResource = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL27AttrDoc_HLSLResourceBinding = internal constant [852 x i8] c"The resource binding attribute sets the virtual register and logical register space for a resource.\0AAttribute spelling in HLSL is: ``register(slot [, space])``.\0A``slot`` takes the format ``[type][number]``,\0Awhere ``type`` is a single character specifying the resource type and ``number`` is the virtual register number.\0A\0ARegister types are:\0At for shader resource views (SRV),\0As for samplers,\0Au for unordered access views (UAV),\0Ab for constant buffer views (CBV).\0A\0ARegister space is specified in the format ``space[number]`` and defaults to ``space0`` if omitted.\0AHere're resource binding examples with and without space:\0A\0A.. code-block:: hlsl\0A\0A  RWBuffer<float> Uav : register(u3, space1);\0A  Buffer<float> Buf : register(t1);\0A\0AThe full documentation is available here: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3d12/resource-binding-in-hlsl\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_HLSLShader = internal constant [719 x i8] c"The ``shader`` type attribute applies to HLSL shader entry functions to\0Aidentify the shader type for the entry function.\0AThe syntax is:\0A\0A.. code-block:: text\0A\0A  ``[shader(string-literal)]``\0A\0Awhere the string literal is one of: \22pixel\22, \22vertex\22, \22geometry\22, \22hull\22,\0A\22domain\22, \22compute\22, \22raygeneration\22, \22intersection\22, \22anyhit\22, \22closesthit\22,\0A\22miss\22, \22callable\22, \22mesh\22, \22amplification\22. Normally the shader type is set\0Aby shader target with the ``-T`` option like ``-Tps_6_1``. When compiling to a\0Alibrary target like ``lib_6_3``, the shader type attribute can help the\0Acompiler to identify the shader type. It is mostly used by Raytracing shaders\0Awhere shaders must be compiled into a library and linked at runtime.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_HLSLWaveSize = internal constant [1275 x i8] c"The ``WaveSize`` attribute specify a wave size on a shader entry point in order\0Ato indicate either that a shader depends on or strongly prefers a specific wave\0Asize.\0AThere're 2 versions of the attribute: ``WaveSize`` and ``RangedWaveSize``.\0AThe syntax for ``WaveSize`` is:\0A\0A.. code-block:: text\0A\0A  ``[WaveSize(<numLanes>)]``\0A\0AThe allowed wave sizes that an HLSL shader may specify are the powers of 2\0Abetween 4 and 128, inclusive.\0AIn other words, the set: [4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128].\0A\0AThe syntax for ``RangedWaveSize`` is:\0A\0A.. code-block:: text\0A\0A  ``[WaveSize(<minWaveSize>, <maxWaveSize>, [prefWaveSize])]``\0A\0AWhere minWaveSize is the minimum wave size supported by the shader representing\0Athe beginning of the allowed range, maxWaveSize is the maximum wave size\0Asupported by the shader representing the end of the allowed range, and\0AprefWaveSize is the optional preferred wave size representing the size expected\0Ato be the most optimal for this shader.\0A\0A``WaveSize`` is available for HLSL shader model 6.6 and later.\0A``RangedWaveSize`` available for HLSL shader model 6.8 and later.\0A\0AThe full documentation is available here: https://microsoft.github.io/DirectX-Specs/d3d/HLSL_SM_6_6_WaveSize.html\0Aand https://microsoft.github.io/hlsl-specs/proposals/0013-wave-size-range.html\00", align 16
@_ZL11AttrDoc_Hot = internal constant [239 x i8] c"``__attribute__((hot))`` marks a function as hot, as a manual alternative to PGO hotness data.\0AIf PGO data is available, the annotation ``__attribute__((hot))`` overrides the profile count based hotness (unlike ``__attribute__((cold))``).\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_HybridPatchable = internal constant [246 x i8] c"The ``hybrid_patchable`` attribute declares an ARM64EC function with an additional\0Ax86-64 thunk, which may be patched at runtime.\0A\0AFor more information see\0A`ARM64EC ABI documentation <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/arm/arm64ec-abi>`_.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_IBAction = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_IBOutlet = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL26AttrDoc_IBOutletCollection = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_InitPriority = internal constant [1589 x i8] c"In C++, the order in which global variables are initialized across translation\0Aunits is unspecified, unlike the ordering within a single translation unit. The\0A``init_priority`` attribute allows you to specify a relative ordering for the\0Ainitialization of objects declared at namespace scope in C++ within a single\0Alinked image on supported platforms. The priority is given as an integer constant\0Aexpression between 101 and 65535 (inclusive). Priorities outside of that range are\0Areserved for use by the implementation. A lower value indicates a higher priority\0Aof initialization. Note that only the relative ordering of values is important.\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  struct SomeType { SomeType(); };\0A  __attribute__((init_priority(200))) SomeType Obj1;\0A  __attribute__((init_priority(101))) SomeType Obj2;\0A\0A``Obj2`` will be initialized *before* ``Obj1`` despite the usual order of\0Ainitialization being the opposite.\0A\0ANote that this attribute does not control the initialization order of objects\0Aacross final linked image boundaries like shared objects and executables.\0A\0AOn Windows, ``init_seg(compiler)`` is represented with a priority of 200 and\0A``init_seg(library)`` is represented with a priority of 400. ``init_seg(user)``\0Auses the default 65535 priority.\0A\0AOn MachO platforms, this attribute also does not control the order of initialization\0Aacross translation units, where it only affects the order within a single TU.\0A\0AThis attribute is only supported for C++ and Objective-C++ and is ignored in\0Aother language modes. Currently, this attribute is not implemented on z/OS.\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_InternalLinkage = internal constant [400 x i8] c"The ``internal_linkage`` attribute changes the linkage type of the declaration\0Ato internal. This is similar to C-style ``static``, but can be used on classes\0Aand class methods. When applied to a class definition, this attribute affects\0Aall methods and static data members of that class. This can be used to contain\0Athe ABI of a C++ library by excluding unwanted class methods from the export\0Atables.\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_LTOVisibilityPublic = internal constant [26 x i8] c"See :doc:`LTOVisibility`.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_LayoutVersion = internal constant [239 x i8] c"The layout_version attribute requests that the compiler utilize the class\0Alayout rules of a particular compiler version.\0AThis attribute only applies to struct, class, and union types.\0AIt is only supported when using the Microsoft C++ ABI.\00", align 16
@_ZL12AttrDoc_Leaf = internal constant [542 x i8] c"The ``leaf`` attribute is used as a compiler hint to improve dataflow analysis\0Ain library functions. Functions marked with the ``leaf`` attribute are not allowed\0Ato jump back into the caller's translation unit, whether through invoking a\0Acallback function, an external function call, use of ``longjmp``, or other means.\0ATherefore, they cannot use or modify any data that does not escape the caller function's\0Acompilation unit.\0A\0AFor more information see\0A`gcc documentation <https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Common-Function-Attributes.html>`\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_LockReturned = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_LocksExcluded = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_M68kInterrupt = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_MIGServerRoutine = internal constant [1153 x i8] c"The Mach Interface Generator release-on-success convention dictates\0Afunctions that follow it to only release arguments passed to them when they\0Areturn \22success\22 (a ``kern_return_t`` error code that indicates that\0Ano errors have occurred). Otherwise the release is performed by the MIG client\0Athat called the function. The annotation ``__attribute__((mig_server_routine))``\0Ais applied in order to specify which functions are expected to follow the\0Aconvention. This allows the Static Analyzer to find bugs caused by violations of\0Athat convention. The attribute would normally appear on the forward declaration\0Aof the actual server routine in the MIG server header, but it may also be\0Aadded to arbitrary functions that need to follow the same convention - for\0Aexample, a user can add them to auxiliary functions called by the server routine\0Athat have their return value of type ``kern_return_t`` unconditionally returned\0Afrom the routine. The attribute can be applied to C++ methods, and in this case\0Ait will be automatically applied to overrides if the method is virtual. The\0Aattribute can also be written using C++11 syntax: ``[[mig::server_routine]]``.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_MSAllocator = internal constant [654 x i8] c"The ``__declspec(allocator)`` attribute is applied to functions that allocate\0Amemory, such as operator new in C++. When CodeView debug information is emitted\0A(enabled by ``clang -gcodeview`` or ``clang-cl /Z7``), Clang will attempt to\0Arecord the code offset of heap allocation call sites in the debug info. It will\0Aalso record the type being allocated using some local heuristics. The Visual\0AStudio debugger uses this information to `profile memory usage`_.\0A\0A.. _profile memory usage: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/profiling/memory-usage\0A\0AThis attribute does not affect optimizations in any way, unlike GCC's\0A``__attribute__((malloc))``.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_MSConstexpr = internal constant [571 x i8] c"The ``[[msvc::constexpr]]`` attribute can be applied only to a function\0Adefinition or a ``return`` statement. It does not impact function declarations.\0AA ``[[msvc::constexpr]]`` function cannot be ``constexpr`` or ``consteval``.\0AA ``[[msvc::constexpr]]`` function is treated as if it were a ``constexpr`` function\0Awhen it is evaluated in a constant context of ``[[msvc::constexpr]] return`` statement.\0AOtherwise, it is treated as a regular function.\0A\0ASemantics of this attribute are enabled only under MSVC compatibility\0A(``-fms-compatibility-version``) 19.33 and later.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_MSInheritance = internal constant [1548 x i8] c"This collection of keywords is enabled under ``-fms-extensions`` and controls\0Athe pointer-to-member representation used on ``*-*-win32`` targets.\0A\0AThe ``*-*-win32`` targets utilize a pointer-to-member representation which\0Avaries in size and alignment depending on the definition of the underlying\0Aclass.\0A\0AHowever, this is problematic when a forward declaration is only available and\0Ano definition has been made yet. In such cases, Clang is forced to utilize the\0Amost general representation that is available to it.\0A\0AThese keywords make it possible to use a pointer-to-member representation other\0Athan the most general one regardless of whether or not the definition will ever\0Abe present in the current translation unit.\0A\0AThis family of keywords belong between the ``class-key`` and ``class-name``:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  struct __single_inheritance S;\0A  int S::*i;\0A  struct S {};\0A\0AThis keyword can be applied to class templates but only has an effect when used\0Aon full specializations:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  template <typename T, typename U> struct __single_inheritance A; // warning: inheritance model ignored on primary template\0A  template <typename T> struct __multiple_inheritance A<T, T>; // warning: inheritance model ignored on partial specialization\0A  template <> struct __single_inheritance A<int, float>;\0A\0ANote that choosing an inheritance model less general than strictly necessary is\0Aan error:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  struct __multiple_inheritance S; // error: inheritance model does not match definition\0A  int S::*i;\0A  struct S {};\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_MSNoVTable = internal constant [227 x i8] c"This attribute can be added to a class declaration or definition to signal to\0Athe compiler that constructors and destructors will not reference the virtual\0Afunction table. It is only supported when using the Microsoft C++ ABI.\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_MSP430Interrupt = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_MSStruct = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_MSVtorDisp = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL25AttrDoc_MaxFieldAlignment = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL16AttrDoc_MayAlias = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_MaybeUndef = internal constant [807 x i8] c"The ``maybe_undef`` attribute can be placed on a function parameter. It indicates\0Athat the parameter is allowed to use undef values. It informs the compiler\0Ato insert a freeze LLVM IR instruction on the function parameter.\0APlease note that this is an attribute that is used as an internal\0Aimplementation detail and not intended to be used by external users.\0A\0AIn languages HIP, CUDA etc., some functions have multi-threaded semantics and\0Ait is enough for only one or some threads to provide defined arguments.\0ADepending on semantics, undef arguments in some threads don't produce\0Aundefined results in the function call. Since, these functions accept undefined\0Aarguments, ``maybe_undef`` attribute can be placed.\0A\0ASample usage:\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void maybeundeffunc(int __attribute__((maybe_undef))param);\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_MicroMips = internal constant [373 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((micromips))`` and\0A``__attribute__((nomicromips))`` attributes on MIPS targets. These attributes\0Amay be attached to a function definition and instructs the backend to generate\0Aor not to generate microMIPS code for that function.\0A\0AThese attributes override the ``-mmicromips`` and ``-mno-micromips`` options\0Aon the command line.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_MinSize = internal constant [259 x i8] c"This function attribute indicates that optimization passes and code generator passes\0Amake choices that keep the function code size as small as possible. Optimizations may\0Aalso sacrifice runtime performance in order to minimize the size of the generated code.\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_MinVectorWidth = internal constant [1249 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((min_vector_width(width)))`` attribute. This\0Aattribute may be attached to a function and informs the backend that this\0Afunction desires vectors of at least this width to be generated. Target-specific\0Amaximum vector widths still apply. This means even if you ask for something\0Alarger than the target supports, you will only get what the target supports.\0AThis attribute is meant to be a hint to control target heuristics that may\0Agenerate narrower vectors than what the target hardware supports.\0A\0AThis is currently used by the X86 target to allow some CPUs that support 512-bit\0Avectors to be limited to using 256-bit vectors to avoid frequency penalties.\0AThis is currently enabled with the ``-prefer-vector-width=256`` command line\0Aoption. The ``min_vector_width`` attribute can be used to prevent the backend\0Afrom trying to split vector operations to match the ``prefer-vector-width``. All\0AX86 vector intrinsics from x86intrin.h already set this attribute. Additionally,\0Ause of any of the X86-specific vector builtins will implicitly set this\0Aattribute on the calling function. The intent is that explicitly writing vector\0Acode using the X86 intrinsics will prevent ``prefer-vector-width`` from\0Aaffecting the code.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Mips16 = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_MipsInterrupt = internal constant [1692 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((interrupt(\22ARGUMENT\22)))`` attribute on\0AMIPS targets. This attribute may be attached to a function definition and instructs\0Athe backend to generate appropriate function entry/exit code so that it can be used\0Adirectly as an interrupt service routine.\0A\0ABy default, the compiler will produce a function prologue and epilogue suitable for\0Aan interrupt service routine that handles an External Interrupt Controller (eic)\0Agenerated interrupt. This behavior can be explicitly requested with the \22eic\22\0Aargument.\0A\0AOtherwise, for use with vectored interrupt mode, the argument passed should be\0Aof the form \22vector=LEVEL\22 where LEVEL is one of the following values:\0A\22sw0\22, \22sw1\22, \22hw0\22, \22hw1\22, \22hw2\22, \22hw3\22, \22hw4\22, \22hw5\22. The compiler will\0Athen set the interrupt mask to the corresponding level which will mask all\0Ainterrupts up to and including the argument.\0A\0AThe semantics are as follows:\0A\0A- The prologue is modified so that the Exception Program Counter (EPC) and\0A  Status coprocessor registers are saved to the stack. The interrupt mask is\0A  set so that the function can only be interrupted by a higher priority\0A  interrupt. The epilogue will restore the previous values of EPC and Status.\0A\0A- The prologue and epilogue are modified to save and restore all non-kernel\0A  registers as necessary.\0A\0A- The FPU is disabled in the prologue, as the floating pointer registers are not\0A  spilled to the stack.\0A\0A- The function return sequence is changed to use an exception return instruction.\0A\0A- The parameter sets the interrupt mask for the function corresponding to the\0A  interrupt level specified. If no mask is specified the interrupt mask\0A  defaults to \22eic\22.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_MipsLongCall = internal constant [809 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((long_call))``, ``__attribute__((far))``,\0Aand ``__attribute__((near))`` attributes on MIPS targets. These attributes may\0Aonly be added to function declarations and change the code generated\0Aby the compiler when directly calling the function. The ``near`` attribute\0Aallows calls to the function to be made using the ``jal`` instruction, which\0Arequires the function to be located in the same naturally aligned 256MB\0Asegment as the caller. The ``long_call`` and ``far`` attributes are synonyms\0Aand require the use of a different call sequence that works regardless\0Aof the distance between the functions.\0A\0AThese attributes have no effect for position-independent code.\0A\0AThese attributes take priority over command line switches such\0Aas ``-mlong-calls`` and ``-mno-long-calls``.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_MipsShortCall = internal constant [879 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((long_call))``, ``__attribute__((far))``,\0A``__attribute__((short__call))``, and ``__attribute__((near))`` attributes\0Aon MIPS targets. These attributes may only be added to function declarations\0Aand change the code generated by the compiler when directly calling\0Athe function. The ``short_call`` and ``near`` attributes are synonyms and\0Aallow calls to the function to be made using the ``jal`` instruction, which\0Arequires the function to be located in the same naturally aligned 256MB segment\0Aas the caller. The ``long_call`` and ``far`` attributes are synonyms and\0Arequire the use of a different call sequence that works regardless\0Aof the distance between the functions.\0A\0AThese attributes have no effect for position-independent code.\0A\0AThese attributes take priority over command line switches such\0Aas ``-mlong-calls`` and ``-mno-long-calls``.\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_NSConsumesSelf = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_NSErrorDomain = internal constant [1033 x i8] c"In Cocoa frameworks in Objective-C, one can group related error codes in enums\0Aand categorize these enums with error domains.\0A\0AThe ``ns_error_domain`` attribute indicates a global ``NSString`` or\0A``CFString`` constant representing the error domain that an error code belongs\0Ato. For pointer uniqueness and code size this is a constant symbol, not a\0Aliteral.\0A\0AThe domain and error code need to be used together. The ``ns_error_domain``\0Aattribute links error codes to their domain at the source level.\0A\0AThis metadata is useful for documentation purposes, for static analysis, and for\0Aimproving interoperability between Objective-C and Swift. It is not used for\0Acode generation in Objective-C.\0A\0AFor example:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: objc\0A\0A    #define NS_ERROR_ENUM(_type, _name, _domain)  \\\0A      enum _name : _type _name; enum __attribute__((ns_error_domain(_domain))) _name : _type\0A\0A    extern NSString *const MyErrorDomain;\0A    typedef NS_ERROR_ENUM(unsigned char, MyErrorEnum, MyErrorDomain) {\0A      MyErrFirst,\0A      MyErrSecond,\0A    };\00", align 16
@_ZL29AttrDoc_NSReturnsAutoreleased = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_NSReturnsNotRetained = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_NVPTXKernel = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_Naked = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_NoAlias = internal constant [183 x i8] c"The ``noalias`` attribute indicates that the only memory accesses inside\0Afunction are loads and stores from objects pointed to by its pointer-typed\0Aarguments, with arbitrary offsets.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_NoCommon = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_NoConvergent = internal constant [875 x i8] c"This attribute prevents a function from being treated as convergent, which\0Ameans that optimizations can only move calls to that function to\0Acontrol-equivalent blocks. If a statement is marked as ``noconvergent`` and\0Acontains calls, it also prevents those calls from being treated as convergent.\0AIn other words, those calls are not restricted to only being moved to\0Acontrol-equivalent blocks.\0A\0AIn languages following SPMD/SIMT programming model, e.g., CUDA/HIP, function\0Adeclarations and calls are treated as convergent by default for correctness.\0AThis ``noconvergent`` attribute is helpful for developers to prevent them from\0Abeing treated as convergent when it's safe.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  __device__ float bar(float);\0A  __device__ float foo(float) __attribute__((noconvergent)) {}\0A\0A  __device__ int example(void) {\0A    float x;\0A    [[clang::noconvergent]] x = bar(x);\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_NoDebug = internal constant [204 x i8] c"The ``nodebug`` attribute allows you to suppress debugging information for a\0Afunction or method, for a variable that is not a parameter or a non-static\0Adata member, or for a typedef or using declaration.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_NoDestroy = internal constant [1336 x i8] c"The ``no_destroy`` attribute specifies that a variable with static or thread\0Astorage duration shouldn't have its exit-time destructor run. Annotating every\0Astatic and thread duration variable with this attribute is equivalent to\0Ainvoking clang with -fno-c++-static-destructors.\0A\0AIf a variable is declared with this attribute, clang doesn't access check or\0Agenerate the type's destructor. If you have a type that you only want to be\0Aannotated with ``no_destroy``, you can therefore declare the destructor private:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  struct only_no_destroy {\0A    only_no_destroy();\0A  private:\0A    ~only_no_destroy();\0A  };\0A\0A  [[clang::no_destroy]] only_no_destroy global; // fine!\0A\0ANote that destructors are still required for subobjects of aggregates annotated\0Awith this attribute. This is because previously constructed subobjects need to\0Abe destroyed if an exception gets thrown before the initialization of the\0Acomplete object is complete. For instance:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void f() {\0A    try {\0A      [[clang::no_destroy]]\0A      static only_no_destroy array[10]; // error, only_no_destroy has a private destructor.\0A    } catch (...) {\0A      // Handle the error\0A    }\0A  }\0A\0AHere, if the construction of ``array[9]`` fails with an exception, ``array[0..8]``\0Awill be destroyed, so the element's destructor needs to be accessible.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_NoDuplicate = internal constant [1034 x i8] c"The ``noduplicate`` attribute can be placed on function declarations to control\0Awhether function calls to this function can be duplicated or not as a result of\0Aoptimizations. This is required for the implementation of functions with\0Acertain special requirements, like the OpenCL \22barrier\22 function, that might\0Aneed to be run concurrently by all the threads that are executing in lockstep\0Aon the hardware. For example this attribute applied on the function\0A\22nodupfunc\22 in the code below avoids that:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void nodupfunc() __attribute__((noduplicate));\0A  // Setting it as a C++11 attribute is also valid\0A  // void nodupfunc() [[clang::noduplicate]];\0A  void foo();\0A  void bar();\0A\0A  nodupfunc();\0A  if (a > n) {\0A    foo();\0A  } else {\0A    bar();\0A  }\0A\0Agets possibly modified by some optimizations into code similar to this:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  if (a > n) {\0A    nodupfunc();\0A    foo();\0A  } else {\0A    nodupfunc();\0A    bar();\0A  }\0A\0Awhere the call to \22nodupfunc\22 is duplicated and sunk into the two branches\0Aof the condition.\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_NoInstrumentFunction = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_NoMicroMips = internal constant [373 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((micromips))`` and\0A``__attribute__((nomicromips))`` attributes on MIPS targets. These attributes\0Amay be attached to a function definition and instructs the backend to generate\0Aor not to generate microMIPS code for that function.\0A\0AThese attributes override the ``-mmicromips`` and ``-mno-micromips`` options\0Aon the command line.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_NoMips16 = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_NoProfileFunction = internal constant [306 x i8] c"Use the ``no_profile_instrument_function`` attribute on a function declaration\0Ato denote that the compiler should not instrument the function with\0Aprofile-related instrumentation, such as via the\0A``-fprofile-generate`` / ``-fprofile-instr-generate`` /\0A``-fcs-profile-generate`` / ``-fprofile-arcs`` flags.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_NoRandomizeLayout = internal constant [961 x i8] c"The attribute ``randomize_layout``, when attached to a C structure, selects it\0Afor structure layout field randomization; a compile-time hardening technique. A\0A\22seed\22 value, is specified via the ``-frandomize-layout-seed=`` command line flag.\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: bash\0A\0A  SEED=`od -A n -t x8 -N 32 /dev/urandom | tr -d ' \\n'`\0A  make ... CFLAGS=\22-frandomize-layout-seed=$SEED\22 ...\0A\0AYou can also supply the seed in a file with ``-frandomize-layout-seed-file=``.\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: bash\0A\0A  od -A n -t x8 -N 32 /dev/urandom | tr -d ' \\n' > /tmp/seed_file.txt\0A  make ... CFLAGS=\22-frandomize-layout-seed-file=/tmp/seed_file.txt\22 ...\0A\0AThe randomization is deterministic based for a given seed, so the entire\0Aprogram should be compiled with the same seed, but keep the seed safe\0Aotherwise.\0A\0AThe attribute ``no_randomize_layout``, when attached to a C structure,\0Ainstructs the compiler that this structure should not have its field layout\0Arandomized.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_NoReturn = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_NoSanitize = internal constant [776 x i8] c"Use the ``no_sanitize`` attribute on a function or a global variable\0Adeclaration to specify that a particular instrumentation or set of\0Ainstrumentations should not be applied.\0A\0AThe attribute takes a list of string literals with the following accepted\0Avalues:\0A* all values accepted by ``-fno-sanitize=``;\0A* ``coverage``, to disable SanitizerCoverage instrumentation.\0A\0AFor example, ``__attribute__((no_sanitize(\22address\22, \22thread\22)))`` specifies\0Athat AddressSanitizer and ThreadSanitizer should not be applied to the function\0Aor variable. Using ``__attribute__((no_sanitize(\22coverage\22)))`` specifies that\0ASanitizerCoverage should not be applied to the function.\0A\0ASee :ref:`Controlling Code Generation <controlling-code-generation>` for a\0Afull list of supported sanitizer flags.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_NoSpecializations = internal constant [233 x i8] c"``[[clang::no_specializations]]`` can be applied to function, class, or variable\0Atemplates which should not be explicitly specialized by users. This is primarily\0Aused to diagnose user specializations of standard library type traits.\00", align 16
@_ZL34AttrDoc_NoSpeculativeLoadHardening = internal constant [1421 x i8] c"This attribute can be applied to a function declaration in order to indicate\0A  that `Speculative Load Hardening <https://llvm.org/docs/SpeculativeLoadHardening.html>`_\0A  is *not* needed for the function body. This can also be applied to a method\0A  in Objective C. This attribute will take precedence over the command line flag in\0A  the case where `-mspeculative-load-hardening <https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangCommandLineReference.html#cmdoption-clang-mspeculative-load-hardening>`_ is specified.\0A\0A  Warning: This attribute may not prevent Speculative Load Hardening from being\0A  enabled for a function which inlines a function that has the\0A  'speculative_load_hardening' attribute. This is intended to provide a\0A  maximally conservative model where the code that is marked with the\0A  'speculative_load_hardening' attribute will always (even when inlined)\0A  be hardened. A user of this attribute may want to mark functions called by\0A  a function they do not want to be hardened with the 'noinline' attribute.\0A\0A  For example:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    __attribute__((speculative_load_hardening))\0A    int foo(int i) {\0A      return i;\0A    }\0A\0A    // Note: bar() may still have speculative load hardening enabled if\0A    // foo() is inlined into bar(). Mark foo() with __attribute__((noinline))\0A    // to avoid this situation.\0A    __attribute__((no_speculative_load_hardening))\0A    int bar(int i) {\0A      return foo(i);\0A    }\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_NoSplitStack = internal constant [166 x i8] c"The ``no_split_stack`` attribute disables the emission of the split stack\0Apreamble for a particular function. It has no effect if ``-fsplit-stack``\0Ais not specified.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_NoStackProtector = internal constant [706 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((no_stack_protector))`` and Microsoft\0Astyle ``__declspec(safebuffers)`` attribute which disables\0Athe stack protector on the specified function. This attribute is useful for\0Aselectively disabling the stack protector on some functions when building with\0A``-fstack-protector`` compiler option.\0A\0AFor example, it disables the stack protector for the function ``foo`` but function\0A``bar`` will still be built with the stack protector with the ``-fstack-protector``\0Aoption.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A    int __attribute__((no_stack_protector))\0A    foo (int x); // stack protection will be disabled for foo.\0A\0A    int bar(int y); // bar can be built with the stack protector.\00", align 16
@_ZL30AttrDoc_NoThreadSafetyAnalysis = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_NoThrow = internal constant [423 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((nothrow))`` and Microsoft style\0A``__declspec(nothrow)`` attribute as an equivalent of ``noexcept`` on function\0Adeclarations. This attribute informs the compiler that the annotated function\0Adoes not throw an exception. This prevents exception-unwinding. This attribute\0Ais particularly useful on functions in the C Standard Library that are\0Aguaranteed to not throw an exception.\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_NoTrivialAutoVarInit = internal constant [435 x i8] c"The ``__declspec(no_init_all)`` attribute disables the automatic initialization that the\0A`-ftrivial-auto-var-init`_ flag would have applied to locals in a marked function, or instances of\0Aa marked type. Note that this attribute has no effect for locals that are automatically initialized\0Awithout the `-ftrivial-auto-var-init`_ flag.\0A\0A.. _`-ftrivial-auto-var-init`: ClangCommandLineReference.html#cmdoption-clang-ftrivial-auto-var-init\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_NoUniqueAddress = internal constant [909 x i8] c"The ``no_unique_address`` attribute allows tail padding in a non-static data\0Amember to overlap other members of the enclosing class (and in the special\0Acase when the type is empty, permits it to fully overlap other members).\0AThe field is laid out as if a base class were encountered at the corresponding\0Apoint within the class (except that it does not share a vptr with the enclosing\0Aobject).\0A\0AExample usage:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  template<typename T, typename Alloc> struct my_vector {\0A    T *p;\0A    [[no_unique_address]] Alloc alloc;\0A    // ...\0A  };\0A  static_assert(sizeof(my_vector<int, std::allocator<int>>) == sizeof(int*));\0A\0A``[[no_unique_address]]`` is a standard C++20 attribute. Clang supports its use\0Ain C++11 onwards.\0A\0AOn MSVC targets, ``[[no_unique_address]]`` is ignored; use\0A``[[msvc::no_unique_address]]`` instead. Currently there is no guarantee of ABI\0Acompatibility or stability with MSVC.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_NoUwtable = internal constant [262 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``nouwtable`` attribute which skips emitting\0Athe unwind table entry for the specified function. This attribute is useful for\0Aselectively emitting the unwind table entry on some functions when building with\0A``-funwind-tables`` compiler option.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_NotTailCalled = internal constant [1502 x i8] c"The ``not_tail_called`` attribute prevents tail-call optimization on statically\0Abound calls. Objective-c methods, and functions marked as ``always_inline``\0Acannot be marked as ``not_tail_called``.\0A\0AFor example, it prevents tail-call optimization in the following case:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    int __attribute__((not_tail_called)) foo1(int);\0A\0A    int foo2(int a) {\0A      return foo1(a); // No tail-call optimization on direct calls.\0A    }\0A\0AHowever, it doesn't prevent tail-call optimization in this case:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    int __attribute__((not_tail_called)) foo1(int);\0A\0A    int foo2(int a) {\0A      int (*fn)(int) = &foo1;\0A\0A      // not_tail_called has no effect on an indirect call even if the call can\0A      // be resolved at compile time.\0A      return (*fn)(a);\0A    }\0A\0AGenerally, marking an overriding virtual function as ``not_tail_called`` is\0Anot useful, because this attribute is a property of the static type. Calls\0Amade through a pointer or reference to the base class type will respect\0Athe ``not_tail_called`` attribute of the base class's member function,\0Aregardless of the runtime destination of the call:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    struct Foo { virtual void f(); };\0A    struct Bar : Foo {\0A      [[clang::not_tail_called]] void f() override;\0A    };\0A    void callera(Bar& bar) {\0A      Foo& foo = bar;\0A      // not_tail_called has no effect on here, even though the\0A      // underlying method is f from Bar.\0A      foo.f();\0A      bar.f(); // No tail-call optimization on here.\0A    }\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_OMPAllocateDecl = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL17AttrDoc_OMPAssume = internal constant [1266 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``[[omp::assume(\22assumption\22)]]`` attribute to\0Aprovide additional information to the optimizer. The string-literal, here\0A\22assumption\22, will be attached to the function declaration such that later\0Aanalysis and optimization passes can assume the \22assumption\22 to hold.\0AThis is similar to :ref:`__builtin_assume <langext-__builtin_assume>` but\0Ainstead of an expression that can be assumed to be non-zero, the assumption is\0Aexpressed as a string and it holds for the entire function.\0A\0AA function can have multiple assume attributes and they propagate from prior\0Adeclarations to later definitions. Multiple assumptions are aggregated into a\0Asingle comma separated string. Thus, one can provide multiple assumptions via\0Aa comma separated string, i.a.,\0A``[[omp::assume(\22assumption1,assumption2\22)]]``.\0A\0AWhile LLVM plugins might provide more assumption strings, the default LLVM\0Aoptimization passes are aware of the following assumptions:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: none\0A\0A    \22omp_no_openmp\22\0A    \22omp_no_openmp_routines\22\0A    \22omp_no_parallelism\22\0A\0AThe OpenMP standard defines the meaning of OpenMP assumptions (\22omp_XYZ\22 is\0Aspelled \22XYZ\22 in the `OpenMP 5.1 Standard`_).\0A\0A.. _`OpenMP 5.1 Standard`: https://www.openmp.org/spec-html/5.1/openmpsu37.html#x56-560002.5.2\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_OMPCaptureNoInit = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL28AttrDoc_OMPDeclareTargetDecl = internal constant [634 x i8] c"The ``declare target`` directive specifies that variables and functions are mapped\0Ato a device for OpenMP offload mechanism.\0A\0AThe syntax of the declare target directive is as follows:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    #pragma omp declare target new-line\0A    declarations-definition-seq\0A    #pragma omp end declare target new-line\0A\0Aor\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    #pragma omp declare target (extended-list) new-line\0A\0Aor\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    #pragma omp declare target clause[ [,] clause ... ] new-line\0A\0Awhere clause is one of the following:\0A\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A     to(extended-list)\0A     link(list)\0A     device_type(host | nohost | any)\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_OMPDeclareVariant = internal constant [2231 x i8] c"The ``declare variant`` directive declares a specialized variant of a base\0Afunction and specifies the context in which that specialized variant is used.\0AThe declare variant directive is a declarative directive.\0AThe syntax of the ``declare variant`` construct is as follows:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: none\0A\0A    #pragma omp declare variant(variant-func-id) clause new-line\0A    [#pragma omp declare variant(variant-func-id) clause new-line]\0A    [...]\0A    function definition or declaration\0A\0Awhere clause is one of the following:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: none\0A\0A    match(context-selector-specification)\0A\0Aand where ``variant-func-id`` is the name of a function variant that is either a\0Abase language identifier or, for C++, a template-id.\0A\0AClang provides the following context selector extensions, used via\0A``implementation={extension(EXTENSION)}``:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: none\0A\0A    match_all\0A    match_any\0A    match_none\0A    disable_implicit_base\0A    allow_templates\0A    bind_to_declaration\0A\0AThe match extensions change when the *entire* context selector is considered a\0Amatch for an OpenMP context. The default is ``all``, with ``none`` no trait in the\0Aselector is allowed to be in the OpenMP context, with ``any`` a single trait in\0Aboth the selector and OpenMP context is sufficient. Only a single match\0Aextension trait is allowed per context selector.\0AThe disable extensions remove default effects of the ``begin declare variant``\0Aapplied to a definition. If ``disable_implicit_base`` is given, we will not\0Aintroduce an implicit base function for a variant if no base function was\0Afound. The variant is still generated but will never be called, due to the\0Aabsence of a base function and consequently calls to a base function.\0AThe allow extensions change when the ``begin declare variant`` effect is\0Aapplied to a definition. If ``allow_templates`` is given, template function\0Adefinitions are considered as specializations of existing or assumed template\0Adeclarations with the same name. The template parameters for the base functions\0Aare used to instantiate the specialization. If ``bind_to_declaration`` is given,\0Aapply the same variant rules to function declarations. This allows the user to\0Aoverride declarations with only a function declaration.\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_OMPThreadPrivateDecl = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL22AttrDoc_OSConsumesThis = internal constant [3243 x i8] c"The behavior of a function with respect to reference counting for Foundation\0A(Objective-C), CoreFoundation (C) and OSObject (C++) is determined by a naming\0Aconvention (e.g. functions starting with \22get\22 are assumed to return at\0A``+0``).\0A\0AIt can be overridden using a family of the following attributes. In\0AObjective-C, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_retained))`` applied to\0Aa function communicates that the object is returned at ``+1``, and the caller\0Ais responsible for freeing it.\0ASimilarly, the annotation ``__attribute__((ns_returns_not_retained))``\0Aspecifies that the object is returned at ``+0`` and the ownership remains with\0Athe callee.\0AThe annotation ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))`` specifies that\0Athe Objective-C method call consumes the reference to ``self``, e.g. by\0Aattaching it to a supplied parameter.\0AAdditionally, parameters can have an annotation\0A``__attribute__((ns_consumed))``, which specifies that passing an owned object\0Aas that parameter effectively transfers the ownership, and the caller is no\0Alonger responsible for it.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AIn C programs using CoreFoundation, a similar set of attributes:\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((cf_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((cf_consumed))``\0Ahave the same respective semantics when applied to CoreFoundation objects.\0AThese attributes affect code generation when interacting with ARC code, and\0Athey are used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AFinally, in C++ interacting with XNU kernel (objects inheriting from OSObject),\0Athe same attribute family is present:\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))`` and ``__attribute__((os_consumed))``,\0Awith the same respective semantics.\0ASimilar to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
