r to ``__attribute__((ns_consumes_self))``,\0A``__attribute__((os_consumes_this))`` specifies that the method call consumes\0Athe reference to \22this\22 (e.g., when attaching it to a different object supplied\0Aas a parameter).\0AOut parameters (parameters the function is meant to write into,\0Aeither via pointers-to-pointers or references-to-pointers)\0Amay be annotated with ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained))``\0Aor ``__attribute__((os_returns_not_retained))`` which specifies that the object\0Awritten into the out parameter should (or respectively should not) be released\0Aafter use.\0ASince often out parameters may or may not be written depending on the exit\0Acode of the function,\0Aannotations ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_zero))``\0Aand ``__attribute__((os_returns_retained_on_non_zero))`` specify that\0Aan out parameter at ``+1`` is written if and only if the function returns a zero\0A(respectively non-zero) error code.\0AObserve that return-code-dependent out parameter annotations are only\0Aavailable for retained out parameters, as non-retained object do not have to be\0Areleased by the callee.\0AThese attributes are only used by the Clang Static Analyzer.\0A\0AThe family of attributes ``X_returns_X_retained`` can be added to functions,\0AC++ methods, and Objective-C methods and properties.\0AAttributes ``X_consumed`` can be added to parameters of methods, functions,\0Aand Objective-C methods.\00", align 16
