@_ZL30AttrDoc_ObjCExternallyRetained = internal constant [1025 x i8] c"The ``objc_externally_retained`` attribute can be applied to strong local\0Avariables, functions, methods, or blocks to opt into\0A`externally-retained semantics\0A<https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AutomaticReferenceCounting.html#externally-retained-variables>`_.\0A\0AWhen applied to the definition of a function, method, or block, every parameter\0Aof the function with implicit strong retainable object pointer type is\0Aconsidered externally-retained, and becomes ``const``. By explicitly annotating\0Aa parameter with ``__strong``, you can opt back into the default\0Anon-externally-retained behavior for that parameter. For instance,\0A``first_param`` is externally-retained below, but not ``second_param``:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  __attribute__((objc_externally_retained))\0A  void f(NSArray *first_param, __strong NSArray *second_param) {\0A    // ...\0A  }\0A\0ALikewise, when applied to a strong local variable, that variable becomes\0A``const`` and is considered externally-retained.\0A\0AWhen compiled without ``-fobjc-arc``, this attribute is ignored.\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_ObjCIndependentClass = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_ObjCMethodFamily = internal constant [1055 x i8] c"Many methods in Objective-C have conventional meanings determined by their\0Aselectors. It is sometimes useful to be able to mark a method as having a\0Aparticular conventional meaning despite not having the right selector, or as\0Anot having the conventional meaning that its selector would suggest. For these\0Ause cases, we provide an attribute to specifically describe the \22method family\22\0Athat a method belongs to.\0A\0A**Usage**: ``__attribute__((objc_method_family(X)))``, where ``X`` is one of\0A``none``, ``alloc``, ``copy``, ``init``, ``mutableCopy``, or ``new``. This\0Aattribute can only be placed at the end of a method declaration:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  - (NSString *)initMyStringValue __attribute__((objc_method_family(none)));\0A\0AUsers who do not wish to change the conventional meaning of a method, and who\0Amerely want to document its non-standard retain and release semantics, should\0Ause the retaining behavior attributes (``ns_returns_retained``,\0A``ns_returns_not_retained``, etc).\0A\0AQuery for this feature with ``__has_attribute(objc_method_family)``.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_ObjCNSObject = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_ObjCPreciseLifetime = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL32AttrDoc_ObjCRequiresPropertyDefs = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_ObjCRequiresSuper = internal constant [1514 x i8] c"Some Objective-C classes allow a subclass to override a particular method in a\0Aparent class but expect that the overriding method also calls the overridden\0Amethod in the parent class. For these cases, we provide an attribute to\0Adesignate that a method requires a \22call to ``super``\22 in the overriding\0Amethod in the subclass.\0A\0A**Usage**: ``__attribute__((objc_requires_super))``. This attribute can only\0Abe placed at the end of a method declaration:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  - (void)foo __attribute__((objc_requires_super));\0A\0AThis attribute can only be applied the method declarations within a class, and\0Anot a protocol. Currently this attribute does not enforce any placement of\0Awhere the call occurs in the overriding method (such as in the case of\0A``-dealloc`` where the call must appear at the end). It checks only that it\0Aexists.\0A\0ANote that on both OS X and iOS that the Foundation framework provides a\0Aconvenience macro ``NS_REQUIRES_SUPER`` that provides syntactic sugar for this\0Aattribute:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  - (void)foo NS_REQUIRES_SUPER;\0A\0AThis macro is conditionally defined depending on the compiler's support for\0Athis attribute. If the compiler does not support the attribute the macro\0Aexpands to nothing.\0A\0AOperationally, when a method has this annotation the compiler will warn if the\0Aimplementation of an override in a subclass does not call super. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A   warning: method possibly missing a [super AnnotMeth] call\0A   - (void) AnnotMeth{};\0A                      ^\00", align 16
@_ZL31AttrDoc_ObjCReturnsInnerPointer = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_ObjCRootClass = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL33AttrDoc_ObjCSubclassingRestricted = internal constant [121 x i8] c"This attribute can be added to an Objective-C ``@interface`` declaration to\0Aensure that this class cannot be subclassed.\00", align 16
@_ZL35AttrDoc_OpenCLIntelReqdSubGroupSize = internal constant [553 x i8] c"The optional attribute intel_reqd_sub_group_size can be used to indicate that\0Athe kernel must be compiled and executed with the specified subgroup size. When\0Athis attribute is present, get_max_sub_group_size() is guaranteed to return the\0Aspecified integer value. This is important for the correctness of many subgroup\0Aalgorithms, and in some cases may be used by the compiler to generate more optimal\0Acode. See `cl_intel_required_subgroup_size\0A<https://www.khronos.org/registry/OpenCL/extensions/intel/cl_intel_required_subgroup_size.txt>`\0Afor details.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_OpenCLKernel = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_OptimizeNone = internal constant [874 x i8] c"The ``optnone`` attribute suppresses essentially all optimizations\0Aon a function or method, regardless of the optimization level applied to\0Athe compilation unit as a whole. This is particularly useful when you\0Aneed to debug a particular function, but it is infeasible to build the\0Aentire application without optimization. Avoiding optimization on the\0Aspecified function can improve the quality of the debugging information\0Afor that function.\0A\0AThis attribute is incompatible with the ``always_inline`` and ``minsize``\0Aattributes.\0A\0ANote that this attribute does not apply recursively to nested functions such as\0Alambdas or blocks when using declaration-specific attribute syntaxes such as double\0Asquare brackets (``[[]]``) or ``__attribute__``. The ``#pragma`` syntax can be\0Aused to apply the attribute to all functions, including nested functions, in a\0Arange of source code.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_Override = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL13AttrDoc_Owner = internal constant [572 x i8] c".. Note:: This attribute is experimental and its effect on analysis is subject to change in\0A  a future version of clang.\0A\0AThe attribute ``[[gsl::Owner(T)]]`` applies to structs and classes that own an\0Aobject of type ``T``:\0A\0A.. code::\0A\0A  class [[gsl::Owner(int)]] IntOwner {\0A  private:\0A    int value;\0A  public:\0A    int *getInt() { return &value; }\0A  };\0A\0AThe argument ``T`` is optional and is ignored.\0AThis attribute may be used by analysis tools and has no effect on code\0Ageneration. A ``void`` argument means that the class can own any type.\0A\0ASee Pointer_ for an example.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_Ownership = internal constant [3220 x i8] c".. note::\0A\0A  In order for the Clang Static Analyzer to acknowledge these attributes, the\0A  ``Optimistic`` config needs to be set to true for the checker\0A  ``unix.DynamicMemoryModeling``:\0A\0A  ``-Xclang -analyzer-config -Xclang unix.DynamicMemoryModeling:Optimistic=true``\0A\0AThese attributes are used by the Clang Static Analyzer's dynamic memory modeling\0Afacilities to mark custom allocating/deallocating functions.\0A\0AAll 3 attributes' first parameter of type string is the type of the allocation:\0A``malloc``, ``new``, etc. to allow for catching :ref:`mismatched deallocation\0A<unix-MismatchedDeallocator>` bugs. The allocation type can be any string, e.g.\0Aa function annotated with\0Areturning a piece of memory of type ``lasagna`` but freed with a function\0Aannotated to release ``cheese`` typed memory will result in mismatched\0Adeallocation warning.\0A\0AThe (currently) only allocation type having special meaning is ``malloc`` --\0Athe Clang Static Analyzer makes sure that allocating functions annotated with\0A``malloc`` are treated like they used the standard ``malloc()``, and can be\0Asafely deallocated with the standard ``free()``.\0A\0A* Use ``ownership_returns`` to mark a function as an allocating function. Takes\0A  1 parameter to denote the allocation type.\0A* Use ``ownership_takes`` to mark a function as a deallocating function. Takes 2\0A  parameters: the allocation type, and the index of the parameter that is being\0A  deallocated (counting from 1).\0A* Use ``ownership_holds`` to mark that a function takes over the ownership of a\0A  piece of memory and will free it at some unspecified point in the future. Like\0A  ``ownership_takes``, this takes 2 parameters: the allocation type, and the\0A  index of the parameter whose ownership will be taken over (counting from 1).\0A\0AThe annotations ``ownership_takes`` and ``ownership_holds`` both prevent memory\0Aleak reports (concerning the specified argument); the difference between them\0Ais that using taken memory is a use-after-free error, while using held memory\0Ais assumed to be legitimate.\0A\0AExample:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  // Denotes that my_malloc will return with a dynamically allocated piece of\0A  // memory using malloc().\0A  void __attribute((ownership_returns(malloc))) *my_malloc(size_t);\0A\0A  // Denotes that my_free will deallocate its parameter using free().\0A  void __attribute((ownership_takes(malloc, 1))) my_free(void *);\0A\0A  // Denotes that my_hold will take over the ownership of its parameter that was\0A  // allocated via malloc().\0A  void __attribute((ownership_holds(malloc, 1))) my_hold(void *);\0A\0AFurther reading about dynamic memory modeling in the Clang Static Analyzer is\0Afound in these checker docs:\0A:ref:`unix.Malloc <unix-Malloc>`, :ref:`unix.MallocSizeof <unix-MallocSizeof>`,\0A:ref:`unix.MismatchedDeallocator <unix-MismatchedDeallocator>`,\0A:ref:`cplusplus.NewDelete <cplusplus-NewDelete>`,\0A:ref:`cplusplus.NewDeleteLeaks <cplusplus-NewDeleteLeaks>`,\0A:ref:`optin.taint.TaintedAlloc <optin-taint-TaintedAlloc>`.\0AMind that many more checkers are affected by dynamic memory modeling changes to\0Asome extent.\0A\0AFurther reading for other annotations:\0A`Source Annotations in the Clang Static Analyzer <https://clang.llvm.org/docs/analyzer/user-docs/Annotations.html>`_.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Packed = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_ParamTypestate = internal constant [297 x i8] c"This attribute specifies expectations about function parameters. Calls to an\0Afunction with annotated parameters will issue a warning if the corresponding\0Aargument isn't in the expected state. The attribute is also used to set the\0Ainitial state of the parameter when analyzing the function's body.\00", align 16
@_ZL30AttrDoc_PatchableFunctionEntry = internal constant [450 x i8] c"``__attribute__((patchable_function_entry(N,M)))`` is used to generate M NOPs\0Abefore the function entry and N-M NOPs after the function entry. This attribute\0Atakes precedence over the command line option ``-fpatchable-function-entry=N,M``.\0A``M`` defaults to 0 if omitted.\0A\0AThis attribute is only supported on\0Aaarch64/aarch64-be/loongarch32/loongarch64/riscv32/riscv64/i386/x86-64/ppc/ppc64 targets.\0AFor ppc/ppc64 targets, AIX is still not supported.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_Pointer = internal constant [1762 x i8] c".. Note:: This attribute is experimental and its effect on analysis is subject to change in\0A  a future version of clang.\0A\0AThe attribute ``[[gsl::Pointer(T)]]`` applies to structs and classes that behave\0Alike pointers to an object of type ``T``:\0A\0A.. code::\0A\0A  class [[gsl::Pointer(int)]] IntPointer {\0A  private:\0A    int *valuePointer;\0A  public:\0A    IntPointer(const IntOwner&);\0A    int *getInt() { return valuePointer; }\0A  };\0A\0AThe argument ``T`` is optional and is ignored.\0AThis attribute may be used by analysis tools and has no effect on code\0Ageneration. A ``void`` argument means that the pointer can point to any type.\0A\0AExample:\0AWhen constructing an instance of a class annotated like this (a Pointer) from\0Aan instance of a class annotated with ``[[gsl::Owner]]`` (an Owner),\0Athen the analysis will consider the Pointer to point inside the Owner.\0AWhen the Owner's lifetime ends, it will consider the Pointer to be dangling.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  int f() {\0A    IntPointer P(IntOwner{}); // P \22points into\22 a temporary IntOwner object\0A    P.getInt(); // P is dangling\0A  }\0A\0AIf a template class is annotated with ``[[gsl::Owner]]``, and the first\0Ainstantiated template argument is a pointer type (raw pointer, or ``[[gsl::Pointer]]``),\0Athe analysis will consider the instantiated class as a container of the pointer.\0AWhen constructing such an object from a GSL owner object, the analysis will\0Aassume that the container holds a pointer to the owner object. Consequently,\0Awhen the owner object is destroyed, the pointer will be considered dangling.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A   int f() {\0A     std::vector<std::string_view> v = {std::string()}; // v holds a dangling pointer.\0A     std::optional<std::string_view> o = std::string(); // o holds a dangling pointer.\0A   }\00", align 16
