@_ZL21AttrDoc_PreferredName = internal constant [1141 x i8] c"The ``preferred_name`` attribute can be applied to a class template, and\0Aspecifies a preferred way of naming a specialization of the template. The\0Apreferred name will be used whenever the corresponding template specialization\0Awould otherwise be printed in a diagnostic or similar context.\0A\0AThe preferred name must be a typedef or type alias declaration that refers to a\0Aspecialization of the class template (not including any type qualifiers). In\0Ageneral this requires the template to be declared at least twice. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  template<typename T> struct basic_string;\0A  using string = basic_string<char>;\0A  using wstring = basic_string<wchar_t>;\0A  template<typename T> struct [[clang::preferred_name(string),\0A                                clang::preferred_name(wstring)]] basic_string {\0A    // ...\0A  };\0A\0A\0ANote that the ``preferred_name`` attribute will be ignored when the compiler\0Awrites a C++20 Module interface now. This is due to a compiler issue\0A(https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/issues/56490) that blocks users to modularize\0Adeclarations with `preferred_name`. This is intended to be fixed in the future.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_PreferredType = internal constant [1857 x i8] c"This attribute allows adjusting the type of a bit-field in debug information.\0AThis can be helpful when a bit-field is intended to store an enumeration value,\0Abut has to be specified as having the enumeration's underlying type in order to\0Afacilitate compiler optimizations or bit-field packing behavior. Normally, the\0Aunderlying type is what is emitted in debug information, which can make it hard\0Afor debuggers to know to map a bit-field's value back to a particular enumeration.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    enum Colors { Red, Green, Blue };\0A\0A    struct S {\0A      [[clang::preferred_type(Colors)]] unsigned ColorVal : 2;\0A      [[clang::preferred_type(bool)]] unsigned UseAlternateColorSpace : 1;\0A    } s = { Green, false };\0A\0AWithout the attribute, a debugger is likely to display the value ``1`` for ``ColorVal``\0Aand ``0`` for ``UseAlternateColorSpace``. With the attribute, the debugger may now\0Adisplay ``Green`` and ``false`` instead.\0A\0AThis can be used to map a bit-field to an arbitrary type that isn't integral\0Aor an enumeration type. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    struct A {\0A      short a1;\0A      short a2;\0A    };\0A\0A    struct B {\0A      [[clang::preferred_type(A)]] unsigned b1 : 32 = 0x000F'000C;\0A    };\0A\0Awill associate the type ``A`` with the ``b1`` bit-field and is intended to display\0Asomething like this in the debugger:\0A\0A.. code-block:: text\0A\0A    Process 2755547 stopped\0A    * thread #1, name = 'test-preferred-', stop reason = step in\0A        frame #0: 0x0000555555555148 test-preferred-type`main at test.cxx:13:14\0A       10   int main()\0A       11   {\0A       12       B b;\0A    -> 13       return b.b1;\0A       14   }\0A    (lldb) v -T\0A    (B) b = {\0A      (A:32) b1 = {\0A        (short) a1 = 12\0A        (short) a2 = 15\0A      }\0A    }\0A\0ANote that debuggers may not be able to handle more complex mappings, and so\0Athis usage is debugger-dependent.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_PtGuardedBy = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_PtGuardedVar = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
