@_ZL22AttrDoc_RISCVInterrupt = internal constant [782 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((interrupt))`` attribute on RISCV\0Atargets. This attribute may be attached to a function definition and instructs\0Athe backend to generate appropriate function entry/exit code so that it can be\0Aused directly as an interrupt service routine.\0A\0APermissible values for this parameter are ``user``, ``supervisor``,\0Aand ``machine``. If there is no parameter, then it defaults to machine.\0A\0ARepeated interrupt attribute on the same declaration will cause a warning\0Ato be emitted. In case of repeated declarations, the last one prevails.\0A\0ARefer to:\0Ahttps://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/RISC-V-Function-Attributes.html\0Ahttps://riscv.org/specifications/privileged-isa/\0AThe RISC-V Instruction Set Manual Volume II: Privileged Architecture\0AVersion 1.10.\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_RandomizeLayout = internal constant [961 x i8] c"The attribute ``randomize_layout``, when attached to a C structure, selects it\0Afor structure layout field randomization; a compile-time hardening technique. A\0A\22seed\22 value, is specified via the ``-frandomize-layout-seed=`` command line flag.\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: bash\0A\0A  SEED=`od -A n -t x8 -N 32 /dev/urandom | tr -d ' \\n'`\0A  make ... CFLAGS=\22-frandomize-layout-seed=$SEED\22 ...\0A\0AYou can also supply the seed in a file with ``-frandomize-layout-seed-file=``.\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: bash\0A\0A  od -A n -t x8 -N 32 /dev/urandom | tr -d ' \\n' > /tmp/seed_file.txt\0A  make ... CFLAGS=\22-frandomize-layout-seed-file=/tmp/seed_file.txt\22 ...\0A\0AThe randomization is deterministic based for a given seed, so the entire\0Aprogram should be compiled with the same seed, but keep the seed safe\0Aotherwise.\0A\0AThe attribute ``no_randomize_layout``, when attached to a C structure,\0Ainstructs the compiler that this structure should not have its field layout\0Arandomized.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_ReadOnlyPlacement = internal constant [2027 x i8] c"This attribute is attached to a structure, class or union declaration.\0A  When attached to a record declaration/definition, it checks if all instances\0A  of this type can be placed in the read-only data segment of the program. If it\0A  finds an instance that can not be placed in a read-only segment, the compiler\0A  emits a warning at the source location where the type was used.\0A\0A  Examples:\0A  * ``struct __attribute__((enforce_read_only_placement)) Foo;``\0A  * ``struct __attribute__((enforce_read_only_placement)) Bar { ... };``\0A\0A  Both ``Foo`` and ``Bar`` types have the ``enforce_read_only_placement`` attribute.\0A\0A  The goal of introducing this attribute is to assist developers with writing secure\0A  code. A ``const``-qualified global is generally placed in the read-only section\0A  of the memory that has additional run time protection from malicious writes. By\0A  attaching this attribute to a declaration, the developer can express the intent\0A  to place all instances of the annotated type in the read-only program memory.\0A\0A  Note 1: The attribute doesn't guarantee that the object will be placed in the\0A  read-only data segment as it does not instruct the compiler to ensure such\0A  a placement. It emits a warning if something in the code can be proven to prevent\0A  an instance from being placed in the read-only data segment.\0A\0A  Note 2: Currently, clang only checks if all global declarations of a given type 'T'\0A  are ``const``-qualified. The following conditions would also prevent the data to be\0A  put into read only segment, but the corresponding warnings are not yet implemented.\0A\0A  1. An instance of type ``T`` is allocated on the heap/stack.\0A  2. Type ``T`` defines/inherits a mutable field.\0A  3. Type ``T`` defines/inherits non-constexpr constructor(s) for initialization.\0A  4. A field of type ``T`` is defined by type ``Q``, which does not bear the\0A     ``enforce_read_only_placement`` attribute.\0A  5. A type ``Q`` inherits from type ``T`` and it does not have the\0A     ``enforce_read_only_placement`` attribute.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_Reinitializes = internal constant [804 x i8] c"The ``reinitializes`` attribute can be applied to a non-static, non-const C++\0Amember function to indicate that this member function reinitializes the entire\0Aobject to a known state, independent of the previous state of the object.\0A\0AThis attribute can be interpreted by static analyzers that warn about uses of an\0Aobject that has been left in an indeterminate state by a move operation. If a\0Amember function marked with the ``reinitializes`` attribute is called on a\0Amoved-from object, the analyzer can conclude that the object is no longer in an\0Aindeterminate state.\0A\0AA typical example where this attribute would be used is on functions that clear\0Aa container class:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  template <class T>\0A  class Container {\0A  public:\0A    ...\0A    [[clang::reinitializes]] void Clear();\0A    ...\0A  };\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_ReleaseCapability = internal constant [44 x i8] c"Marks a function as releasing a capability.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_ReqdWorkGroupSize = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL26AttrDoc_RequiresCapability = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_Restrict = internal constant [214 x i8] c"The ``malloc`` attribute indicates that the function acts like a system memory\0Aallocation function, returning a pointer to allocated storage disjoint from the\0Astorage for any other object accessible to the caller.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Retain = internal constant [981 x i8] c"This attribute, when attached to a function or variable definition, prevents\0Asection garbage collection in the linker. It does not prevent other discard\0Amechanisms, such as archive member selection, and COMDAT group resolution.\0A\0AIf the compiler does not emit the definition, e.g. because it was not used in\0Athe translation unit or the compiler was able to eliminate all of the uses,\0Athis attribute has no effect.  This attribute is typically combined with the\0A``used`` attribute to force the definition to be emitted and preserved into the\0Afinal linked image.\0A\0AThis attribute is only necessary on ELF targets; other targets prevent section\0Agarbage collection by the linker when using the ``used`` attribute alone.\0AUsing the attributes together should result in consistent behavior across\0Atargets.\0A\0AThis attribute requires the linker to support the ``SHF_GNU_RETAIN`` extension.\0AThis support is available in GNU ``ld`` and ``gold`` as of binutils 2.36, as\0Awell as in ``ld.lld`` 13.\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_ReturnTypestate = internal constant [572 x i8] c"The ``return_typestate`` attribute can be applied to functions or parameters.\0AWhen applied to a function the attribute specifies the state of the returned\0Avalue. The function's body is checked to ensure that it always returns a value\0Ain the specified state. On the caller side, values returned by the annotated\0Afunction are initialized to the given state.\0A\0AWhen applied to a function parameter it modifies the state of an argument after\0Aa call to the function returns. The function's body is checked to ensure that\0Athe parameter is in the expected state before returning.\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_ReturnsNonNull = internal constant [724 x i8] c"The ``returns_nonnull`` attribute indicates that a particular function (or\0AObjective-C method) always returns a non-null pointer. For example, a\0Aparticular system ``malloc`` might be defined to terminate a process when\0Amemory is not available rather than returning a null pointer:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    extern void * malloc (size_t size) __attribute__((returns_nonnull));\0A\0AThe ``returns_nonnull`` attribute implies that returning a null pointer is\0Aundefined behavior, which the optimizer may take advantage of. The ``_Nonnull``\0Atype qualifier indicates that a pointer cannot be null in a more general manner\0A(because it is part of the type system) and does not imply undefined behavior,\0Amaking it more widely applicable\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_ReturnsTwice = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_SYCLKernel = internal constant [2857 x i8] c"The ``sycl_kernel`` attribute specifies that a function template will be used\0Ato outline device code and to generate an OpenCL kernel.\0AHere is a code example of the SYCL program, which demonstrates the compiler's\0Aoutlining job:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  int foo(int x) { return ++x; }\0A\0A  using namespace cl::sycl;\0A  queue Q;\0A  buffer<int, 1> a(range<1>{1024});\0A  Q.submit([&](handler& cgh) {\0A    auto A = a.get_access<access::mode::write>(cgh);\0A    cgh.parallel_for<init_a>(range<1>{1024}, [=](id<1> index) {\0A      A[index] = index[0] + foo(42);\0A    });\0A  }\0A\0AA C++ function object passed to the ``parallel_for`` is called a \22SYCL kernel\22.\0AA SYCL kernel defines the entry point to the \22device part\22 of the code. The\0Acompiler will emit all symbols accessible from a \22kernel\22. In this code\0Aexample, the compiler will emit \22foo\22 function. More details about the\0Acompilation of functions for the device part can be found in the SYCL 1.2.1\0Aspecification Section 6.4.\0ATo show to the compiler entry point to the \22device part\22 of the code, the SYCL\0Aruntime can use the ``sycl_kernel`` attribute in the following way:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  namespace cl {\0A  namespace sycl {\0A  class handler {\0A    template <typename KernelName, typename KernelType/*, ...*/>\0A    __attribute__((sycl_kernel)) void sycl_kernel_function(KernelType KernelFuncObj) {\0A      // ...\0A      KernelFuncObj();\0A    }\0A\0A    template <typename KernelName, typename KernelType, int Dims>\0A    void parallel_for(range<Dims> NumWorkItems, KernelType KernelFunc) {\0A  #ifdef __SYCL_DEVICE_ONLY__\0A      sycl_kernel_function<KernelName, KernelType, Dims>(KernelFunc);\0A  #else\0A      // Host implementation\0A  #endif\0A    }\0A  };\0A  } // namespace sycl\0A  } // namespace cl\0A\0AThe compiler will also generate an OpenCL kernel using the function marked with\0Athe ``sycl_kernel`` attribute.\0AHere is the list of SYCL device compiler expectations with regard to the\0Afunction marked with the ``sycl_kernel`` attribute:\0A\0A- The function must be a template with at least two type template parameters.\0A  The compiler generates an OpenCL kernel and uses the first template parameter\0A  as a unique name for the generated OpenCL kernel. The host application uses\0A  this unique name to invoke the OpenCL kernel generated for the SYCL kernel\0A  specialized by this name and second template parameter ``KernelType`` (which\0A  might be an unnamed function object type).\0A- The function must have at least one parameter. The first parameter is\0A  required to be a function object type (named or unnamed i.e. lambda). The\0A  compiler uses function object type fields to generate OpenCL kernel\0A  parameters.\0A- The function must return void. The compiler reuses the body of marked functions to\0A  generate the OpenCL kernel body, and the OpenCL kernel must return ``void``.\0A\0AThe SYCL kernel in the previous code sample meets these expectations.\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_SYCLKernelEntryPoint = internal constant [8180 x i8] c"The ``sycl_kernel_entry_point`` attribute facilitates the generation of an\0Aoffload kernel entry point, sometimes called a SYCL kernel caller function,\0Asuitable for invoking a SYCL kernel on an offload device. The attribute is\0Aintended for use in the implementation of SYCL kernel invocation functions\0Alike the ``single_task`` and ``parallel_for`` member functions of the\0A``sycl::handler`` class specified in section 4.9.4, \22Command group ``handler``\0Aclass\22, of the SYCL 2020 specification.\0A\0AThe attribute requires a single type argument that specifies a class type that\0Ameets the requirements for a SYCL kernel name as described in section 5.2,\0A\22Naming of kernels\22, of the SYCL 2020 specification. A unique kernel name type\0Ais required for each function declared with the attribute. The attribute may\0Anot first appear on a declaration that follows a definition of the function.\0A\0AThe attribute only appertains to functions and only those that meet the\0Afollowing requirements.\0A\0A* Has a non-deduced ``void`` return type.\0A* Is not a non-static member function, constructor, or destructor.\0A* Is not a C variadic function.\0A* Is not a coroutine.\0A* Is not defined as deleted or as defaulted.\0A* Is not defined with a function try block.\0A* Is not declared with the ``constexpr`` or ``consteval`` specifiers.\0A* Is not declared with the ``[[noreturn]]`` attribute.\0A\0AUse in the implementation of a SYCL kernel invocation function might look as\0Afollows.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  namespace sycl {\0A  class handler {\0A    template<typename KernelNameType, typename KernelType>\0A    [[ clang::sycl_kernel_entry_point(KernelNameType) ]]\0A    static void kernel_entry_point(KernelType kernel) {\0A      kernel();\0A    }\0A\0A  public:\0A    template<typename KernelNameType, typename KernelType>\0A    void single_task(KernelType kernel) {\0A      // Call kernel_entry_point() to trigger generation of an offload\0A      // kernel entry point.\0A      kernel_entry_point<KernelNameType>(kernel);\0A      // Call functions appropriate for the desired offload backend\0A      // (OpenCL, CUDA, HIP, Level Zero, etc...).\0A    }\0A  };\0A  } // namespace sycl\0A\0AA SYCL kernel is a callable object of class type that is constructed on a host,\0Aoften via a lambda expression, and then passed to a SYCL kernel invocation\0Afunction to be executed on an offload device. A SYCL kernel invocation function\0Ais responsible for copying the provided SYCL kernel object to an offload\0Adevice and initiating a call to it. The SYCL kernel object and its data members\0Aconstitute the parameters of an offload kernel.\0A\0AA SYCL kernel type is required to satisfy the device copyability requirements\0Aspecified in section 3.13.1, \22Device copyable\22, of the SYCL 2020 specification.\0AAdditionally, any data members of the kernel object type are required to satisfy\0Asection 4.12.4, \22Rules for parameter passing to kernels\22. For most types, these\0Arules require that the type is trivially copyable.  However, the SYCL\0Aspecification mandates that certain special SYCL types, such as\0A``sycl::accessor`` and ``sycl::stream`` be device copyable even if they are not\0Atrivially copyable. These types require special handling because they cannot\0Abe copied to device memory as if by ``memcpy()``. Additionally, some offload\0Abackends, OpenCL for example, require objects of some of these types to be\0Apassed as individual arguments to the offload kernel.\0A\0AAn offload kernel consists of an entry point function that declares the\0Aparameters of the offload kernel and the set of all functions and variables that\0Aare directly or indirectly used by the entry point function.\0A\0AA SYCL kernel invocation function invokes a SYCL kernel on a device by\0Aperforming the following tasks (likely with the help of an offload backend\0Alike OpenCL):\0A\0A#. Identifying the offload kernel entry point to be used for the SYCL kernel.\0A\0A#. Deconstructing the SYCL kernel object, if necessary, to produce the set of\0A   offload kernel arguments required by the offload kernel entry point.\0A\0A#. Copying the offload kernel arguments to device memory.\0A\0A#. Initiating execution of the offload kernel entry point.\0A\0AThe offload kernel entry point for a SYCL kernel performs the following tasks:\0A\0A#. Reconstituting the SYCL kernel object, if necessary, using the offload\0A   kernel parameters.\0A\0A#. Calling the ``operator()`` member function of the (reconstituted) SYCL kernel\0A   object.\0A\0AThe ``sycl_kernel_entry_point`` attribute automates generation of an offload\0Akernel entry point that performs those latter tasks. The parameters and body of\0Aa function declared with the ``sycl_kernel_entry_point`` attribute specify a\0Apattern from which the parameters and body of the entry point function are\0Aderived. Consider the following call to a SYCL kernel invocation function.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  struct S { int i; };\0A  void f(sycl::handler &handler, sycl::stream &sout, S s) {\0A    handler.single_task<struct KN>([=] {\0A      sout << \22The value of s.i is \22 << s.i << \22\\n\22;\0A    });\0A  }\0A\0AThe SYCL kernel object is the result of the lambda expression. It has two\0Adata members corresponding to the captures of ``sout`` and ``s``. Since one\0Aof these data members corresponds to a special SYCL type that must be passed\0Aindividually as an offload kernel parameter, it is necessary to decompose the\0ASYCL kernel object into its constituent parts; the offload kernel will have\0Atwo kernel parameters. Given a SYCL implementation that uses a\0A``sycl_kernel_entry_point`` attributed function like the one shown above, an\0Aoffload kernel entry point function will be generated that looks approximately\0Aas follows.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void sycl-kernel-caller-for-KN(sycl::stream sout, S s) {\0A    kernel-type kernel = { sout, s );\0A    kernel();\0A  }\0A\0AThere are a few items worthy of note:\0A\0A#. The name of the generated function incorporates the SYCL kernel name,\0A   ``KN``, that was passed as the ``KernelNameType`` template parameter to\0A   ``kernel_entry_point()`` and provided as the argument to the\0A   ``sycl_kernel_entry_point`` attribute. There is a one-to-one correspondence\0A   between SYCL kernel names and offload kernel entry points.\0A\0A#. The SYCL kernel is a lambda closure type and therefore has no name;\0A   ``kernel-type`` is substituted above and corresponds to the ``KernelType``\0A   template parameter deduced in the call to ``kernel_entry_point()``.\0A   Lambda types cannot be declared and initialized using the aggregate\0A   initialization syntax used above, but the intended behavior should be clear.\0A\0A#. ``S`` is a device copyable type that does not directly or indirectly contain\0A   a data member of a SYCL special type. It therefore does not need to be\0A   decomposed into its constituent members to be passed as a kernel argument.\0A\0A#. The depiction of the ``sycl::stream`` parameter as a single self contained\0A   kernel parameter is an oversimplification. SYCL special types may require\0A   additional decomposition such that the generated function might have three\0A   or more parameters depending on how the SYCL library implementation defines\0A   these types.\0A\0A#. The call to ``kernel_entry_point()`` has no effect other than to trigger\0A   emission of the entry point function. The statments that make up the body\0A   of the function are not executed when the function is called; they are\0A   only used in the generation of the entry point function.\0A\0AIt is not necessary for a function declared with the ``sycl_kernel_entry_point``\0Aattribute to be called for the offload kernel entry point to be emitted. For\0Ainline functions and function templates, any ODR-use will suffice. For other\0Afunctions, an ODR-use is not required; the offload kernel entry point will be\0Aemitted if the function is defined.\0A\0AFunctions declared with the ``sycl_kernel_entry_point`` attribute are not\0Alimited to the simple example shown above. They may have additional template\0Aparameters, declare additional function parameters, and have complex control\0Aflow in the function body. Function parameter decomposition and reconstitution\0Ais performed for all function parameters. The function must abide by the\0Alanguage feature restrictions described in section 5.4, \22Language restrictions\0Afor device functions\22 in the SYCL 2020 specification.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_SYCLSpecialClass = internal constant [1866 x i8] c"SYCL defines some special classes (accessor, sampler, and stream) which require\0Aspecific handling during the generation of the SPIR entry point.\0AThe ``__attribute__((sycl_special_class))`` attribute is used in SYCL\0Aheaders to indicate that a class or a struct needs a specific handling when\0Ait is passed from host to device.\0ASpecial classes will have a mandatory ``__init`` method and an optional\0A``__finalize`` method (the ``__finalize`` method is used only with the\0A``stream`` type). Kernel parameters types are extract from the ``__init`` method\0Aparameters. The kernel function arguments list is derived from the\0Aarguments of the ``__init`` method. The arguments of the ``__init`` method are\0Acopied into the kernel function argument list and the ``__init`` and\0A``__finalize`` methods are called at the beginning and the end of the kernel,\0Arespectively.\0AThe ``__init`` and ``__finalize`` methods must be defined inside the\0Aspecial class.\0APlease note that this is an attribute that is used as an internal\0Aimplementation detail and not intended to be used by external users.\0A\0AThe syntax of the attribute is as follows:\0A\0A.. code-block:: text\0A\0A  class __attribute__((sycl_special_class)) accessor {};\0A  class [[clang::sycl_special_class]] accessor {};\0A\0AThis is a code example that illustrates the use of the attribute:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  class __attribute__((sycl_special_class)) SpecialType {\0A    int F1;\0A    int F2;\0A    void __init(int f1) {\0A      F1 = f1;\0A      F2 = f1;\0A    }\0A    void __finalize() {}\0A  public:\0A    SpecialType() = default;\0A    int getF2() const { return F2; }\0A  };\0A\0A  int main () {\0A    SpecialType T;\0A    cgh.single_task([=] {\0A      T.getF2();\0A    });\0A  }\0A\0AThis would trigger the following kernel entry point in the AST:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void __sycl_kernel(int f1) {\0A    SpecialType T;\0A    T.__init(f1);\0A    ...\0A    T.__finalize()\0A  }\00", align 16
