@_ZL20AttrDoc_SetTypestate = internal constant [168 x i8] c"Annotate methods that transition an object into a new state with\0A``__attribute__((set_typestate(new_state)))``. The new state must be\0Aunconsumed, consumed, or unknown.\00", align 16
@_ZL29AttrDoc_SharedTrylockFunction = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL32AttrDoc_SpeculativeLoadHardening = internal constant [1261 x i8] c"This attribute can be applied to a function declaration in order to indicate\0A  that `Speculative Load Hardening <https://llvm.org/docs/SpeculativeLoadHardening.html>`_\0A  should be enabled for the function body. This can also be applied to a method\0A  in Objective C. This attribute will take precedence over the command line flag in\0A  the case where `-mno-speculative-load-hardening <https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangCommandLineReference.html#cmdoption-clang-mspeculative-load-hardening>`_ is specified.\0A\0A  Speculative Load Hardening is a best-effort mitigation against\0A  information leak attacks that make use of control flow\0A  miss-speculation - specifically miss-speculation of whether a branch\0A  is taken or not. Typically vulnerabilities enabling such attacks are\0A  classified as \22Spectre variant #1\22. Notably, this does not attempt to\0A  mitigate against miss-speculation of branch target, classified as\0A  \22Spectre variant #2\22 vulnerabilities.\0A\0A  When inlining, the attribute is sticky. Inlining a function that\0A  carries this attribute will cause the caller to gain the\0A  attribute. This is intended to provide a maximally conservative model\0A  where the code in a function annotated with this attribute will always\0A  (even after inlining) end up hardened.\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_StandaloneDebug = internal constant [301 x i8] c"The ``standalone_debug`` attribute causes debug info to be emitted for a record\0Atype regardless of the debug info optimizations that are enabled with\0A-fno-standalone-debug. This attribute only has an effect when debug info\0Aoptimizations are enabled (e.g. with -fno-standalone-debug), and is C++-only.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_StrictFP = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL29AttrDoc_StrictGuardStackCheck = internal constant [591 x i8] c"Clang supports the Microsoft style ``__declspec((strict_gs_check))`` attribute\0Awhich upgrades the stack protector check from ``-fstack-protector`` to\0A``-fstack-protector-strong``.\0A\0AFor example, it upgrades the stack protector for the function ``foo`` to\0A``-fstack-protector-strong`` but function ``bar`` will still be built with the\0Astack protector with the ``-fstack-protector`` option.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A    __declspec((strict_gs_check))\0A    int foo(int x); // stack protection will be upgraded for foo.\0A\0A    int bar(int y); // bar can be built with the standard stack protector checks.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_SwiftAsync = internal constant [1119 x i8] c"The ``swift_async`` attribute specifies if and how a particular function or\0AObjective-C method is imported into a swift async method. For instance:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  @interface MyClass : NSObject\0A  -(void)notActuallyAsync:(int)p1 withCompletionHandler:(void (^)())handler\0A      __attribute__((swift_async(none)));\0A\0A  -(void)actuallyAsync:(int)p1 callThisAsync:(void (^)())fun\0A      __attribute__((swift_async(swift_private, 1)));\0A  @end\0A\0AHere, ``notActuallyAsync:withCompletionHandler`` would have been imported as\0A``async`` (because it's last parameter's selector piece is\0A``withCompletionHandler``) if not for the ``swift_async(none)`` attribute.\0AConversely, ``actuallyAsync:callThisAsync`` wouldn't have been imported as\0A``async`` if not for the ``swift_async`` attribute because it doesn't match the\0Anaming convention.\0A\0AWhen using ``swift_async`` to enable importing, the first argument to the\0Aattribute is either ``swift_private`` or ``not_swift_private`` to indicate\0Awhether the function/method is private to the current framework, and the second\0Aargument is the index of the completion handler parameter.\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_SwiftAsyncError = internal constant [1426 x i8] c"The ``swift_async_error`` attribute specifies how an error state will be\0Arepresented in a swift async method. It's a bit analogous to the ``swift_error``\0Aattribute for the generated async method. The ``swift_async_error`` attribute\0Acan indicate a variety of different ways of representing an error.\0A\0A- ``__attribute__((swift_async_error(zero_argument, N)))``, specifies that the\0A  async method is considered to have failed if the Nth argument to the\0A  completion handler is zero.\0A\0A- ``__attribute__((swift_async_error(nonzero_argument, N)))``, specifies that\0A  the async method is considered to have failed if the Nth argument to the\0A  completion handler is non-zero.\0A\0A- ``__attribute__((swift_async_error(nonnull_error)))``, specifies that the\0A  async method is considered to have failed if the ``NSError *`` argument to the\0A  completion handler is non-null.\0A\0A- ``__attribute__((swift_async_error(none)))``, specifies that the async method\0A  cannot fail.\0A\0A\0AFor instance:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  @interface MyClass : NSObject\0A  -(void)asyncMethod:(void (^)(char, int, float))handler\0A      __attribute__((swift_async(swift_private, 1)))\0A      __attribute__((swift_async_error(zero_argument, 2)));\0A  @end\0A\0AHere, the ``swift_async`` attribute specifies that ``handler`` is the completion\0Ahandler for this method, and the ``swift_async_error`` attribute specifies that\0Athe ``int`` parameter is the one that represents the error.\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_SwiftAsyncName = internal constant [729 x i8] c"The ``swift_async_name`` attribute provides the name of the ``async`` overload for\0Athe given declaration in Swift. If this attribute is absent, the name is\0Atransformed according to the algorithm built into the Swift compiler.\0A\0AThe argument is a string literal that contains the Swift name of the function or\0Amethod. The name may be a compound Swift name. The function or method with such\0Aan attribute must have more than zero parameters, as its last parameter is\0Aassumed to be a callback that's eliminated in the Swift ``async`` name.\0A\0A  .. code-block:: objc\0A\0A    @interface URL\0A    + (void) loadContentsFrom:(URL *)url callback:(void (^)(NSData *))data __attribute__((__swift_async_name__(\22URL.loadContentsFrom(_:)\22)))\0A    @end\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_SwiftBridge = internal constant [552 x i8] c"The ``swift_bridge`` attribute indicates that the declaration to which the\0Aattribute appertains is bridged to the named Swift type.\0A\0A  .. code-block:: objc\0A\0A    __attribute__((__objc_root__))\0A    @interface Base\0A    - (instancetype)init;\0A    @end\0A\0A    __attribute__((__swift_bridge__(\22BridgedI\22)))\0A    @interface I : Base\0A    @end\0A\0AIn this example, the Objective-C interface ``I`` will be made available to Swift\0Awith the name ``BridgedI``. It would be possible for the compiler to refer to\0A``I`` still in order to bridge the type back to Objective-C.\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_SwiftBridgedTypedef = internal constant [604 x i8] c"The ``swift_bridged_typedef`` attribute indicates that when the typedef to which\0Athe attribute appertains is imported into Swift, it should refer to the bridged\0ASwift type (e.g. Swift's ``String``) rather than the Objective-C type as written\0A(e.g. ``NSString``).\0A\0A  .. code-block:: objc\0A\0A    @interface NSString;\0A    typedef NSString *AliasedString __attribute__((__swift_bridged_typedef__));\0A\0A    extern void acceptsAliasedString(AliasedString _Nonnull parameter);\0A\0AIn this case, the function ``acceptsAliasedString`` will be imported into Swift\0Aas a function which accepts a ``String`` type parameter.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_SwiftError = internal constant [1870 x i8] c"The ``swift_error`` attribute controls whether a particular function (or\0AObjective-C method) is imported into Swift as a throwing function, and if so,\0Awhich dynamic convention it uses.\0A\0AAll of these conventions except ``none`` require the function to have an error\0Aparameter. Currently, the error parameter is always the last parameter of type\0A``NSError**`` or ``CFErrorRef*``. Swift will remove the error parameter from\0Athe imported API. When calling the API, Swift will always pass a valid address\0Ainitialized to a null pointer.\0A\0A* ``swift_error(none)`` means that the function should not be imported as\0A  throwing. The error parameter and result type will be imported normally.\0A\0A* ``swift_error(null_result)`` means that calls to the function should be\0A  considered to have thrown if they return a null value. The return type must be\0A  a pointer type, and it will be imported into Swift with a non-optional type.\0A  This is the default error convention for Objective-C methods that return\0A  pointers.\0A\0A* ``swift_error(zero_result)`` means that calls to the function should be\0A  considered to have thrown if they return a zero result. The return type must be\0A  an integral type. If the return type would have been imported as ``Bool``, it\0A  is instead imported as ``Void``. This is the default error convention for\0A  Objective-C methods that return a type that would be imported as ``Bool``.\0A\0A* ``swift_error(nonzero_result)`` means that calls to the function should be\0A  considered to have thrown if they return a non-zero result. The return type must\0A  be an integral type. If the return type would have been imported as ``Bool``,\0A  it is instead imported as ``Void``.\0A\0A* ``swift_error(nonnull_error)`` means that calls to the function should be\0A  considered to have thrown if they leave a non-null error in the error parameter.\0A  The return type is left unmodified.\00", align 16
@_ZL31AttrDoc_SwiftImportAsNonGeneric = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL38AttrDoc_SwiftImportPropertyAsAccessors = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL17AttrDoc_SwiftName = internal constant [688 x i8] c"The ``swift_name`` attribute provides the name of the declaration in Swift. If\0Athis attribute is absent, the name is transformed according to the algorithm\0Abuilt into the Swift compiler.\0A\0AThe argument is a string literal that contains the Swift name of the function,\0Avariable, or type. When renaming a function, the name may be a compound Swift\0Aname. For a type, enum constant, property, or variable declaration, the name\0Amust be a simple or qualified identifier.\0A\0A  .. code-block:: objc\0A\0A    @interface URL\0A    - (void) initWithString:(NSString *)s __attribute__((__swift_name__(\22URL.init(_:)\22)))\0A    @end\0A\0A    void __attribute__((__swift_name__(\22squareRoot()\22))) sqrt(double v) {\0A    }\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_SwiftNewType = internal constant [983 x i8] c"The ``swift_newtype`` attribute indicates that the typedef to which the\0Aattribute appertains is imported as a new Swift type of the typedef's name.\0APreviously, the attribute was spelt ``swift_wrapper``. While the behaviour of\0Athe attribute is identical with either spelling, ``swift_wrapper`` is\0Adeprecated, only exists for compatibility purposes, and should not be used in\0Anew code.\0A\0A* ``swift_newtype(struct)`` means that a Swift struct will be created for this\0A  typedef.\0A\0A* ``swift_newtype(enum)`` means that a Swift enum will be created for this\0A  typedef.\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    // Import UIFontTextStyle as an enum type, with enumerated values being\0A    // constants.\0A    typedef NSString * UIFontTextStyle __attribute__((__swift_newtype__(enum)));\0A\0A    // Import UIFontDescriptorFeatureKey as a structure type, with enumerated\0A    // values being members of the type structure.\0A    typedef NSString * UIFontDescriptorFeatureKey __attribute__((__swift_newtype__(struct)));\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_SwiftPrivate = internal constant [310 x i8] c"Declarations marked with the ``swift_private`` attribute are hidden from the\0Aframework client but are still made available for use within the framework or\0ASwift SDK overlay.\0A\0AThe purpose of this attribute is to permit a more idomatic implementation of\0Adeclarations in Swift while hiding the non-idiomatic one.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_TLSModel = internal constant [223 x i8] c"The ``tls_model`` attribute allows you to specify which thread-local storage\0Amodel to use. It accepts the following strings:\0A\0A* global-dynamic\0A* local-dynamic\0A* initial-exec\0A* local-exec\0A\0ATLS models are mutually exclusive.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Target = internal constant [2245 x i8] c"Clang supports the GNU style ``__attribute__((target(\22OPTIONS\22)))`` attribute.\0AThis attribute may be attached to a function definition and instructs\0Athe backend to use different code generation options than were passed on the\0Acommand line.\0A\0AThe current set of options correspond to the existing \22subtarget features\22 for\0Athe target with or without a \22-mno-\22 in front corresponding to the absence\0Aof the feature, as well as ``arch=\22CPU\22`` which will change the default \22CPU\22\0Afor the function.\0A\0AFor X86, the attribute also allows ``tune=\22CPU\22`` to optimize the generated\0Acode for the given CPU without changing the available instructions.\0A\0AFor AArch64, ``arch=\22Arch\22`` will set the architecture, similar to the -march\0Acommand line options. ``cpu=\22CPU\22`` can be used to select a specific cpu,\0Aas per the ``-mcpu`` option, similarly for ``tune=``. The attribute also allows the\0A\22branch-protection=<args>\22 option, where the permissible arguments and their\0Aeffect on code generation are the same as for the command-line option\0A``-mbranch-protection``.\0A\0AExample \22subtarget features\22 from the x86 backend include: \22mmx\22, \22sse\22, \22sse4.2\22,\0A\22avx\22, \22xop\22 and largely correspond to the machine specific options handled by\0Athe front end.\0A\0ANote that this attribute does not apply transitively to nested functions such\0Aas blocks or C++ lambdas.\0A\0AAdditionally, this attribute supports function multiversioning for ELF based\0Ax86/x86-64 targets, which can be used to create multiple implementations of the\0Asame function that will be resolved at runtime based on the priority of their\0A``target`` attribute strings. A function is considered a multiversioned function\0Aif either two declarations of the function have different ``target`` attribute\0Astrings, or if it has a ``target`` attribute string of ``default``. For\0Aexample:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    __attribute__((target(\22arch=atom\22)))\0A    void foo() {} // will be called on 'atom' processors.\0A    __attribute__((target(\22default\22)))\0A    void foo() {} // will be called on any other processors.\0A\0AAll multiversioned functions must contain a ``default`` (fallback)\0Aimplementation, otherwise usages of the function are considered invalid.\0AAdditionally, a function may not become multiversioned after its first use.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_TargetClones = internal constant [2291 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``target_clones(\22OPTIONS\22)`` attribute. This attribute may be\0Aattached to a function declaration and causes function multiversioning, where\0Amultiple versions of the function will be emitted with different code\0Ageneration options.  Additionally, these versions will be resolved at runtime\0Abased on the priority of their attribute options. All ``target_clone`` functions\0Aare considered multiversioned functions.\0A\0AFor AArch64 target:\0AThe attribute contains comma-separated strings of target features joined by \22+\22\0Asign. For example:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    __attribute__((target_clones(\22sha2+memtag\22, \22fcma+sve2-pmull128\22)))\0A    void foo() {}\0A\0AFor every multiversioned function a ``default`` (fallback) implementation\0Aalways generated if not specified directly.\0A\0AFor x86/x86-64 targets:\0AAll multiversioned functions must contain a ``default`` (fallback)\0Aimplementation, otherwise usages of the function are considered invalid.\0AAdditionally, a function may not become multiversioned after its first use.\0A\0AThe options to ``target_clones`` can either be a target-specific architecture\0A(specified as ``arch=CPU``), or one of a list of subtarget features.\0A\0AExample \22subtarget features\22 from the x86 backend include: \22mmx\22, \22sse\22, \22sse4.2\22,\0A\22avx\22, \22xop\22 and largely correspond to the machine specific options handled by\0Athe front end.\0A\0AThe versions can either be listed as a comma-separated sequence of string\0Aliterals or as a single string literal containing a comma-separated list of\0Aversions.  For compatibility with GCC, the two formats can be mixed.  For\0Aexample, the following will emit 4 versions of the function:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    __attribute__((target_clones(\22arch=atom,avx2\22,\22arch=ivybridge\22,\22default\22)))\0A    void foo() {}\0A\0AFor targets that support the GNU indirect function (IFUNC) feature, dispatch\0Ais performed by emitting an indirect function that is resolved to the appropriate\0Atarget clone at load time. The indirect function is given the name the\0Amultiversioned function would have if it had been declared without the attribute.\0AFor backward compatibility with earlier Clang releases, a function alias with an\0A``.ifunc`` suffix is also emitted. The  ``.ifunc`` suffixed symbol is a deprecated\0Afeature and support for it may be removed in the future.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_TargetVersion = internal constant [1036 x i8] c"For AArch64 target clang supports function multiversioning by\0A``__attribute__((target_version(\22OPTIONS\22)))`` attribute. When applied to a\0Afunction it instructs compiler to emit multiple function versions based on\0A``target_version`` attribute strings, which resolved at runtime depend on their\0Apriority and target features availability. One of the versions is always\0A( implicitly or explicitly ) the ``default`` (fallback). Attribute strings can\0Acontain dependent features names joined by the \22+\22 sign.\0A\0AFor targets that support the GNU indirect function (IFUNC) feature, dispatch\0Ais performed by emitting an indirect function that is resolved to the appropriate\0Atarget clone at load time. The indirect function is given the name the\0Amultiversioned function would have if it had been declared without the attribute.\0AFor backward compatibility with earlier Clang releases, a function alias with an\0A``.ifunc`` suffix is also emitted. The  ``.ifunc`` suffixed symbol is a deprecated\0Afeature and support for it may be removed in the future.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_TestTypestate = internal constant [135 x i8] c"Use ``__attribute__((test_typestate(tested_state)))`` to indicate that a method\0Areturns true if the object is in the specified state..\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_TransparentUnion = internal constant [609 x i8] c"This attribute can be applied to a union to change the behavior of calls to\0Afunctions that have an argument with a transparent union type. The compiler\0Abehavior is changed in the following manner:\0A\0A- A value whose type is any member of the transparent union can be passed as an\0A  argument without the need to cast that value.\0A\0A- The argument is passed to the function using the calling convention of the\0A  first member of the transparent union. Consequently, all the members of the\0A  transparent union should have the same calling convention as its first member.\0A\0ATransparent unions are not supported in C++.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_TrivialABI = internal constant [1828 x i8] c"The ``trivial_abi`` attribute can be applied to a C++ class, struct, or union.\0AIt instructs the compiler to pass and return the type using the C ABI for the\0Aunderlying type when the type would otherwise be considered non-trivial for the\0Apurpose of calls.\0AA class annotated with ``trivial_abi`` can have non-trivial destructors or\0Acopy/move constructors without automatically becoming non-trivial for the\0Apurposes of calls. For example:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    // A is trivial for the purposes of calls because ``trivial_abi`` makes the\0A    // user-provided special functions trivial.\0A    struct __attribute__((trivial_abi)) A {\0A      ~A();\0A      A(const A &);\0A      A(A &&);\0A      int x;\0A    };\0A\0A    // B's destructor and copy/move constructor are considered trivial for the\0A    // purpose of calls because A is trivial.\0A    struct B {\0A      A a;\0A    };\0A\0AIf a type is trivial for the purposes of calls, has a non-trivial destructor,\0Aand is passed as an argument by value, the convention is that the callee will\0Adestroy the object before returning. The lifetime of the copy of the parameter\0Ain the caller ends without a destructor call when the call begins.\0A\0AIf a type is trivial for the purpose of calls, it is assumed to be trivially\0Arelocatable for the purpose of ``__is_trivially_relocatable``.\0A\0AAttribute ``trivial_abi`` has no effect in the following cases:\0A\0A- The class directly declares a virtual base or virtual methods.\0A- Copy constructors and move constructors of the class are all deleted.\0A- The class has a base class that is non-trivial for the purposes of calls.\0A- The class has a non-static data member whose type is non-trivial for the\0A  purposes of calls, which includes:\0A\0A  - classes that are non-trivial for the purposes of calls\0A  - __weak-qualified types in Objective-C++\0A  - arrays of any of the above\00", align 16
@_ZL28AttrDoc_TryAcquireCapability = internal constant [270 x i8] c"Marks a function that attempts to acquire a capability. This function may fail to\0Aactually acquire the capability; they accept a Boolean value determining\0Awhether acquiring the capability means success (true), or failing to acquire\0Athe capability means success (false).\00", align 16
@_ZL26AttrDoc_TypeTagForDatatype = internal constant [4819 x i8] c"When declaring a variable, use\0A``__attribute__((type_tag_for_datatype(kind, type)))`` to create a type tag that\0Ais tied to the ``type`` argument given to the attribute.\0A\0AIn the attribute prototype above:\0A  * ``kind`` is an identifier that should be used when annotating all applicable\0A    type tags.\0A  * ``type`` indicates the name of the type.\0A\0AClang supports annotating type tags of two forms.\0A\0A  * **Type tag that is a reference to a declared identifier.**\0A    Use ``__attribute__((type_tag_for_datatype(kind, type)))`` when declaring that\0A    identifier:\0A\0A    .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A      typedef int MPI_Datatype;\0A      extern struct mpi_datatype mpi_datatype_int\0A          __attribute__(( type_tag_for_datatype(mpi,int) ));\0A      #define MPI_INT ((MPI_Datatype) &mpi_datatype_int)\0A      // &mpi_datatype_int is a type tag. It is tied to type \22int\22.\0A\0A  * **Type tag that is an integral literal.**\0A    Declare a ``static const`` variable with an initializer value and attach\0A    ``__attribute__((type_tag_for_datatype(kind, type)))`` on that declaration:\0A\0A    .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A      typedef int MPI_Datatype;\0A      static const MPI_Datatype mpi_datatype_int\0A          __attribute__(( type_tag_for_datatype(mpi,int) )) = 42;\0A      #define MPI_INT ((MPI_Datatype) 42)\0A      // The number 42 is a type tag. It is tied to type \22int\22.\0A\0A\0AThe ``type_tag_for_datatype`` attribute also accepts an optional third argument\0Athat determines how the type of the function argument specified by either\0A``arg_idx`` or ``ptr_idx`` is compared against the type associated with the type\0Atag. (Recall that for the ``argument_with_type_tag`` attribute, the type of the\0Afunction argument specified by ``arg_idx`` is compared against the type\0Aassociated with the type tag. Also recall that for the ``pointer_with_type_tag``\0Aattribute, the pointee type of the function argument specified by ``ptr_idx`` is\0Acompared against the type associated with the type tag.) There are two supported\0Avalues for this optional third argument:\0A\0A  * ``layout_compatible`` will cause types to be compared according to\0A    layout-compatibility rules (In C++11 [class.mem] p 17, 18, see the\0A    layout-compatibility rules for two standard-layout struct types and for two\0A    standard-layout union types). This is useful when creating a type tag\0A    associated with a struct or union type. For example:\0A\0A    .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A      /* In mpi.h */\0A      typedef int MPI_Datatype;\0A      struct internal_mpi_double_int { double d; int i; };\0A      extern struct mpi_datatype mpi_datatype_double_int\0A          __attribute__(( type_tag_for_datatype(mpi,\0A                          struct internal_mpi_double_int, layout_compatible) ));\0A\0A      #define MPI_DOUBLE_INT ((MPI_Datatype) &mpi_datatype_double_int)\0A\0A      int MPI_Send(void *buf, int count, MPI_Datatype datatype, ...)\0A          __attribute__(( pointer_with_type_tag(mpi,1,3) ));\0A\0A      /* In user code */\0A      struct my_pair { double a; int b; };\0A      struct my_pair *buffer;\0A      MPI_Send(buffer, 1, MPI_DOUBLE_INT /*, ... */); // no warning because the\0A                                                       // layout of my_pair is\0A                                                       // compatible with that of\0A                                                       // internal_mpi_double_int\0A\0A      struct my_int_pair { int a; int b; }\0A      struct my_int_pair *buffer2;\0A      MPI_Send(buffer2, 1, MPI_DOUBLE_INT /*, ... */); // warning because the\0A                                                        // layout of my_int_pair\0A                                                        // does not match that of\0A                                                        // internal_mpi_double_int\0A\0A  * ``must_be_null`` specifies that the function argument specified by either\0A    ``arg_idx`` (for the ``argument_with_type_tag`` attribute) or ``ptr_idx`` (for\0A    the ``pointer_with_type_tag`` attribute) should be a null pointer constant.\0A    The second argument to the ``type_tag_for_datatype`` attribute is ignored. For\0A    example:\0A\0A    .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A      /* In mpi.h */\0A      typedef int MPI_Datatype;\0A      extern struct mpi_datatype mpi_datatype_null\0A          __attribute__(( type_tag_for_datatype(mpi, void, must_be_null) ));\0A\0A      #define MPI_DATATYPE_NULL ((MPI_Datatype) &mpi_datatype_null)\0A      int MPI_Send(void *buf, int count, MPI_Datatype datatype, ...)\0A          __attribute__(( pointer_with_type_tag(mpi,1,3) ));\0A\0A      /* In user code */\0A      struct my_pair { double a; int b; };\0A      struct my_pair *buffer;\0A      MPI_Send(buffer, 1, MPI_DATATYPE_NULL /*, ... */); // warning: MPI_DATATYPE_NULL\0A                                                          // was specified but buffer\0A                                                          // is not a null pointer\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_TypeVisibility = internal constant [695 x i8] c"The ``type_visibility`` attribute allows the visibility of a type and its vague\0Alinkage objects (vtable, typeinfo, typeinfo name) to be controlled separately from\0Athe visibility of functions and data members of the type.\0A\0AFor example, this can be used to give default visibility to the typeinfo and the vtable\0Aof a type while still keeping hidden visibility on its member functions and static data\0Amembers.\0A\0AThis attribute can only be applied to types and namespaces.\0A\0AIf both ``visibility`` and ``type_visibility`` are applied to a type or a namespace, the\0Avisibility specified with the ``type_visibility`` attribute overrides the visibility\0Aprovided with the regular ``visibility`` attribute.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_Unavailable = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_Uninitialized = internal constant [419 x i8] c"The command-line parameter ``-ftrivial-auto-var-init=*`` can be used to\0Ainitialize trivial automatic stack variables. By default, trivial automatic\0Astack variables are uninitialized. This attribute is used to override the\0Acommand-line parameter, forcing variables to remain uninitialized. It has no\0Asemantic meaning in that using uninitialized values is undefined behavior,\0Ait rather documents the programmer's intent.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_UnsafeBufferUsage = internal constant [4238 x i8] c"The attribute ``[[clang::unsafe_buffer_usage]]`` should be placed on functions\0Athat need to be avoided as they are prone to buffer overflows or unsafe buffer\0Astruct fields. It is designed to work together with the off-by-default compiler\0Awarning ``-Wunsafe-buffer-usage`` to help codebases transition away from raw pointer\0Abased buffer management, in favor of safer abstractions such as C++20 ``std::span``.\0AThe attribute causes ``-Wunsafe-buffer-usage`` to warn on every use of the function or\0Athe field it is attached to, and it may also lead to emission of automatic fix-it\0Ahints which would help the user replace the use of unsafe functions(/fields) with safe\0Aalternatives, though the attribute can be used even when the fix can't be automated.\0A\0A* Attribute attached to functions: The attribute does not suppress\0A  ``-Wunsafe-buffer-usage`` inside the function to which it is attached.\0A  These warnings still need to be addressed.\0A\0A  The attribute is warranted even if the only way a function can overflow\0A  the buffer is by violating the function's preconditions. For example, it\0A  would make sense to put the attribute on function ``foo()`` below because\0A  passing an incorrect size parameter would cause a buffer overflow:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    [[clang::unsafe_buffer_usage]]\0A    void foo(int *buf, size_t size) {\0A      for (size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i) {\0A        buf[i] = i;\0A      }\0A    }\0A\0A  The attribute is NOT warranted when the function uses safe abstractions,\0A  assuming that these abstractions weren't misused outside the function.\0A  For example, function ``bar()`` below doesn't need the attribute,\0A  because assuming that the container ``buf`` is well-formed (has size that\0A  fits the original buffer it refers to), overflow cannot occur:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    void bar(std::span<int> buf) {\0A      for (size_t i = 0; i < buf.size(); ++i) {\0A        buf[i] = i;\0A      }\0A    }\0A\0A  In this case function ``bar()`` enables the user to keep the buffer\0A  \22containerized\22 in a span for as long as possible. On the other hand,\0A  Function ``foo()`` in the previous example may have internal\0A  consistency, but by accepting a raw buffer it requires the user to unwrap\0A  their span, which is undesirable according to the programming model\0A  behind ``-Wunsafe-buffer-usage``.\0A\0A  The attribute is warranted when a function accepts a raw buffer only to\0A  immediately put it into a span:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    [[clang::unsafe_buffer_usage]]\0A    void baz(int *buf, size_t size) {\0A      std::span<int> sp{ buf, size };\0A      for (size_t i = 0; i < sp.size(); ++i) {\0A        sp[i] = i;\0A      }\0A    }\0A\0A  In this case ``baz()`` does not contain any unsafe operations, but the awkward\0A  parameter type causes the caller to unwrap the span unnecessarily.\0A  Note that regardless of the attribute, code inside ``baz()`` isn't flagged\0A  by ``-Wunsafe-buffer-usage`` as unsafe. It is definitely undesirable,\0A  but if ``baz()`` is on an API surface, there is no way to improve it\0A  to make it as safe as ``bar()`` without breaking the source and binary\0A  compatibility with existing users of the function. In such cases\0A  the proper solution would be to create a different function (possibly\0A  an overload of ``baz()``) that accepts a safe container like ``bar()``,\0A  and then use the attribute on the original ``baz()`` to help the users\0A  update their code to use the new function.\0A\0A* Attribute attached to fields: The attribute should only be attached to\0A  struct fields, if the fields can not be updated to a safe type with bounds\0A  check, such as std::span. In other words, the buffers prone to unsafe accesses\0A  should always be updated to use safe containers/views and attaching the attribute\0A  must be last resort when such an update is infeasible.\0A\0A  The attribute can be placed on individual fields or a set of them as shown below.\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A    struct A {\0A      [[clang::unsafe_buffer_usage]]\0A      int *ptr1;\0A\0A      [[clang::unsafe_buffer_usage]]\0A      int *ptr2, buf[10];\0A\0A      [[clang::unsafe_buffer_usage]]\0A      size_t sz;\0A    };\0A\0A  Here, every read/write to the fields ptr1, ptr2, buf and sz will trigger a warning\0A  that the field has been explcitly marked as unsafe due to unsafe-buffer operations.\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Unused = internal constant [820 x i8] c"When passing the ``-Wunused`` flag to Clang, entities that are unused by the\0Aprogram may be diagnosed. The ``[[maybe_unused]]`` (or\0A``__attribute__((unused))``) attribute can be used to silence such diagnostics\0Awhen the entity cannot be removed. For instance, a local variable may exist\0Asolely for use in an ``assert()`` statement, which makes the local variable\0Aunused when ``NDEBUG`` is defined.\0A\0AThe attribute may be applied to the declaration of a class, a typedef, a\0Avariable, a function or method, a function parameter, an enumeration, an\0Aenumerator, a non-static data member, or a label.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  #include <cassert>\0A\0A  [[maybe_unused]] void f([[maybe_unused]] bool thing1,\0A                          [[maybe_unused]] bool thing2) {\0A    [[maybe_unused]] bool b = thing1 && thing2;\0A    assert(b);\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL12AttrDoc_Used = internal constant [1194 x i8] c"This attribute, when attached to a function or variable definition, indicates\0Athat there may be references to the entity which are not apparent in the source\0Acode.  For example, it may be referenced from inline ``asm``, or it may be\0Afound through a dynamic symbol or section lookup.\0A\0AThe compiler must emit the definition even if it appears to be unused, and it\0Amust not apply optimizations which depend on fully understanding how the entity\0Ais used.\0A\0AWhether this attribute has any effect on the linker depends on the target and\0Athe linker. Most linkers support the feature of section garbage collection\0A(``--gc-sections``), also known as \22dead stripping\22 (``ld64 -dead_strip``) or\0Adiscarding unreferenced sections (``link.exe /OPT:REF``). On COFF and Mach-O\0Atargets (Windows and Apple platforms), the `used` attribute prevents symbols\0Afrom being removed by linker section GC. On ELF targets, it has no effect on its\0Aown, and the linker may remove the definition if it is not otherwise referenced.\0AThis linker GC can be avoided by also adding the ``retain`` attribute.  Note\0Athat ``retain`` requires special support from the linker; see that attribute's\0Adocumentation for further information.\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_UsingIfExists = internal constant [935 x i8] c"The ``using_if_exists`` attribute applies to a using-declaration. It allows\0Aprogrammers to import a declaration that potentially does not exist, instead\0Adeferring any errors to the point of use. For instance:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  namespace empty_namespace {};\0A  __attribute__((using_if_exists))\0A  using empty_namespace::does_not_exist; // no error!\0A\0A  does_not_exist x; // error: use of unresolved 'using_if_exists'\0A\0AThe C++ spelling of the attribute (`[[clang::using_if_exists]]`) is also\0Asupported as a clang extension, since ISO C++ doesn't support attributes in this\0Aposition. If the entity referred to by the using-declaration is found by name\0Alookup, the attribute has no effect. This attribute is useful for libraries\0A(primarily, libc++) that wish to redeclare a set of declarations in another\0Anamespace, when the availability of those declarations is difficult or\0Aimpossible to detect at compile time with the preprocessor.\00", align 16
@_ZL12AttrDoc_Uuid = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL35AttrDoc_VTablePointerAuthentication = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_VecReturn = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_VecTypeHint = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_Visibility = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_WarnUnused = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_WarnUnusedResult = internal constant [1941 x i8] c"Clang supports the ability to diagnose when the results of a function call\0Aexpression are discarded under suspicious circumstances. A diagnostic is\0Agenerated when a function or its return type is marked with ``[[nodiscard]]``\0A(or ``__attribute__((warn_unused_result))``) and the function call appears as a\0Apotentially-evaluated discarded-value expression that is not explicitly cast to\0A``void``.\0A\0AA string literal may optionally be provided to the attribute, which will be\0Areproduced in any resulting diagnostics. Redeclarations using different forms\0Aof the attribute (with or without the string literal or with different string\0Aliteral contents) are allowed. If there are redeclarations of the entity with\0Adiffering string literals, it is unspecified which one will be used by Clang\0Ain any resulting diagnostics.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  struct [[nodiscard]] error_info { /*...*/ };\0A  error_info enable_missile_safety_mode();\0A\0A  void launch_missiles();\0A  void test_missiles() {\0A    enable_missile_safety_mode(); // diagnoses\0A    launch_missiles();\0A  }\0A  error_info &foo();\0A  void f() { foo(); } // Does not diagnose, error_info is a reference.\0A\0AAdditionally, discarded temporaries resulting from a call to a constructor\0Amarked with ``[[nodiscard]]`` or a constructor of a type marked\0A``[[nodiscard]]`` will also diagnose. This also applies to type conversions that\0Ause the annotated ``[[nodiscard]]`` constructor or result in an annotated type.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  struct [[nodiscard]] marked_type {/*..*/ };\0A  struct marked_ctor {\0A    [[nodiscard]] marked_ctor();\0A    marked_ctor(int);\0A  };\0A\0A  struct S {\0A    operator marked_type() const;\0A    [[nodiscard]] operator int() const;\0A  };\0A\0A  void usages() {\0A    marked_type(); // diagnoses.\0A    marked_ctor(); // diagnoses.\0A    marked_ctor(3); // Does not diagnose, int constructor isn't marked nodiscard.\0A\0A    S s;\0A    static_cast<marked_type>(s); // diagnoses\0A    (int)s; // diagnoses\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL12AttrDoc_Weak = internal constant [1908 x i8] c"In supported output formats the ``weak`` attribute can be used to\0Aspecify that a variable or function should be emitted as a symbol with\0A``weak`` (if a definition) or ``extern_weak`` (if a declaration of an\0Aexternal symbol) `linkage\0A<https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#linkage-types>`_.\0A\0AIf there is a non-weak definition of the symbol the linker will select\0Athat over the weak. They must have same type and alignment (variables\0Amust also have the same size), but may have a different value.\0A\0AIf there are multiple weak definitions of same symbol, but no non-weak\0Adefinition, they should have same type, size, alignment and value, the\0Alinker will select one of them (see also selectany_ attribute).\0A\0AIf the ``weak`` attribute is applied to a ``const`` qualified variable\0Adefinition that variable is no longer consider a compiletime constant\0Aas its value can change during linking (or dynamic linking). This\0Ameans that it can e.g no longer be part of an initializer expression.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  const int ANSWER __attribute__ ((weak)) = 42;\0A\0A  /* This function may be replaced link-time */\0A  __attribute__ ((weak)) void debug_log(const char *msg)\0A  {\0A      fprintf(stderr, \22DEBUG: %s\\n\22, msg);\0A  }\0A\0A  int main(int argc, const char **argv)\0A  {\0A      debug_log (\22Starting up...\22);\0A\0A      /* This may print something else than \226 * 7 = 42\22,\0A         if there is a non-weak definition of \22ANSWER\22 in\0A         an object linked in */\0A      printf(\226 * 7 = %d\\n\22, ANSWER);\0A\0A      return 0;\0A   }\0A\0AIf an external declaration is marked weak and that symbol does not\0Aexist during linking (possibly dynamic) the address of the symbol will\0Aevaluate to NULL.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  void may_not_exist(void) __attribute__ ((weak));\0A\0A  int main(int argc, const char **argv)\0A  {\0A      if (may_not_exist) {\0A          may_not_exist();\0A      } else {\0A          printf(\22Function did not exist\\n\22);\0A      }\0A      return 0;\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_WeakImport = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_WeakRef = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL29AttrDoc_WebAssemblyExportName = internal constant [516 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((export_name(<name>)))``\0Aattribute for the WebAssembly target. This attribute may be attached to a\0Afunction declaration, where it modifies how the symbol is to be exported\0Afrom the linked WebAssembly.\0A\0AWebAssembly functions are exported via string name. By default when a symbol\0Ais exported, the export name for C/C++ symbols are the same as their C/C++\0Asymbol names. This attribute can be used to override the default behavior, and\0Arequest a specific string name be used instead.\00", align 16
@_ZL31AttrDoc_WebAssemblyImportModule = internal constant [681 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((import_module(<module_name>)))``\0Aattribute for the WebAssembly target. This attribute may be attached to a\0Afunction declaration, where it modifies how the symbol is to be imported\0Awithin the WebAssembly linking environment.\0A\0AWebAssembly imports use a two-level namespace scheme, consisting of a module\0Aname, which typically identifies a module from which to import, and a field\0Aname, which typically identifies a field from that module to import. By\0Adefault, module names for C/C++ symbols are assigned automatically by the\0Alinker. This attribute can be used to override the default behavior, and\0Arequest a specific module name be used instead.\00", align 16
@_ZL29AttrDoc_WebAssemblyImportName = internal constant [670 x i8] c"Clang supports the ``__attribute__((import_name(<name>)))``\0Aattribute for the WebAssembly target. This attribute may be attached to a\0Afunction declaration, where it modifies how the symbol is to be imported\0Awithin the WebAssembly linking environment.\0A\0AWebAssembly imports use a two-level namespace scheme, consisting of a module\0Aname, which typically identifies a module from which to import, and a field\0Aname, which typically identifies a field from that module to import. By\0Adefault, field names for C/C++ symbols are the same as their C/C++ symbol\0Anames. This attribute can be used to override the default behavior, and\0Arequest a specific field name be used instead.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_WorkGroupSizeHint = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL31AttrDoc_X86ForceAlignArgPointer = internal constant [674 x i8] c"Use this attribute to force stack alignment.\0A\0ALegacy x86 code uses 4-byte stack alignment. Newer aligned SSE instructions\0A(like 'movaps') that work with the stack require operands to be 16-byte aligned.\0AThis attribute realigns the stack in the function prologue to make sure the\0Astack can be used with SSE instructions.\0A\0ANote that the x86_64 ABI forces 16-byte stack alignment at the call site.\0ABecause of this, 'force_align_arg_pointer' is not needed on x86_64, except in\0Arare cases where the caller does not align the stack properly (e.g. flow\0Ajumps from i386 arch code).\0A\0A  .. code-block:: c\0A\0A    __attribute__ ((force_align_arg_pointer))\0A    void f () {\0A      ...\0A    }\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_XRayInstrument = internal constant [843 x i8] c"``__attribute__((xray_always_instrument))`` or\0A``[[clang::xray_always_instrument]]`` is used to mark member functions (in C++),\0Amethods (in Objective C), and free functions (in C, C++, and Objective C) to be\0Ainstrumented with XRay. This will cause the function to always have space at\0Athe beginning and exit points to allow for runtime patching.\0A\0AConversely, ``__attribute__((xray_never_instrument))`` or\0A``[[clang::xray_never_instrument]]`` will inhibit the insertion of these\0Ainstrumentation points.\0A\0AIf a function has neither of these attributes, they become subject to the XRay\0Aheuristics used to determine whether a function should be instrumented or\0Aotherwise.\0A\0A``__attribute__((xray_log_args(N)))`` or ``[[clang::xray_log_args(N)]]`` is\0Aused to preserve N function arguments for the logging function. Currently,\0Aonly N==1 is supported.\00", align 16
@_ZL19AttrDoc_XRayLogArgs = internal constant [843 x i8] c"``__attribute__((xray_always_instrument))`` or\0A``[[clang::xray_always_instrument]]`` is used to mark member functions (in C++),\0Amethods (in Objective C), and free functions (in C, C++, and Objective C) to be\0Ainstrumented with XRay. This will cause the function to always have space at\0Athe beginning and exit points to allow for runtime patching.\0A\0AConversely, ``__attribute__((xray_never_instrument))`` or\0A``[[clang::xray_never_instrument]]`` will inhibit the insertion of these\0Ainstrumentation points.\0A\0AIf a function has neither of these attributes, they become subject to the XRay\0Aheuristics used to determine whether a function should be instrumented or\0Aotherwise.\0A\0A``__attribute__((xray_log_args(N)))`` or ``[[clang::xray_log_args(N)]]`` is\0Aused to preserve N function arguments for the logging function. Currently,\0Aonly N==1 is supported.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_ZeroCallUsedRegs = internal constant [1587 x i8] c"This attribute, when attached to a function, causes the compiler to zero a\0Asubset of all call-used registers before the function returns. It's used to\0Aincrease program security by either mitigating `Return-Oriented Programming`_\0A(ROP) attacks or preventing information leakage through registers.\0A\0AThe term \22call-used\22 means registers which are not guaranteed to be preserved\0Aunchanged for the caller by the current calling convention. This could also be\0Adescribed as \22caller-saved\22 or \22not callee-saved\22.\0A\0AThe `choice` parameters gives the programmer flexibility to choose the subset\0Aof the call-used registers to be zeroed:\0A\0A- ``skip`` doesn't zero any call-used registers. This choice overrides any\0A  command-line arguments.\0A- ``used`` only zeros call-used registers used in the function. By ``used``, we\0A  mean a register whose contents have been set or referenced in the function.\0A- ``used-gpr`` only zeros call-used GPR registers used in the function.\0A- ``used-arg`` only zeros call-used registers used to pass arguments to the\0A  function.\0A- ``used-gpr-arg`` only zeros call-used GPR registers used to pass arguments to\0A  the function.\0A- ``all`` zeros all call-used registers.\0A- ``all-gpr`` zeros all call-used GPR registers.\0A- ``all-arg`` zeros all call-used registers used to pass arguments to the\0A  function.\0A- ``all-gpr-arg`` zeros all call-used GPR registers used to pass arguments to\0A  the function.\0A\0AThe default for the attribute is controlled by the ``-fzero-call-used-regs``\0Aflag.\0A\0A.. _Return-Oriented Programming: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Return-oriented_programming\00", align 16
@_ZL14AttrDoc_AbiTag = internal constant [627 x i8] c"The ``abi_tag`` attribute can be applied to a function, variable, class or\0Ainline namespace declaration to modify the mangled name of the entity. It gives\0Athe ability to distinguish between different versions of the same entity but\0Awith different ABI versions supported. For example, a newer version of a class\0Acould have a different set of data members and thus have a different size. Using\0Athe ``abi_tag`` attribute, it is possible to have different mangled names for\0Aa global variable of the class type. Therefore, the old code could keep using\0Athe old mangled name and the new code will use the new mangled name with tags.\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_Alias = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_AlignValue = internal constant [633 x i8] c"The align_value attribute can be added to the typedef of a pointer type or the\0Adeclaration of a variable of pointer or reference type. It specifies that the\0Apointer will point to, or the reference will bind to, only objects with at\0Aleast the provided alignment. This alignment value must be some positive power\0Aof 2.\0A\0A   .. code-block:: c\0A\0A     typedef double * aligned_double_ptr __attribute__((align_value(64)));\0A     void foo(double & x  __attribute__((align_value(128)),\0A              aligned_double_ptr y) { ... }\0A\0AIf the pointer value does not have the specified alignment at runtime, the\0Abehavior of the program is undefined.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_BuiltinAlias = internal constant [834 x i8] c"This attribute is used in the implementation of the C intrinsics.\0AIt allows the C intrinsic functions to be declared using the names defined\0Ain target builtins, and still be recognized as clang builtins equivalent to the\0Aunderlying name. For example, ``riscv_vector.h`` declares the function ``vadd``\0Awith ``__attribute__((clang_builtin_alias(__builtin_rvv_vadd_vv_i8m1)))``.\0AThis ensures that both functions are recognized as that clang builtin,\0Aand in the latter case, the choice of which builtin to identify the\0Afunction as can be deferred until after overload resolution.\0A\0AThis attribute can only be used to set up the aliases for certain ARM/RISC-V\0AC intrinsic functions; it is intended for use only inside ``arm_*.h`` and\0A``riscv_*.h`` and is not a general mechanism for declaring arbitrary aliases\0Afor clang builtin functions.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_CalledOnce = internal constant [1490 x i8] c"The ``called_once`` attribute specifies that the annotated function or method\0Aparameter is invoked exactly once on all execution paths. It only applies\0Ato parameters with function-like types, i.e. function pointers or blocks. This\0Aconcept is particularly useful for asynchronous programs.\0A\0AClang implements a check for ``called_once`` parameters,\0A``-Wcalled-once-parameter``. It is on by default and finds the following\0Aviolations:\0A\0A* Parameter is not called at all.\0A\0A* Parameter is called more than once.\0A\0A* Parameter is not called on one of the execution paths.\0A\0AIn the latter case, Clang pinpoints the path where parameter is not invoked\0Aby showing the control-flow statement where the path diverges.\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  void fooWithCallback(void (^callback)(void) __attribute__((called_once))) {\0A    if (somePredicate()) {\0A      ...\0A      callback();\0A    } else {\0A      callback(); // OK: callback is called on every path\0A    }\0A  }\0A\0A  void barWithCallback(void (^callback)(void) __attribute__((called_once))) {\0A    if (somePredicate()) {\0A      ...\0A      callback(); // note: previous call is here\0A    }\0A    callback(); // warning: callback is called twice\0A  }\0A\0A  void foobarWithCallback(void (^callback)(void) __attribute__((called_once))) {\0A    if (somePredicate()) {  // warning: callback is not called when condition is false\0A      ...\0A      callback();\0A    }\0A  }\0A\0AThis attribute is useful for API developers who want to double-check if they\0Aimplemented their method correctly.\00", align 16
@_ZL13AttrDoc_IFunc = internal constant [1460 x i8] c"``__attribute__((ifunc(\22resolver\22)))`` is used to mark that the address of a\0Adeclaration should be resolved at runtime by calling a resolver function.\0A\0AThe symbol name of the resolver function is given in quotes. A function with\0Athis name (after mangling) must be defined in the current translation unit; it\0Amay be ``static``. The resolver function should return a pointer.\0A\0AThe ``ifunc`` attribute may only be used on a function declaration. A function\0Adeclaration with an ``ifunc`` attribute is considered to be a definition of the\0Adeclared entity. The entity must not have weak linkage; for example, in C++,\0Ait cannot be applied to a declaration if a definition at that location would be\0Aconsidered inline.\0A\0ANot all targets support this attribute:\0A\0A- ELF target support depends on both the linker and runtime linker, and is\0A  available in at least lld 4.0 and later, binutils 2.20.1 and later, glibc\0A  v2.11.1 and later, and FreeBSD 9.1 and later.\0A- Mach-O targets support it, but with slightly different semantics: the resolver\0A  is run at first call, instead of at load time by the runtime linker.\0A- Windows target supports it on AArch64, but with different semantics: the\0A  ``ifunc`` is replaced with a global function pointer, and the call is replaced\0A  with an indirect call. The function pointer is initialized by a constructor\0A  that calls the resolver.\0A- Baremetal target supports it on AVR.\0A- Other targets currently do not support this attribute.\00", align 16
@_ZL15AttrDoc_InitSeg = internal constant [603 x i8] c"The attribute applied by ``pragma init_seg()`` controls the section into\0Awhich global initialization function pointers are emitted. It is only\0Aavailable with ``-fms-extensions``. Typically, this function pointer is\0Aemitted into ``.CRT$XCU`` on Windows. The user can change the order of\0Ainitialization by using a different section name with the same\0A``.CRT$XC`` prefix and a suffix that sorts lexicographically before or\0Aafter the standard ``.CRT$XCU`` sections. See the init_seg_\0Adocumentation on MSDN for more information.\0A\0A.. _init_seg: http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/7977wcck(v=vs.110).aspx\00", align 16
@_ZL27AttrDoc_LoaderUninitialized = internal constant [1133 x i8] c"The ``loader_uninitialized`` attribute can be placed on global variables to\0Aindicate that the variable does not need to be zero initialized by the loader.\0AOn most targets, zero-initialization does not incur any additional cost.\0AFor example, most general purpose operating systems deliberately ensure\0Athat all memory is properly initialized in order to avoid leaking privileged\0Ainformation from the kernel or other programs. However, some targets\0Ado not make this guarantee, and on these targets, avoiding an unnecessary\0Azero-initialization can have a significant impact on load times and/or code\0Asize.\0A\0AA declaration with this attribute is a non-tentative definition just as if it\0Aprovided an initializer. Variables with this attribute are considered to be\0Auninitialized in the same sense as a local variable, and the programs must\0Awrite to them before reading from them. If the variable's type is a C++ class\0Atype with a non-trivial default constructor, or an array thereof, this attribute\0Aonly suppresses the static zero-initialization of the variable, not the dynamic\0Ainitialization provided by executing the default constructor.\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_LoopHint = internal constant [523 x i8] c"The ``#pragma clang loop`` directive allows loop optimization hints to be\0Aspecified for the subsequent loop. The directive allows pipelining to be\0Adisabled, or vectorization, vector predication, interleaving, and unrolling to\0Abe enabled or disabled. Vector width, vector predication, interleave count,\0Aunrolling count, and the initiation interval for pipelining can be explicitly\0Aspecified. See `language extensions\0A<http://clang.llvm.org/docs/LanguageExtensions.html#extensions-for-loop-hint-optimizations>`_\0Afor details.\00", align 16
@_ZL12AttrDoc_Mode = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL17AttrDoc_NoBuiltin = internal constant [1161 x i8] c"The ``__attribute__((no_builtin))`` is similar to the ``-fno-builtin`` flag\0Aexcept it is specific to the body of a function. The attribute may also be\0Aapplied to a virtual function but has no effect on the behavior of overriding\0Afunctions in a derived class.\0A\0AIt accepts one or more strings corresponding to the specific names of the\0Abuiltins to disable (e.g. \22memcpy\22, \22memset\22).\0AIf the attribute is used without parameters it will disable all buitins at\0Aonce.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  // The compiler is not allowed to add any builtin to foo's body.\0A  void foo(char* data, size_t count) __attribute__((no_builtin)) {\0A    // The compiler is not allowed to convert the loop into\0A    // `__builtin_memset(data, 0xFE, count);`.\0A    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i)\0A      data[i] = 0xFE;\0A  }\0A\0A  // The compiler is not allowed to add the `memcpy` builtin to bar's body.\0A  void bar(char* data, size_t count) __attribute__((no_builtin(\22memcpy\22))) {\0A    // The compiler is allowed to convert the loop into\0A    // `__builtin_memset(data, 0xFE, count);` but cannot generate any\0A    // `__builtin_memcpy`\0A    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i)\0A      data[i] = 0xFE;\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL16AttrDoc_NoEscape = internal constant [1125 x i8] c"``noescape`` placed on a function parameter of a pointer type is used to inform\0Athe compiler that the pointer cannot escape: that is, no reference to the object\0Athe pointer points to that is derived from the parameter value will survive\0Aafter the function returns. Users are responsible for making sure parameters\0Aannotated with ``noescape`` do not actually escape. Calling ``free()`` on such\0Aa parameter does not constitute an escape.\0A\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int *gp;\0A\0A  void nonescapingFunc(__attribute__((noescape)) int *p) {\0A    *p += 100; // OK.\0A  }\0A\0A  void escapingFunc(__attribute__((noescape)) int *p) {\0A    gp = p; // Not OK.\0A  }\0A\0AAdditionally, when the parameter is a `block pointer\0A<https://clang.llvm.org/docs/BlockLanguageSpec.html>`, the same restriction\0Aapplies to copies of the block. For example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  typedef void (^BlockTy)();\0A  BlockTy g0, g1;\0A\0A  void nonescapingFunc(__attribute__((noescape)) BlockTy block) {\0A    block(); // OK.\0A  }\0A\0A  void escapingFunc(__attribute__((noescape)) BlockTy block) {\0A    g0 = block; // Not OK.\0A    g1 = Block_copy(block); // Not OK either.\0A  }\00", align 16
@_ZL22AttrDoc_OMPCaptureKind = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL26AttrDoc_OMPDeclareSimdDecl = internal constant [1045 x i8] c"The ``declare simd`` construct can be applied to a function to enable the creation\0Aof one or more versions that can process multiple arguments using SIMD\0Ainstructions from a single invocation in a SIMD loop. The ``declare simd``\0Adirective is a declarative directive. There may be multiple ``declare simd``\0Adirectives for a function. The use of a ``declare simd`` construct on a function\0Aenables the creation of SIMD versions of the associated function that can be\0Aused to process multiple arguments from a single invocation from a SIMD loop\0Aconcurrently.\0AThe syntax of the ``declare simd`` construct is as follows:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: none\0A\0A    #pragma omp declare simd [clause[[,] clause] ...] new-line\0A    [#pragma omp declare simd [clause[[,] clause] ...] new-line]\0A    [...]\0A    function definition or declaration\0A\0Awhere clause is one of the following:\0A\0A  .. code-block:: none\0A\0A    simdlen(length)\0A    linear(argument-list[:constant-linear-step])\0A    aligned(argument-list[:alignment])\0A    uniform(argument-list)\0A    inbranch\0A    notinbranch\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_OMPReferencedVar = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL19AttrDoc_ObjCBoxable = internal constant [571 x i8] c"Structs and unions marked with the ``objc_boxable`` attribute can be used\0Awith the Objective-C boxed expression syntax, ``@(...)``.\0A\0A**Usage**: ``__attribute__((objc_boxable))``. This attribute\0Acan only be placed on a declaration of a trivially-copyable struct or union:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  struct __attribute__((objc_boxable)) some_struct {\0A    int i;\0A  };\0A  union __attribute__((objc_boxable)) some_union {\0A    int i;\0A    float f;\0A  };\0A  typedef struct __attribute__((objc_boxable)) _some_struct some_struct;\0A\0A  // ...\0A\0A  some_struct ss;\0A  NSValue *boxed = @(ss);\00", align 16
@_ZL21AttrDoc_ObjCClassStub = internal constant [693 x i8] c"This attribute specifies that the Objective-C class to which it applies is\0Ainstantiated at runtime.\0A\0AUnlike ``__attribute__((objc_runtime_visible))``, a class having this attribute\0Astill has a \22class stub\22 that is visible to the linker. This allows categories\0Ato be defined. Static message sends with the class as a receiver use a special\0Aaccess pattern to ensure the class is lazily instantiated from the class stub.\0A\0AClasses annotated with this attribute cannot be subclassed and cannot have\0Aimplementations defined for them. This attribute is intended for use in\0ASwift-generated headers for classes defined in Swift.\0A\0AAdding or removing this attribute to a class is an ABI-breaking change.\00", align 16
@_ZL33AttrDoc_ObjCDesignatedInitializer = internal constant [18 x i8] c"No documentation.\00", align 16
@_ZL18AttrDoc_ObjCDirect = internal constant [3608 x i8] c"The ``objc_direct`` attribute can be used to mark an Objective-C method as\0Abeing *direct*. A direct method is treated statically like an ordinary method,\0Abut dynamically it behaves more like a C function. This lowers some of the costs\0Aassociated with the method but also sacrifices some of the ordinary capabilities\0Aof Objective-C methods.\0A\0AA message send of a direct method calls the implementation directly, as if it\0Awere a C function, rather than using ordinary Objective-C method dispatch. This\0Ais substantially faster and potentially allows the implementation to be inlined,\0Abut it also means the method cannot be overridden in subclasses or replaced\0Adynamically, as ordinary Objective-C methods can.\0A\0AFurthermore, a direct method is not listed in the class's method lists. This\0Asubstantially reduces the code-size overhead of the method but also means it\0Acannot be called dynamically using ordinary Objective-C method dispatch at all;\0Ain particular, this means that it cannot override a superclass method or satisfy\0Aa protocol requirement.\0A\0ABecause a direct method cannot be overridden, it is an error to perform\0Aa ``super`` message send of one.\0A\0AAlthough a message send of a direct method causes the method to be called\0Adirectly as if it were a C function, it still obeys Objective-C semantics in other\0Aways:\0A\0A- If the receiver is ``nil``, the message send does nothing and returns the zero value\0A  for the return type.\0A\0A- A message send of a direct class method will cause the class to be initialized,\0A  including calling the ``+initialize`` method if present.\0A\0A- The implicit ``_cmd`` parameter containing the method's selector is still defined.\0A  In order to minimize code-size costs, the implementation will not emit a reference\0A  to the selector if the parameter is unused within the method.\0A\0ASymbols for direct method implementations are implicitly given hidden\0Avisibility, meaning that they can only be called within the same linkage unit.\0A\0AIt is an error to do any of the following:\0A\0A- declare a direct method in a protocol,\0A- declare an override of a direct method with a method in a subclass,\0A- declare an override of a non-direct method with a direct method in a subclass,\0A- declare a method with different directness in different class interfaces, or\0A- implement a non-direct method (as declared in any class interface) with a direct method.\0A\0AIf any of these rules would be violated if every method defined in an\0A``@implementation`` within a single linkage unit were declared in an\0Aappropriate class interface, the program is ill-formed with no diagnostic\0Arequired. If a violation of this rule is not diagnosed, behavior remains\0Awell-defined; this paragraph is simply reserving the right to diagnose such\0Aconflicts in the future, not to treat them as undefined behavior.\0A\0AAdditionally, Clang will warn about any ``@selector`` expression that\0Anames a selector that is only known to be used for direct methods.\0A\0AFor the purpose of these rules, a \22class interface\22 includes a class's primary\0A``@interface`` block, its class extensions, its categories, its declared protocols,\0Aand all the class interfaces of its superclasses.\0A\0AAn Objective-C property can be declared with the ``direct`` property\0Aattribute. If a direct property declaration causes an implicit declaration of\0Aa getter or setter method (that is, if the given method is not explicitly\0Adeclared elsewhere), the method is declared to be direct.\0A\0ASome programmers may wish to make many methods direct at once. In order\0Ato simplify this, the ``objc_direct_members`` attribute is provided; see its\0Adocumentation for more information.\00", align 16
@_ZL25AttrDoc_ObjCDirectMembers = internal constant [993 x i8] c"The ``objc_direct_members`` attribute can be placed on an Objective-C\0A``@interface`` or ``@implementation`` to mark that methods declared\0Atherein should be considered direct by default. See the documentation\0Afor ``objc_direct`` for more information about direct methods.\0A\0AWhen ``objc_direct_members`` is placed on an ``@interface`` block, every\0Amethod in the block is considered to be declared as direct. This includes any\0Aimplicit method declarations introduced by property declarations. If the method\0Aredeclares a non-direct method, the declaration is ill-formed, exactly as if the\0Amethod was annotated with the ``objc_direct`` attribute.\0A\0AWhen ``objc_direct_members`` is placed on an ``@implementation`` block,\0Amethods defined in the block are considered to be declared as direct unless\0Athey have been previously declared as non-direct in any interface of the class.\0AThis includes the implicit method definitions introduced by synthesized\0Aproperties, including auto-synthesized properties.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_ObjCNonLazyClass = internal constant [585 x i8] c"This attribute can be added to an Objective-C ``@interface`` or\0A``@implementation`` declaration to add the class to the list of non-lazily\0Ainitialized classes. A non-lazy class will be initialized eagerly when the\0AObjective-C runtime is loaded. This is required for certain system classes which\0Ahave instances allocated in non-standard ways, such as the classes for blocks\0Aand constant strings. Adding this attribute is essentially equivalent to\0Aproviding a trivial ``+load`` method but avoids the (fairly small) load-time\0Aoverheads associated with defining and calling such a method.\00", align 16
@_ZL30AttrDoc_ObjCNonRuntimeProtocol = internal constant [657 x i8] c"The ``objc_non_runtime_protocol`` attribute can be used to mark that an\0AObjective-C protocol is only used during static type-checking and doesn't need\0Ato be represented dynamically. This avoids several small code-size and run-time\0Aoverheads associated with handling the protocol's metadata. A non-runtime\0Aprotocol cannot be used as the operand of a ``@protocol`` expression, and\0Adynamic attempts to find it with ``objc_getProtocol`` will fail.\0A\0AIf a non-runtime protocol inherits from any ordinary protocols, classes and\0Aderived protocols that declare conformance to the non-runtime protocol will\0Adynamically list their conformance to those bare protocols.\00", align 16
@_ZL23AttrDoc_ObjCRuntimeName = internal constant [531 x i8] c"By default, the Objective-C interface or protocol identifier is used\0Ain the metadata name for that object. The ``objc_runtime_name``\0Aattribute allows annotated interfaces or protocols to use the\0Aspecified string argument in the object's metadata name instead of the\0Adefault name.\0A\0A**Usage**: ``__attribute__((objc_runtime_name(\22MyLocalName\22)))``. This attribute\0Acan only be placed before an @protocol or @interface declaration:\0A\0A.. code-block:: objc\0A\0A  __attribute__((objc_runtime_name(\22MyLocalName\22)))\0A  @interface Message\0A  @end\00", align 16
@_ZL26AttrDoc_ObjCRuntimeVisible = internal constant [237 x i8] c"This attribute specifies that the Objective-C class to which it applies is\0Avisible to the Objective-C runtime but not to the linker. Classes annotated\0Awith this attribute cannot be subclassed and cannot have categories defined for\0Athem.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_OpenCLAccess = internal constant [683 x i8] c"The access qualifiers must be used with image object arguments or pipe arguments\0Ato declare if they are being read or written by a kernel or function.\0A\0AThe read_only/__read_only, write_only/__write_only and read_write/__read_write\0Anames are reserved for use as access qualifiers and shall not be used otherwise.\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  kernel void\0A  foo (read_only image2d_t imageA,\0A       write_only image2d_t imageB) {\0A    ...\0A  }\0A\0AIn the above example imageA is a read-only 2D image object, and imageB is a\0Awrite-only 2D image object.\0A\0AThe read_write (or __read_write) qualifier can not be used with pipe.\0A\0AMore details can be found in the OpenCL C language Spec v2.0, Section 6.6.\00", align 16
@_ZL20AttrDoc_Overloadable = internal constant [5181 x i8] c"Clang provides support for C++ function overloading in C. Function overloading\0Ain C is introduced using the ``overloadable`` attribute. For example, one\0Amight provide several overloaded versions of a ``tgsin`` function that invokes\0Athe appropriate standard function computing the sine of a value with ``float``,\0A``double``, or ``long double`` precision:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  #include <math.h>\0A  float __attribute__((overloadable)) tgsin(float x) { return sinf(x); }\0A  double __attribute__((overloadable)) tgsin(double x) { return sin(x); }\0A  long double __attribute__((overloadable)) tgsin(long double x) { return sinl(x); }\0A\0AGiven these declarations, one can call ``tgsin`` with a ``float`` value to\0Areceive a ``float`` result, with a ``double`` to receive a ``double`` result,\0Aetc. Function overloading in C follows the rules of C++ function overloading\0Ato pick the best overload given the call arguments, with a few C-specific\0Asemantics:\0A\0A* Conversion from ``float`` or ``double`` to ``long double`` is ranked as a\0A  floating-point promotion (per C99) rather than as a floating-point conversion\0A  (as in C++).\0A\0A* A conversion from a pointer of type ``T*`` to a pointer of type ``U*`` is\0A  considered a pointer conversion (with conversion rank) if ``T`` and ``U`` are\0A  compatible types.\0A\0A* A conversion from type ``T`` to a value of type ``U`` is permitted if ``T``\0A  and ``U`` are compatible types. This conversion is given \22conversion\22 rank.\0A\0A* If no viable candidates are otherwise available, we allow a conversion from a\0A  pointer of type ``T*`` to a pointer of type ``U*``, where ``T`` and ``U`` are\0A  incompatible. This conversion is ranked below all other types of conversions.\0A  Please note: ``U`` lacking qualifiers that are present on ``T`` is sufficient\0A  for ``T`` and ``U`` to be incompatible.\0A\0AThe declaration of ``overloadable`` functions is restricted to function\0Adeclarations and definitions. If a function is marked with the ``overloadable``\0Aattribute, then all declarations and definitions of functions with that name,\0Aexcept for at most one (see the note below about unmarked overloads), must have\0Athe ``overloadable`` attribute. In addition, redeclarations of a function with\0Athe ``overloadable`` attribute must have the ``overloadable`` attribute, and\0Aredeclarations of a function without the ``overloadable`` attribute must *not*\0Ahave the ``overloadable`` attribute. e.g.,\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int f(int) __attribute__((overloadable));\0A  float f(float); // error: declaration of \22f\22 must have the \22overloadable\22 attribute\0A  int f(int); // error: redeclaration of \22f\22 must have the \22overloadable\22 attribute\0A\0A  int g(int) __attribute__((overloadable));\0A  int g(int) { } // error: redeclaration of \22g\22 must also have the \22overloadable\22 attribute\0A\0A  int h(int);\0A  int h(int) __attribute__((overloadable)); // error: declaration of \22h\22 must not\0A                                            // have the \22overloadable\22 attribute\0A\0AFunctions marked ``overloadable`` must have prototypes. Therefore, the\0Afollowing code is ill-formed:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  int h() __attribute__((overloadable)); // error: h does not have a prototype\0A\0AHowever, ``overloadable`` functions are allowed to use a ellipsis even if there\0Aare no named parameters (as is permitted in C++). This feature is particularly\0Auseful when combined with the ``unavailable`` attribute:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c++\0A\0A  void honeypot(...) __attribute__((overloadable, unavailable)); // calling me is an error\0A\0AFunctions declared with the ``overloadable`` attribute have their names mangled\0Aaccording to the same rules as C++ function names. For example, the three\0A``tgsin`` functions in our motivating example get the mangled names\0A``_Z5tgsinf``, ``_Z5tgsind``, and ``_Z5tgsine``, respectively. There are two\0Acaveats to this use of name mangling:\0A\0A* Future versions of Clang may change the name mangling of functions overloaded\0A  in C, so you should not depend on an specific mangling. To be completely\0A  safe, we strongly urge the use of ``static inline`` with ``overloadable``\0A  functions.\0A\0A* The ``overloadable`` attribute has almost no meaning when used in C++,\0A  because names will already be mangled and functions are already overloadable.\0A  However, when an ``overloadable`` function occurs within an ``extern \22C\22``\0A  linkage specification, its name *will* be mangled in the same way as it\0A  would in C.\0A\0AFor the purpose of backwards compatibility, at most one function with the same\0Aname as other ``overloadable`` functions may omit the ``overloadable``\0Aattribute. In this case, the function without the ``overloadable`` attribute\0Awill not have its name mangled.\0A\0AFor example:\0A\0A.. code-block:: c\0A\0A  // Notes with mangled names assume Itanium mangling.\0A  int f(int);\0A  int f(double) __attribute__((overloadable));\0A  void foo() {\0A    f(5); // Emits a call to f (not _Z1fi, as it would with an overload that\0A          // was marked with overloadable).\0A    f(1.0); // Emits a call to _Z1fd.\0A  }\0A\0ASupport for unmarked overloads is not present in some versions of clang. You may\0Aquery for it using ``__has_extension(overloadable_unmarked)``.\0A\0AQuery for this attribute with ``__has_attribute(overloadable)``.\00", align 16
@_ZL24AttrDoc_SwiftObjCMembers = internal constant [197 x i8] c"This attribute indicates that Swift subclasses and members of Swift extensions\0Aof this class will be implicitly marked with the ``@objcMembers`` Swift\0Aattribute, exposing them back to Objective-C.\00", align 16
@_ZL30AttrDoc_SwiftVersionedAddition = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL29AttrDoc_SwiftVersionedRemoval = internal constant [1 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 1
@_ZL14AttrDoc_Thread = internal constant [633 x i8] c"The ``__declspec(thread)`` attribute declares a variable with thread local\0Astorage. It is available under the ``-fms-extensions`` flag for MSVC\0Acompatibility. See the documentation for `__declspec(thread)`_ on MSDN.\0A\0A.. _`__declspec(thread)`: http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/9w1sdazb.aspx\0A\0AIn Clang, ``__declspec(thread)`` is generally equivalent in functionality to the\0AGNU ``__thread`` keyword. The variable must not have a destructor and must have\0Aa constant initializer, if any. The attribute only applies to variables\0Adeclared with static storage duration, such as globals, class static data\0Amembers, and static locals.\00", align 16

